ll,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":22,\"currentRank\":22,\"rankDelta\":0,\"improvementPct\":26.51},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1079682248003376\",\"collationId\":\"1101079172644155\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-02T06:06:23.972Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-09-30T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-10-17T11:53:31.042Z\",\"daysRunning\":17,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/0e9fe18dbacd967996f5fc5e5d723ecd6ed55633b9e5b9fb65b5b17550aa8aae.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1302281089625752\",\"name\":\"Dr. Nourish\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/profile_picture/1302281089625752.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1302281089625752\",\"liveAdsCount\":46,\"reach30d\":0,\"totalReach\":0,\"countriesCount\":1,\"facebookLikes\":14,\"instagramFollowers\":41},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Most liver teas come in flat opaque bags. You never actually see what is inside.\\n\\nDr. Nourish Kutki-Kalmegh Liver Care Herbal Tea uses a see-through pyramid bag. Hold it to the light  You will see Kutki root, Kalmegh leaf, Ceylon cinnamon bark, Amla, Tulsi, Mulethi, and Pippali. Seven whole herbs. Visible. Real.\\n\\nKutki, traditionally used in Ayurveda to support bile flow. Kalmegh, traditionally used to support liver enzyme function.\\n\\n50  bags. Caffeine free. No capsules. Made in India.\\n\\nLaunch offer, full pack for just Rs. 599.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"SHOP NOW\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://drnourish.in/products/kutki-kalmegh-liver-care-tea-fp\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"drnourish.in\",\"ctaDescription\":\"7 Herbs. One Cup. Support Your Liver Daily.\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Launch Offer, Full 50-bag pack for Rs. 599.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":45,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"IN\"],\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":11,\"currentRank\":11,\"rankDelta\":null,\"improvementPct\":78.57},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1053820354082735\",\"collationId\":\"1753967912807545\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-09-30T12:20:51.230Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-09-29T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-10-05T16:36:28.326Z\",\"daysRunning\":6,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/c836813489c0fb01f2864cc54445e02bb199d2c97ff88e58b8211453a102e260.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"124148927438400\",\"name\":\"Ahuja Pulliam Montgomery\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/124148927438400.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"124148927438400\",\"liveAdsCount\":3437,\"reach30d\":232122,\"totalReach\":622979,\"countriesCount\":91,\"facebookLikes\":481,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I've been on Metformin for 9 years. I've been a braider for 37. A wife for 33. A grandmother for 7. The drug gave me perfect numbers. Then it took my hands.\\n\\nI need to tell you what happened, because if your blood sugar is high and you're taking Metformin or thinking about it or trying to manage it on your own with cinnamon and apple cider vinegar and every other thing the internet says works, you need to read this. All of it.\\n\\nMy name is Joanne. I'm 61 years old. I opened my shop when I was 24, saved every dollar from 5 years of braiding hair on my grandmother's porch and put a deposit on a storefront that smelled like floor cleaner and old paint. Built it from nothing. Survived three recessions. Outlasted a strip mall demolition. Moved twice. Rebuilt twice. Never once closed. Not for COVID. Not for anything.\\n\\nI braided the way I was taught. Knotless box braids that lay flat from the scalp. Fulani patterns that looked like they were stitched in by hand. Tension so clean the parts could cut you. My grandmother was a braider. She braided in our living room from a wooden stool, six clients a day, every day, for 40 years. She gave me her wooden parting comb when I opened my shop and said, \\\"The day your fingers stop feeling the hair is the day you stop.\\\" I hung that comb on the wall behind my chair and I've used it every working day for 37 years.\\n\\nOr I did. Until two years ago.\\n\\nMy doctor put me on Metformin when I was 52. A1C was 7.4. He said 1000 milligrams, twice a day, come back in 90 days. I didn't question it. Why would I? I was healthy. I was active. I was on my feet 10 hours a day braiding hair. But my numbers were high and he said the drug was the smart move.\\n\\n90 days later my A1C was 6.6. Fasting glucose dropped from 156 to 118. He was thrilled. I was thrilled. The drug was doing exactly what it was supposed to do.\\n\\nSome tingling in my fingers in the mornings. Nothing I thought twice about. I was 52. I'd been working with my hands since I was a teenager. Tingling happens.\\n\\nBy year three the tingling had a name. Numbness.\\n\\nMy hands. Fingertips first, then up through my fingers. A dull, dead feeling that settled in around noon every day and didn't let up until I went to bed. Mornings were the worst. I couldn't feel the comb in my hand when I woke up. Had to run my hands under hot water for 10 minutes before I could grip a section of hair properly. 10 minutes. Every morning. Standing over the sink at 5 AM rubbing fingertips that didn't want to wake up, trying to get them ready for a day of work they used to do without thinking.\\n\\nI told my doctor.\\n\\nHe ran my labs. Looked at the A1C.\\n\\n\\\"Your A1C is 6.4. Fasting glucose is 112. These are excellent numbers, Joanne. Whatever you're doing, keep doing it.\\\"\\n\\nI said, \\\"I'm not asking about my A1C. I'm asking about my hands.\\\"\\n\\nHe said neuropathy is a known issue in diabetic patients. Affects some people. For most, the benefits of the medication significantly outweigh the risks. He'd keep an eye on it.\\n\\nKeep an eye on it.\\n\\nEasy to say when your hands aren't your whole life.\\n\\nBy year five the knotless box braids were gone.\\n\\nNot all at once. I didn't wake up one morning and decide to stop. It happened in pieces. First I stopped booking knotless on Mondays because my hands were stiffest at the start of the week. Then Mondays became Monday through Wednesday. Then I started telling new clients I was only doing regular box braids and twists. Then it was twists only, all the time, for everyone.\\n\\nWomen used to drive across town specifically for my knotless. I could feed in extensions so smoothly the braid looked like it grew that way. That wasn't a skill I learned in a year. That was 30 years of muscle memory built one braid at a time. And I could feel it leaving.\\n\\nThe shaking started around year five. My hands. Not dramatic. Just a fine tremor when I tried to hold tension on a small section. Barely visible to anyone who wasn't looking. But I was looking. Every day I was looking. Every time I picked up a section of hair I was watching my own fingers for the shake, hoping today would be the day it stopped.\\n\\nIt didn't stop. It got worse.\\n\\nClients started noticing. Nobody said anything. You don't tell your braider her hands are shaking the same way you don't tell a singer her voice is going. But I saw them glance in the mirror. I saw the regulars who used to close their eyes in my chair and lean back into the work now watching my hands with both eyes open.\\n\\nI started going slower. A woman who's braided over a thousand heads doesn't suddenly need 4 extra hours unless something is wrong. I was going slower because slower meant steadier. Slower meant I could hide the shake. Slower meant nobody had to see that the hands they'd trusted for years were failing.\\n\\nThe wooden parting comb, my grandmother's comb, started gathering dust.\\n\\nI went back to my doctor. Told him the numbness was worse. Showed him my hands. Told him I couldn't do knotless anymore, the thing that made my shop my shop, the thing women drove across town for, the thing my grandmother taught me, gone.\\n\\nHe looked at my chart.\\n\\n\\\"Joanne, your A1C is 6.2. Fasting glucose is 108. These are excellent numbers. I understand the neuropathy is frustrating, but the cardiovascular and kidney protection Metformin provides at your age is significant. The benefits outweigh the risks. I'd strongly recommend you stay on it.\\\"\\n\\nThe benefits outweigh the risks.\\n\\nHe said it the way you'd compliment a child's report card. Like the number on the chart was the only thing that mattered. Like the woman attached to the chart, the woman whose hands were dying, whose livelihood was disappearing, whose grandmother's comb was collecting dust, was irrelevant.\\n\\nI decided if the doctor wasn't going to help me, I'd help myself. Maybe I could get my numbers down naturally that I could lower the Metformin dose. Maybe come off it entirely with my doctor's approval. Get my hands back.\\n\\nSo I tried everything. And I mean everything.\\n\\nI started with cinnamon. Real Ceylon cinnamon. Not the cheap stuff. Every morning at 5 AM, stirring it into hot water because somebody told me it slows down how fast carbs hit your blood. I did that for a year. A full year. 365 mornings of cinnamon water. My A1C dropped 2 points. 2 points. My hands were still numb.\\n\\nApple cider vinegar. 2 tablespoons before every meal. The kind with the mother in it. 7 months. My morning numbers came down a little. Post meal? Nothing. And the taste was so bad I'd dread sitting down to dinner.\\n\\nBerberine. The one everybody on TikTok was calling nature's Ozempic. Three capsules a day, three different brands over six months. The cramping was worse than the Metformin. My numbers barely moved.\\n\\nMy doctor glanced at the results and said, \\\"Your A1C looks great, Joanne. Keep taking the Metformin.\\\"\\n\\nI could have put my fist through the wall. Except my fist didn't fully close anymore.\\n\\nAfter that I tried everything else I could get my hands on. Chromium. Bitter melon. Fenugreek. Banaba leaf. Gymnema sylvestre. Alpha lipoic acid because I'd read it helps with neuropathy. CoQ10. Magnesium. Psyllium husk. Some for months. Some for weeks. None of them moved the needle. My kitchen counter looked like a pharmacy. My husband counted the bottles one night and said, \\\"You have more pills than the Metformin was supposed to replace.\\\"\\n\\nHe was right.\\n\\nAnd the worst part? Through all of it, my A1C barely moved without the drug. And my hands kept getting worse on it.\\n\\nI was doing everything you're supposed to do. Everything the blogs say. Everything the health food store recommends. Everything my cousin swears by and everything the lady at church credits for her numbers dropping. None of it worked. And I couldn't figure out why.\\n\\nThe day I almost closed my shop was a Saturday in November.\\n\\nI was sectioning hair for a regular, a woman I'd been braiding for 16 years. Simple style. Big box braids, nothing fancy. Should have been a 4 hour appointment. I was holding her section in my left hand and my fingers seized. Not shook. SEIZED. The muscles in my hand cramped and my fingers locked and the section slipped through and pulled her hair the wrong direction.\\n\\nShe winced.\\n\\nI pulled back. Apologized. She told me it was fine. But I saw her glance at her phone after that, checking the time more than she should have. She didn't say anything. I didn't say anything. I finished the braids. She paid and left.\\n\\nShe didn't rebook.\\n\\nI sat in my own chair after she left. The shop was empty. 6 PM on a Saturday, a time when 3 women should have been waiting. I sat there looking at my hands, palms up, and I realized these aren't my hands anymore. These are what's left of my hands after 9 years on a drug that was supposed to keep me healthy.\\n\\nI looked at the photographs on the wall. The first dollar bill in a frame by the register. The wooden parting comb.\\n\\nMy grandmother said the day your fingers stop feeling the hair is the day you stop.\\n\\nI called my husband and told him I was thinking about closing. He was quiet for a long time. Then he said, \\\"Don't close yet. Don't make that decision yet. Just don't decide today.\\\"\\n\\nI didn't close. But I went home that night knowing something had to change. The Metformin was destroying me. The supplements weren't helping me. And my doctor was watching a number on a chart while my career died.\\n\\nI didn't start with the right question. I started where everyone starts. \\\"Can Metformin cause hand neuropathy.\\\"\\n\\n200,000 results.\\n\\n\\\"Metformin B12 depletion.\\\" Millions.\\n\\n\\\"Metformin nerve damage hands.\\\" Forums full of women describing exactly what I was living. Quilters who couldn't thread a needle. Pianists who couldn't feel the keys. Surgeons, SURGEONS, who had to stop operating. I wasn't alone. I wasn't crazy. And I wasn't \\\"just getting old.\\\"\\n\\nBut the search that changed everything was one I almost didn't click. \\\"Why do people on Metformin still see their A1C climb.\\\"\\n\\nThat's where I fell down the rabbit hole. And I didn't come back up the same person.\\n\\nHere's what 9 years of doctor visits never explained to me, and what every supplement I'd tried completely ignored.\\n\\nMetformin doesn't address what's actually broken inside your body.\\n\\nStay with me here. This is the part that changed everything for me.\\n\\nYour body has a pathway that controls how fast the food you eat turns into sugar in your blood. When food comes in, the pathway breaks it down at a controlled pace. Sugar trickles into your blood slowly. Your insulin sees it coming and handles it calmly. Your numbers stay steady.\\n\\nThat's how it worked when you were 30 and you could eat anything.\\n\\nBut that pathway is fragile. Stress wears it down. Processed food wears it down. Years of carrying extra weight wear it down. And one day it stops being able to do its job.\\n\\nFood doesn't trickle into your blood anymore. It floods in. The carbs in your meal that used to take 90 minutes to enter your blood now hit in 15. The flood arrives faster than your insulin can handle. So you spike. Your pancreas panics and dumps a wave of insulin to catch up. That wave is too much. So an hour later you crash.\\n\\nThat flood is the problem. Not the food. The broken pathway.\\n\\nNow here's what Metformin actually does. It works on your liver. It tells your liver to stop releasing stored sugar into your blood overnight. That's helpful for your fasting glucose number. The morning reading on the meter comes down. Your A1C looks better on paper.\\n\\nBut it doesn't touch the pathway that's letting food flood your blood every time you eat. It doesn't slow the breakdown. It doesn't restore the system that controls the speed. The flood still happens every meal. The spike still happens. The crash still happens.\\n\\nYour doctor was watching the morning number. Nobody was watching the flood.\\n\\nAnd here's where I saw my own hands in the research.\\n\\nMetformin depletes vitamin B12. Long term use, year after year, slowly drains your B12 levels. And your nerves need B12 to function. Your fingertips need it to feel. Your hands need it to grip, to hold tension, to braid hair the way they've been doing it for 37 years.\\n\\nI read that and I felt it in my fingers. I saw 9 years of mornings running hot water over fingertips that wouldn't wake up. I saw 37 years of muscle memory slowly losing the nutrient it needed to fire. The numbness wasn't aging. It wasn't \\\"just diabetes.\\\" It was my nerves running out of B12 because the drug was depleting it as part of how it works.\\n\\nThat's not a side effect. That's the drug doing what it does. And nobody told me.\\n\\nAbout 30% of long term Metformin users develop B12 deficiency. That's not a rare complication. That's nearly one in three. And doctors keep charting it as \\\"manageable\\\" while their patients can't thread needles and can't feel piano keys and can't part hair with their grandmother's comb.\\n\\nAnd here's what finally explained why everything else I tried also failed.\\n\\nAll those supplements I was taking? Cinnamon, apple cider vinegar, berberine, chromium, bitter melon, every single one of them was working downstream. They were trying to nudge insulin around the edges. Help my cells listen a little better to the wave of insulin my body dumps after a spike. None of them touched the pathway that's letting food flood my blood in the first place.\\n\\nI spent 2 years mopping the floor while the roof was leaking.\\n\\nThat's why I could drink cinnamon water at 5 AM every morning for a year and watch my A1C drop 2 points. That's why I could swallow apple cider vinegar before every meal until I dreaded eating and still see my post meal numbers spike. That's why I could fill my kitchen counter with bottles and spend $200 a month and still sit in my own braiding chair on a Saturday afternoon wondering if I had to close my shop.\\n\\nEverything I'd tried, the drug AND the natural alternatives, was aimed at the symptom. Nothing had addressed the broken pathway driving the symptom.\\n\\nSo I went back to the research. And I searched for the thing I should have been searching for from the beginning. \\\"What restores the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar.\\\"\\n\\nThat's when I found something I'd never heard of. And by the end of that night, I understood why nothing else had worked.\\n\\nThere's a fruit that's been used in Central Asia for over 5,000 years.\\n\\nThe healers who used it back then didn't have a name for blood sugar. They didn't know what insulin was. They didn't have a way to measure what was happening inside the body. But they knew what they saw. People who ate this fruit daily had steadier energy. Calmer digestion. They didn't crash after meals. They didn't fall into the slow decline everyone else seemed to. They lived longer. They aged better.\\n\\nThey called it the fruit of life.\\n\\nToday we call it pomegranate.\\n\\nI'd heard of pomegranate. You probably have too. That bothered me at first. If this worked, wouldn't I have seen it at the health food store next to the berberine that didn't work and the bitter melon that didn't move my numbers?\\n\\nBut you can't patent a fruit. And there's no billion dollar industry built around something that grows on a tree.\\n\\nThen I looked at the research. And I stopped caring that I'd heard of it. Because modern science now knows exactly why it works.\\n\\nPomegranate contains specific compounds called punicalagins. One of the most powerful compounds your body uses to slow the breakdown of food into sugar back down to its natural pace.\\n\\nHere's what punicalagins do.\\n\\nThey restore the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar in your blood. Not in your liver like Metformin. Not in your gut like cinnamon. Not insulin sensitization like berberine. The actual pathway that controls the speed of the breakdown in the first place.\\n\\nWhen the punicalagins are back in your system, the pathway has what it needs to do its job. Food enters your blood gently again. The way it's supposed to. The flood stops. Your insulin sees the trickle coming. It handles it calmly. The spikes ease. The crashes ease. The cravings ease.\\n\\nThe floodgate holds.\\n\\nThen I found the clinical analysis that made me sit back in my chair.\\n\\nA meta analysis of 34 clinical trials on standardized pomegranate extract. Across more than 1,500 people. Measurable improvements in fasting glucose. Insulin levels. Insulin resistance. Without depleting B12. Without numbness. Without the merry go round of side effects.\\n\\nA fruit. Backed by 34 clinical trials. And nobody in my doctor's office had ever mentioned it because you can't patent something that grows on a tree.\\n\\nI'll be honest. I almost didn't try it.\\n\\nI'd spent 2 years trying things that didn't work. I'd been burned by berberine that wrecked my stomach. By apple cider vinegar that ruined my appetite. By cinnamon that did almost nothing. Bottles on my counter and nothing to show for it except lighter pockets and the same numb hands.\\n\\nBut this was different. I could see it was different. Not because the marketing was better but because the mechanism was different. Everything else I'd tried was working downstream, on the symptoms, on the surface. This was working upstream, restoring the pathway that controlled how my body handled food in the first place. And it wasn't depleting B12. It wasn't blocking anything my body needed. It was giving my body back the raw material it had been missing.\\n\\nI found a company called Alevia that makes a standardized pomegranate extract. Not cheap fruit powder, not heat processed scraps with no consistency. A clinical strength extract, verified to contain the concentration of punicalagins that actually produces results. Organic. Third party tested for purity and potency. Made in the USA.\\n\\nI talked to my doctor about working with him to monitor my numbers monthly while I added something to my routine. He wasn't thrilled. But I told him my hands couldn't wait for him to be thrilled. We agreed to monitor my bloodwork closely.\\n\\nI ordered Alevia's pomegranate that night.\\n\\nThe first thing that changed was the mornings.\\n\\nDay 4. I woke up, rolled out of bed, and felt the sheet under my fingertips. Not strongly. But I felt it. No hot water. No 10 minutes standing over the sink rubbing fingertips that wouldn't wake up. Just felt it. The way you do without thinking. The way I used to before the drug took it from me.\\n\\nI stood in my bathroom at 5 AM staring at my own hands. I'd forgotten what waking up with feeling in my fingers felt like.\\n\\nBy week 2, my fasting glucose was running consistently lower. Not dramatically. But the kind of consistent drop that builds week after week. The kind of number that doesn't lie. And the deep numbness that hit every afternoon and had me dipping my hands in warm water between clients was fading. Not gone. Fading. Like someone slowly turning down a sound I'd gotten so used to I'd forgotten it was there.\\n\\nWeek 3, a client closed her eyes in my chair. First time in months someone had done that. She wasn't watching my hands. She just leaned back and closed her eyes the way you do when you trust the hands holding your hair. I almost had to stop braiding because the lump in my throat hit me out of nowhere.\\n\\nWeek 4, the parts were sharp again. The tension was even. The rhythm was back. I wasn't going slower to hide the shake because there was less shake to hide. The flow came back. That thing, the instinct, the thing you can't fake and 37 years of muscle memory either gives you or it doesn't, it was coming back.\\n\\nWeek 6, a new client sat down and asked if I did knotless box braids. I said not right now. She said, \\\"That's too bad. I heard you were the best.\\\" I looked at my hands. They were steady. Not still. I'm 61, there's a difference between youth and recovery. But steady.\\n\\nI thought about it for the rest of the day.\\n\\nLast Saturday.\\n\\nOne of my regulars, a woman I've been braiding for 19 years, sat down in my chair. Normal appointment. Normal day. She'd asked for twists.\\n\\nAnd then I stopped.\\n\\nI walked over to the wall. Took down the wooden parting comb. My grandmother's comb. The one that had been gathering dust for over a year. I ran my thumb across the wood. It was dry. Needed oil. I didn't care.\\n\\nI picked up the comb. Made the first part. Slow. Deliberate. The sound of the comb dividing the section so clean you could hear it. The other braiders looked up. The women waiting looked up. Nobody said a word.\\n\\nI turned to my client.\\n\\n\\\"Sit still for me, baby.\\\"\\n\\nI started sectioning for knotless. Pulling the smallest amount of her own hair at the base. Feeling the tension. Feeding in the extension. The way my grandmother taught me. The way I'd done it 10,000 times before the drug took it away.\\n\\nMy hands weren't shaking.\\n\\nWhen I finished the first braid I held it up where she could see it in the mirror. She looked at it. Looked at me. She'd been watching my hands decline for 2 years. She knew what this meant.\\n\\n\\\"Welcome back, Joanne.\\\"\\n\\nI didn't trust myself to speak. I just hung the comb back on the wall where it belongs and kept braiding.\\n\\nWhen she went to pay, I shook my head.\\n\\n\\\"This one's on me, sister.\\\"\\n\\nShe understood.\\n\\nI ran my bloodwork at 3 months.\\n\\nMy A1C: 5.9. On Metformin alone, it had been 6.2. With the pomegranate added in, my A1C was lower.\\n\\nFasting glucose: 102. On Metformin it had been 108. Lower.\\n\\nPost meal numbers: holding under 140 even after real meals. Bread, rice, the foods I used to be terrified of. The supplements I'd taken for 7 months never got my post meal numbers anywhere close to that.\\n\\nMy doctor studied the labs for a long time.\\n\\n\\\"Your numbers are the best I've seen on you in 9 years,\\\" he said. \\\"What changed?\\\"\\n\\nI told him. Pomegranate. The punicalagins. The 34 trial meta analysis. The 5,000 years of use. He listened. When I finished he said, \\\"I'm going to be honest with you. I don't know enough about this yet. But your numbers don't lie. And you look like a different woman than the one who was sitting in that chair 6 months ago.\\\"\\n\\nHe was right. I was a different woman. Because for 9 years a drug had been slowly erasing me, and in 3 months, I'd come back.\\n\\nI'm writing this for every woman sitting in a chair somewhere, a braiding chair, a sewing machine, a piano bench, a kitchen counter, whose hands aren't what they were.\\n\\n37 years. I wasn't ready to stop.\\n\\nIf you're on Metformin and your fingertips are tingling, your hands are numb in the mornings, your grip is weaker than it should be, your forearms ache in a way rest doesn't fix.\\n\\nIf you've been trying to manage your blood sugar naturally, the cinnamon, the apple cider vinegar, the berberine, the supplements that cost you $200 a month, and your numbers won't budge.\\n\\nIf your doctor keeps watching a number on a chart while your body falls apart.\\n\\nIf you've been told \\\"the benefits outweigh the risks\\\" by someone who doesn't have to live with the risks.\\n\\nMaybe it's not just aging. Maybe it's the drug. And maybe everything you've tried is working downstream while the problem is upstream. In the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar in your blood. Where nothing you've taken can reach.\\n\\nI spent 9 years on a drug that lowered a number while the pathway underneath stayed broken. Then I spent 2 years and thousands of dollars on supplements that were never going to reach the real problem.\\n\\nAlevia's pomegranate addressed it. The punicalagins restore the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar in your blood, the one thing the Metformin never did and the supplements never could. No B12 depletion. No enzyme blocking. No nerve damage. No numb hands.\\n\\nAlevia uses a process that preserves the active compounds at clinical strength. Organic. Third party tested for purity and potency. Made in the USA. 2 capsules every morning. That's it.\\n\\nThat's the difference between a supplement that sits in your system doing nothing and one that actually addresses the pathway driving your numbers up.\\n\\nRight now, they're running a promotion with significant savings. But Alevia is a small company. They make everything in small batches to preserve the active compounds. When they sell out, it's usually weeks before the next batch is ready.\\n\\n90 days to try it. If nothing changes, full refund. No questions asked.\\n\\nBut if it works?\\n\\nIf you wake up on day 4 and feel the sheet under your fingertips without running hot water over your hands for 10 minutes?\\n\\nIf by week 2 the numbness in your forearms starts fading and you stop dipping your hands in warm water between clients?\\n\\nIf a month from now your clients are closing their eyes in your chair again because they trust the hands holding their hair?\\n\\nIf 3 months from now you're sitting in your doctor's office watching him stare at numbers he can't explain, and he says \\\"whatever you're doing, keep doing it\\\"?\\n\\nIf your A1C comes down lower than it ever was on the drug alone?\\n\\nIf your grandmother's comb comes off the wall and goes back where it belongs?\\n\\nYou'll wish you started 9 years ago.\\n\\nI know I do.\\n\\n👉https://po.muvost.com/\\n\\nYou don't have to keep lowering a number while the pathway underneath stays broken.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"SHOP NOW\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://po.muvost.com/\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"po.muvost.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Organic Pomegranate Extract Capsules | AT2A\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"The 5,000-year-old fruit of life in a pure, potent organic extract.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":128,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":1,\"duplicates\":41,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AE\",\"AG\",\"AI\",\"AS\",\"AT\",\"AU\",\"AW\",\"BB\",\"BE\",\"BG\",\"BH\",\"BL\",\"BM\",\"BN\",\"BS\",\"BZ\",\"CA\",\"CC\",\"CG\",\"CK\",\"CO\",\"CW\",\"CY\",\"DE\",\"DK\",\"DM\",\"DO\",\"EC\",\"EE\",\"ES\",\"FJ\",\"FK\",\"FM\",\"FO\",\"GD\",\"GF\",\"GG\",\"GI\",\"GL\",\"GP\",\"GR\",\"GU\",\"GY\",\"IE\",\"IL\",\"IT\",\"JE\",\"JM\",\"JO\",\"KI\",\"KN\",\"KR\",\"KW\",\"KY\",\"LA\",\"LC\",\"MF\",\"MH\",\"MP\",\"MQ\",\"MS\",\"MX\",\"MY\",\"NF\",\"NR\",\"NU\",\"NZ\",\"OM\",\"PL\",\"PR\",\"PT\",\"PW\",\"RE\",\"SA\",\"SB\",\"SE\",\"SK\",\"SR\",\"SV\",\"SX\",\"TC\",\"TO\",\"TT\",\"US\",\"UY\",\"VC\",\"VG\",\"VI\",\"VU\",\"WS\",\"YT\"],\"mainCountry\":\"IE\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":62,\"currentRank\":62,\"rankDelta\":null,\"improvementPct\":7.62},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":true}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1056081283825222\",\"collationId\":\"1020695257239560\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-07-23T18:08:56.958Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-07-23T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-07-26T19:37:10.210Z\",\"daysRunning\":127,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/7cbf2f2869ec6525ebe942447f13534d82e9cdffafb0977da330f0a8667f1ddb.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1018883361310799\",\"name\":\"Sandra Keller, RN\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/1018883361310799.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1018883361310799\",\"liveAdsCount\":585,\"reach30d\":657004,\"totalReach\":4231542,\"countriesCount\":5,\"facebookLikes\":40683,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I don't give a f*ck if this upsets you. I am going to say something that nobody with Type 2 diabetes wants to hear.\\n\\nI placed my 1,412th coronary stent on October 3rd, 2024.\\n\\nSixteen days later, at 2:47 AM on October 19th, I was on the receiving end of one.\\n\\nI was 58 years old.\\n\\nI am a board-certified interventional cardiologist. Twenty-two years of practice. Two stents placed in my own left anterior descending artery by a fellow I had trained three years earlier.\\n\\nI coded on the table for eleven seconds.\\n\\nThe hospital bill was $71,000. Cardiac rehab was another $9,200.\\n\\nThey will let me operate again. My hospital privileges are intact. But I will never place a stent again without remembering that I was on that same table with someone I had trained running a wire into my own artery because everything I was taught to prescribe had not saved me.\\n\\nI am writing this because I know exactly what my colleagues will say when they see it. I do not care.\\n\\nIf you are over 50 and you have Type 2 diabetes, or someone you love does, please read the entire thing.\\n\\nI know it is long. I know you are scrolling. I know you have things to do.\\n\\nFifteen months ago I would have given anything for someone with my credential to sit me down and tell me what I am about to tell you.\\n\\nNobody did.\\n\\nI was diagnosed with Type 2 diabetes six years ago at 52. Fasting glucose 141. A1C 6.9.\\n\\nThe diagnosis surprised me and it did not. My father had died of a myocardial infarction at 64. He was a Type 2 diabetic managed on M*tformin and a blood pressure pill for eleven years. My mother's A1C was borderline for a decade before she passed. I had watched the pattern in my own family and I had built my entire practice on treating cardiac events in patients who looked like my father.\\n\\nI did what any cardiologist would prescribe.\\n\\nI started M*tformin at diagnosis. My internist, a colleague at my own hospital, added Lisinopril the following year when my blood pressure crept to 138 over 84. She added a statin the year after that when my LDL climbed to 149.\\n\\nThree medications. Two of them medications I had personally prescribed to hundreds of my own patients.\\n\\nI walked four miles a day. I stopped drinking. I lost 18 pounds in the first year of the diagnosis and kept it off. I ate the Mediterranean protocol I recommend to every patient after a stent placement. Lean protein. Olive oil. Leafy greens. Minimal refined carbohydrates. I ordered a coronary calcium scan on myself the year I was diagnosed. Score of 12.\\n\\nI checked every box on my own protocol.\\n\\nBy last September, my A1C was 6.2. My blood pressure was 128 over 80. My LDL was 96.\\n\\nBy the standard of the guidelines I had helped write for our hospital's cardiology group, I was managed.\\n\\nManaged.\\n\\nThat word again.\\n\\nI ordered a second coronary calcium scan on myself last September, six years after the first one. I did it because a hunch would not leave me alone.\\n\\nThe number came back at 847.\\n\\nFor context, I have called patients over the phone with scores half that size and used the sentence, we need to talk today, not tomorrow.\\n\\nI called my interventionalist partner and asked her to do a stress test on me. She did. It was equivocal. She recommended a CT angiogram to be safe.\\n\\nI scheduled it. And then I did not go.\\n\\nI want to tell you why.\\n\\nBecause sitting in my office at 6 PM staring at my own calcium score on my own screen, I recognized something. I was doing exactly what my father had done. He had been managed for eleven years before the heart attack that killed him. He had been on Lisinopril and a statin. His A1C had read fine. His numbers had been controlled.\\n\\nAnd he had died at 64 in the emergency room of the hospital where I did my cardiology fellowship.\\n\\nI did not go to my own CT angiogram because I already knew what it would show. I did not want to see it in writing. I told myself I had time.\\n\\nTwenty-four days later I woke my husband at 2 AM.\\n\\nThe pain started in my chest at 1:52 AM. I remember the time because I looked at my bedside clock and thought, this is not indigestion.\\n\\nIt was not.\\n\\nIt was the pressure I had described to a thousand patients in follow-up appointments. Central. Substernal. Radiating into my left jaw. My husband, an oral surgeon, sat up before I could finish the sentence. He is not a cardiac clinician but he has heard me describe this exact presentation at our dinner table for twenty years.\\n\\nHe drove me.\\n\\nWe went to a competing hospital, not my own. Because I did not want the fellows I had trained to see me on that gurney.\\n\\nThey saw me anyway.\\n\\nThe interventionalist on call was a woman I had trained through her fellowship in 2021. She walked into the room, saw me on the gurney, and her face did the thing.\\n\\nShe said my name.\\n\\n— I need a cath, I told her. LAD occlusion. Ninety-five percent or better. Get me in the lab.\\n\\n— Yes, ma'am, she said.\\n\\nThey wheeled me down the corridor I had walked down thousands of times going the other direction. I looked at the ceiling tiles and counted them because I did not want to look at the fellow who was about to run a wire into my heart.\\n\\nI coded on the table forty seconds after they got me on it. My fellow later told me my rhythm went ventricular fibrillation, then flatlined for eleven seconds before they shocked me back.\\n\\nTwo stents in the left anterior descending. Both in the same twenty-minute window I had done for other patients four hundred times.\\n\\nI was in the ICU for three days. Cardiac step-down for four. I came home on October 26th with my husband, a cane, two stents in my chest, and a shopping bag of prescriptions I had personally recommended to hundreds of patients over twenty-two years.\\n\\nI did not want the full metabolic workup my colleagues were suggesting.\\n\\nI ran it on myself anyway.\\n\\nBecause I knew that if I did not, in eighteen months I would be reading somebody else's autopsy from an outside institution and seeing my own name on it.\\n\\nThe nephrology consult was with a colleague I had trained with in residency. She pulled my last four creatinine readings up on her screen. They had been climbing quietly for three years. My eGFR was 71 and had been 89 four years earlier. She used the phrase, early stage 2 diabetic nephropathy. Nobody had flagged it because my A1C had read managed and a creatinine of 1.1 does not trigger a nephrology referral in the standard-of-care algorithm I had signed off on for our hospital.\\n\\nThe retinal specialist found small hemorrhages in both my eyes. He used the phrase, early background retinopathy. I had been squinting to read echocardiogram reports for eight months. I had blamed my reading glasses.\\n\\nThe hepatologist ordered a liver ultrasound. Grade 2 hepatic steatosis. My ALT had been 47 for two years. My colleagues had said, keep an eye on it. Nobody kept an eye on it.\\n\\nThe podiatry consult ran a nerve conduction study. Measurable peripheral neuropathy in both feet, right worse than left. I had been dropping surgical instruments occasionally for six months. I had blamed a wrist strain.\\n\\nEvery one of my organs was showing the pattern I had watched in hundreds of my own patients after a first cardiac event.\\n\\nSame disease. Same trapped sugar. Damage in my feet. Damage in my kidneys. Damage in my eyes. Damage in my liver. And finally, damage in my heart.\\n\\nI sat in my office on November 8th with all five reports in front of me and I understood something I had never let myself understand before.\\n\\nEvery drug in my medication cascade had been treating a downstream measurement.\\n\\nM*tformin was treating my glucose output.\\n\\nLisinopril was treating my blood pressure.\\n\\nThe statin was treating my LDL.\\n\\nNot one of them had touched what was doing this to five organs at once.\\n\\nI am a cardiologist. I am supposed to be the person who knows how to prevent this. I had prescribed the exact three-drug protocol I was on to a thousand patients who looked like my father. Every one of those prescriptions had passed peer review. Every one had followed the guidelines. Every one had been the correct standard of care.\\n\\nAnd it had failed me at 58.\\n\\nI sat at my desk that night at 11 PM after my husband had gone to bed and I did something I had not done since medical school.\\n\\nI opened PubMed and I searched for the actual mechanism.\\n\\nNot the management guidelines. Not the drug metabolism papers. The mechanism.\\n\\nI read for four hours.\\n\\nI found what my training had covered lightly and never in the depth it deserved. GLUT4. Insulin receptor substrate. Endothelial nitric oxide synthase impairment secondary to chronic hyperglycemic exposure. NF-kB activation in the arterial wall. The mechanistic explanation for why the three medications I had been prescribing for twenty-two years managed downstream measurements without addressing the upstream failure driving all of them.\\n\\nNone of this was hidden. It sat in journals I had had access to for twenty-two years. Diabetes Care. Circulation. The New England Journal.\\n\\nI had read those journals every month. I had read them for the interventional trials. I had never read them for the mechanism papers because my training had told me the mechanism was managed and the treatment was to manage the downstream measurements.\\n\\nMy training was wrong.\\n\\nI closed my laptop at 3 AM. I sat at my kitchen table. I could not sleep.\\n\\nI understood at that moment that I had prescribed the exact protocol that failed me to hundreds of other people. And every one of them was walking around with the same trapped sugar grinding through their vessels, waiting for their October 19th to arrive.\\n\\nTwo weeks later I saw a patient in follow-up who should not have been in the numbers she was in.\\n\\nHer name was Priyanka. Type 2 diabetic. Twelve years post-diagnosis. She had been my patient for three years after transferring to me following a stent placement. Her A1C at intake three years earlier had been 7.8. She had been on M*tformin, Lisinopril, and a statin.\\n\\nI pulled her chart before she came in expecting to add another medication.\\n\\nHer A1C was 5.6.\\n\\nHer LDL was 82.\\n\\nHer blood pressure was 118 over 74. She had come off Lisinopril twenty months earlier at her own insistence and her numbers had not moved back up.\\n\\nI looked at her across the exam room and asked her what she was doing.\\n\\nShe was quiet for a moment. Then she said, — Doctor, my aunt Kumari runs a small community in central New Jersey. She has been telling all of us for years. I did not bring it up because I did not think you would take it seriously.\\n\\nI asked her what her aunt had told her.\\n\\nShe wrote a name and an address on the back of my prescription pad.\\n\\nKumari Perera. A town I had never heard of in central New Jersey. A house with a garden of Sri Lankan medicinal plants she had transplanted from her grandmother's village.\\n\\nI drove there the following Saturday.\\n\\nI did not tell my husband where I was going.\\n\\nI did not tell my practice partner.\\n\\nI did not tell anyone in my institution that a board-certified interventional cardiologist was driving three hours to meet a 79-year-old Sri Lankan grandmother because everything I had been taught in twenty-two years of practice had not saved me.\\n\\nI pulled into the driveway of a small ranch house at 11:40 AM.\\n\\nThe garden along the front walkway was unlike anything I had seen. Curry leaf. Gotu kola. A small cinnamon tree by the front door. Plants I recognized from the botanical section of pharmacology journals I had not thought about since medical school.\\n\\nShe was on the porch sorting bark into a wooden bowl. Small woman. Thin. Late seventies. Dark hair pulled back with gray streaks running through it.\\n\\nNo swollen knuckles. No brace on her wrist. Just easy, steady work.\\n\\nI introduced myself. Not as a cardiologist. As Sandra. She looked up with sharp clear eyes and asked me why I had come.\\n\\nI told her.\\n\\nI told her I was a cardiologist. Twenty-two years in practice. That I had my own heart attack five weeks earlier. That my three medications had not saved me. That five organs in my body were showing the pattern I had watched in hundreds of my own patients. That one of my patients had told me about her.\\n\\nShe listened. She did not interrupt.\\n\\nWhen I finished she set the bowl down and said, — Come inside, doctor.\\n\\nHer kitchen smelled like cardamom and cinnamon.\\n\\nShe poured me tea without asking.\\n\\nShe sat across from me and said, — Your medications are fighting the wrong battle. You know this. That is why you are sitting at my kitchen table.\\n\\nI did know it.\\n\\nShe said, — Every drug you prescribed for yourself. Every drug you prescribed for your patients. All of them work on the number on the chart. That is all. The trapped sugar underneath — the sugar that is still moving through your vessels every heartbeat, every meal, every year — none of them touch it.\\n\\nI said, — I understand the mechanism. I read the papers last week.\\n\\nShe smiled. — Then you understand it clinically. You do not yet understand it in your own body.\\n\\nShe picked up two pieces of cinnamon bark from her bowl. Then a third. Then a fourth. Then a fifth.\\n\\nShe set them on the table in front of me.\\n\\n— This one is your feet. This one is your kidneys. This one is your eyes. This one is your liver. This one is your heart.\\n\\nShe tapped each piece.\\n\\n— Same trapped sugar. Grinding through all five. Every one of your specialists has told you they are treating five different problems. It is one problem. You already know this. But you have been trained for twenty-two years to prescribe for each of them separately.\\n\\nI nodded. I could not speak.\\n\\nShe said, — The M*tformin quiets your glucose number. The Lisinopril quiets your blood pressure number. The statin quiets your LDL number. Three numbers on three different chart lines. Below all three, the trapped sugar has been grinding through your endothelium. Year after year. Every heartbeat. Every meal.\\n\\nShe leaned forward.\\n\\n— The stents your fellow placed did not repair the arterial wall. They opened a channel through a wall that is still failing. If the trapped sugar is not addressed, you will be back in that cath lab within four years for another vessel. This is not opinion. This is the mechanism you already understand.\\n\\nI said, — I know.\\n\\nShe said, — Now here is what nobody taught you in medical school. For over a thousand years the women in my grandmother's village have been using one thing for this. My grandmother grew it. Her grandmother before her. I am seventy-nine. I have never been on a medication. My A1C has never been elevated. My daughters do not have diabetes.\\n\\nShe stood. She walked to a shelf. She pulled down a dark glass jar. Inside were curled pieces of deep red-brown bark, layered like rolled paper.\\n\\n— True Ceylon cinnamon, she said. Cinnamomum verum. Not what your patients are buying on Amazon. What is sold as cinnamon at the drugstore is cassia. A different plant entirely. Cheaper. From China and Vietnam. It contains almost none of the active compounds. It contains up to two hundred and fifty times more coumarin, which strains the liver at daily supplement doses.\\n\\nShe set the jar down.\\n\\n— Real Ceylon has two compounds your body needs. The first is called MHCP. This is what opens the cellular doors your training tells you are managed by insulin. Once the doors open, the trapped sugar finally moves out of your bloodstream and into your cells where it becomes energy. The grinding through your vessels stops.\\n\\nShe held up the jar.\\n\\n— The second is called cinnamaldehyde. The compound that gives cinnamon its smell. It repairs the endothelial lining of the vessels the trapped sugar has been damaging for years. The vessels feeding your feet. Your kidneys. Your eyes. Your liver. And the arteries feeding your heart. The ones your fellow placed stents in three weeks ago.\\n\\nShe looked at me.\\n\\n— Two compounds. From one plant. One opens the doors. The other heals what the trapped sugar damaged while the doors were closed. That is why the women in my grandmother's village do not have heart attacks like yours.\\n\\nI asked her if the form mattered.\\n\\nShe nodded.\\n\\n— Real Ceylon has to be at the right species, at the clinical dose, suspended in the right oil. Cassia does nothing. Ceylon at the drugstore is in dry capsule form. The active compounds are fat-soluble. Without an oil to carry them into the bloodstream, most of what you swallow passes through the body and never absorbs. In Sri Lanka we eat cinnamon with coconut. In every dish. My grandmother did not know the biochemistry. She knew that in our kitchen, coconut and cinnamon go together in every meal. That is why our women live into their nineties without heart disease.\\n\\nShe paused.\\n\\n— There is a company my niece in Boston told me about. They source the real species. DNA-verified from Sri Lanka. They concentrate it twelve to one to match the dose in the published research. They suspend it in MCT oil, which is coconut-derived. The same oil my grandmother used. Just concentrated into a softgel so it actually absorbs. Third-party tested. Made in the USA.\\n\\nShe wrote a name on the back of a receipt and slid it across the table.\\n\\nMetabolae.\\n\\nI ordered three bags from her kitchen table before I left New Jersey.\\n\\nI drove three hours home.\\n\\nI did not tell my husband where I had been.\\n\\nThe bags arrived the following Wednesday.\\n\\nI took 1 softgel with my breakfast the next morning.\\n\\nWeek one.\\n\\nMy afternoon energy returned. I had been napping in my office between afternoon consults since the heart attack. On day five, I read the medical journals I had let stack up for six weeks. I read all four in one sitting without dozing off. I sat at my desk at 4 PM and I recognized that I felt awake for the first time in five weeks.\\n\\nWeek two.\\n\\nThe tingling in both feet that my podiatry consult had flagged began to quiet. I had been sleeping with my feet outside the covers since August because the burning would wake me at 3 AM. That week I pulled the covers up for the first time.\\n\\nWeek three.\\n\\nI ran a home BP cuff twice a day. My readings had been sitting at 132 over 82 since discharge. That week they moved to 122 over 76. I had not changed a single medication.\\n\\nWeek four.\\n\\nI ordered my own lipid panel through my hospital's outpatient lab. LDL 84. Triglycerides down 62 points from my discharge labs. I ran the panel twice because I did not believe it. Both runs matched.\\n\\nWeek six.\\n\\nI ran a fasting glucose panel. 94. Down from 138 at discharge. I ordered a follow-up A1C two weeks later.\\n\\nWeek eight.\\n\\nA1C 5.7. Down from 6.6 at discharge.\\n\\nWeek twelve.\\n\\nFull metabolic panel plus lipids plus renal.\\n\\nA1C 5.4.\\n\\nFasting glucose 87.\\n\\nLDL 71.\\n\\nTriglycerides down 84 points from discharge.\\n\\nCreatinine back to 0.9. eGFR climbed from 71 to 83.\\n\\nLiver enzymes back in normal range.\\n\\nBlood pressure sitting at 116 over 72 without any change to my medication.\\n\\nI called my nephrologist colleague back and asked her to repeat the renal panel. She did. Same numbers.\\n\\nI sat in my office at 6 PM staring at my own labs on my screen and I understood that I was looking at a reversal I had not seen in a cardiology chart in twenty-two years.\\n\\nI brought the labs to my practice partner. Another interventional cardiologist. Fifteen years of practice. Someone I had trained.\\n\\nI put the discharge panel next to the twelve-week panel on her desk and I did not say anything.\\n\\nShe read them.\\n\\nShe looked at me.\\n\\n— Sandra. What did you do.\\n\\nI told her.\\n\\nThe Sri Lankan grandmother. The kitchen table. The two compounds. The trapped sugar. All of it.\\n\\nShe listened for forty minutes without interrupting. When I finished she was quiet for a long time.\\n\\nThen she said, — I have not been trained on this. But I want to see this in my own patients. Tell me the name of the product.\\n\\nI gave it to her.\\n\\nShe has been running it with fourteen of her post-stent patients for the last three months. She called me last week. Nine of them have moved out of pre-diabetic range. Three have come off Lisinopril at their internists' recommendation.\\n\\nI am still on the two stents. I am still on Lisinopril at a reduced dose. My statin was discontinued at week fourteen by my internist.\\n\\nI ate rice with my husband last week for the first time in six years. One bowl at a small Thai restaurant near our house. My glucose the following morning was 91.\\n\\nMy last A1C two weeks ago was 5.3.\\n\\nI came within eleven seconds on the table of dying at 58.\\n\\nI am writing this because I still have my practice. I still have my husband. And I have watched every complication in my body reverse. I have watched a colleague I trained look at my labs and say the word I had waited eleven months to hear.\\n\\nImproving.\\n\\nNot managed.\\n\\nImproving.\\n\\nI am also writing this because I know the truth about what my profession is doing.\\n\\nI did not sit at that grandmother's kitchen table because I wanted to. I sat there because everything my medical school and my board certification and my twenty-two years of practice had taught me about how to treat this disease had failed me.\\n\\nI placed 1,412 stents before I placed one in myself. Every one of those stents was in a person walking the same trajectory I was walking. Every one of them left my cath lab with the same three-drug protocol I was on. Every one of them was told they were managed.\\n\\nI do not know how many of them are already back.\\n\\nI do not know how many of them are already gone.\\n\\nI know that the standard of care my hospital taught me was built on the assumption that managing three downstream numbers with three separate medications was equivalent to addressing the mechanism driving all three. It is not. I have five organs' worth of scan reports in my own body that prove it is not. And my father's autopsy report from thirty-four years ago proves it was not for him either.\\n\\nIf your husband, your father, your brother, your mother, or you are a Type 2 diabetic and you have been told your A1C is managed while your body is quietly falling apart, please listen to what I am telling you.\\n\\nThe trapped sugar is what is killing you. Not the number on the chart.\\n\\nThe M*tformin lowers the number. It does not open the doors. It does not move the trapped sugar. It does not repair the damage the trapped sugar has already done to your feet, your kidneys, your eyes, your liver, and your heart.\\n\\nCutting the bread does not save you. I did that for six years and it did not stop what was coming.\\n\\nOz*mpic does not save you. Same idea. Different lever. Neither one opens the doors.\\n\\nThere is one thing I know of that opens the doors and repairs the damage. The two compounds in real Ceylon cinnamon. MHCP opens the doors. Cinnamaldehyde repairs the vessels. Together, at the right dose, in the right delivery form, they address what my three prescriptions did not touch.\\n\\nMetabolae.\\n\\nDNA-verified true Ceylon from Sri Lanka. Concentrated twelve to one to match the dose in the clinical research. Suspended in organic MCT oil so the active compounds actually absorb. Third-party tested. Made in the USA.\\n\\n1 softgel every morning with breakfast. That is the whole protocol.\\n\\n90-day money-back guarantee. If your numbers do not move, you pay nothing.\\n\\nThe system that trained me did not give this to me when I needed it.\\n\\nIt is reaching you now.\\n\\nP.S. My father was a Type 2 diabetic managed on M*tformin and a blood pressure pill for eleven years before he died in the emergency room at 64. My mother's A1C was borderline for a decade before she passed. I built my entire cardiology practice on the assumption that I would prevent for my patients what I could not prevent for my father. Instead I have watched myself walk the same trajectory in my own body. The difference is that I got the eleven-second warning my father did not get. Please do not let your family's warning be shorter than mine was.\\n\\nP.P.S. I do not work for Metabolae. I do not receive a penny from this. I am writing this at 11:20 PM on a Sunday, alone in my office, because a patient came in Friday whose LDL had dropped 60 points in six weeks and I recognized my own numbers on her chart. She had found Metabolae on her own after reading a post from a woman named Carolyn about her husband Robert. She told me the post had been circulating among the Type 2 diabetics in her book club. I read it that night. It was the second time in six months I had recognized my own patient in a story written by a stranger. Please share this with anyone you love who has Type 2 diabetes. Even a little. Even borderline. Even if they think they are managed. I promise you they are not.\\n\\n~ Dr. Sandra Keller, MD, FACC, age 60, interventional cardiology\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/lp-ceylon-cinnamon-insulin-1\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Metabolae\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":51704,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":465,\"duplicates\":40,\"reachDelta1d\":118,\"reachDelta7d\":1807,\"reachDelta30d\":2557},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AU\",\"CA\",\"GB\",\"NZ\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":116,\"currentRank\":116,\"rankDelta\":1,\"improvementPct\":19.83},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1284117653324199\",\"collationId\":\"1036735228509883\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2025-08-04T21:53:11.159Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2025-07-01T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-02-11T08:00:00.000Z\",\"daysRunning\":225,\"media\":{\"type\":\"video\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/thumbnails/edb6c86e71ace24b35164ceb4728b1400bae216bbb668e067532725c1ac97fbe.jpg\",\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/video/cdc87b097d47a34757cea790317e84394bafb2ddccd336ac1a1a1275a8c54ddf.mp4\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"217751718277989\",\"name\":\"PipingRock\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/217751718277989.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"217751718277989\",\"liveAdsCount\":5,\"reach30d\":70238,\"totalReach\":11133712,\"countriesCount\":3,\"facebookLikes\":66849,\"instagramFollowers\":29895},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Piping Rock's Berberine + Ceylon Cinnamon =  the dynamic duo for you\\n🌟 Advanced 2,000 mg Complex\\n🌟 Convenient Vegetarian Capsules\",\"transcript\":\"Did you know there are simple, powerful ways to keep your overall health in check? I've always loved using herbs, so finding out that I could use them in supplement form, that was a pleasant surprise. Herbarine paired with Salon Cinnamon is a dynamic duo. Just one vegetarian capsule a day. Easy, effective, and made possible by piping rock. I honestly couldn't ask for any more.\",\"callToAction\":\"SHOP NOW\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"http://pipingrock.com/en-ie/products/berberine-ceylon-cinnamon-complex-2000-mg-120-vegetarian-capsules-16241?prd=12e99ca1&utm_medium=social&utm_source=facebook&utm_campaign=ll25engintieberberine&utm_term=12e99ca1\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"pipingrock.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"{{product.name}}\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"100% Satisfaction Guaranteed\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":40,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"GB\",\"IE\",\"NL\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":\"all\",\"ageMin\":18,\"ageMax\":65},\"rank\":null,\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":true,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1083733121236523\",\"collationId\":\"1129385330038263\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-12T01:24:04.234Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-10-11T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-10-19T01:40:38.024Z\",\"daysRunning\":8,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/26076c12428e9f5503b5d0434ed5ed2c0f1ecc98ebce74f6a40ce62cda7102b8.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1105421125992305\",\"name\":\"Blood Sugar Relief Community\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/1105421125992305.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1105421125992305\",\"liveAdsCount\":0,\"reach30d\":65,\"totalReach\":3467,\"countriesCount\":6,\"facebookLikes\":8,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Tried berberine before and it either wrecked your stomach or did absolutely nothing? 🫠\\n\\nNot your fault. Not the ingredient. The delivery.\\n\\nCapsules take 45 minutes to dissolve. ⏱️\\nYour blood sugar spikes 15 minutes after eating. 📈\\n\\nThe capsule never had a chance. And your gut paid the price for it getting nowhere near the problem.\\n\\nGLUCO GONE is liquid. Sublingual. Under your tongue.\\n\\n90 seconds → bloodstream → liver. Before the spike starts. Zero gut destruction. Zero waiting.\\n\\nAnd it's not just berberine. 12 ingredients working together 👇\\n\\n🌿 Berberine — clinically shown to support healthy glucose metabolism\\n🌿 Gymnema — blocks sugar receptors. Cravings gone.\\n🌿 Ceylon Cinnamon — insulin sensitivity support\\n🌿 Bitter Melon + Chromium + Turmeric — multi-pathway glucose control\\n\\nWhat Ozempic does for $1,200 a month — naturally. No prescription. No injection. No side effects. 💉❌\\n\\n⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ 1,503+ women. A1C dropping. Cravings quieting. Weight following.\\n\\n90-day guarantee. You keep the bottle either way.\\n\\n👇 Try the formula that actually reaches your liver.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://try-elixir.co/products/berberine-with-ceylon-cinnamon-drops?selling_plan=690157846813&variant=50166257352989\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"try-elixir.co\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Finally. A Blood Sugar Drop That Doesn't Destroy Your Gut.\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"You Don't Fight Cravings With This. They Just Stop.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":15,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":0,\"duplicates\":37,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AU\",\"CA\",\"GB\",\"NZ\",\"SG\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":9,\"currentRank\":9,\"rankDelta\":null,\"improvementPct\":15.79},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1141954384968585\",\"collationId\":\"1406478794324497\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-03T11:44:28.262Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-10-02T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-10-14T17:20:15.947Z\",\"daysRunning\":12,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/c836813489c0fb01f2864cc54445e02bb199d2c97ff88e58b8211453a102e260.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"114943371701055\",\"name\":\"Diaz Rivera Raegan\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/114943371701055.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"114943371701055\",\"liveAdsCount\":0,\"reach30d\":0,\"totalReach\":1153396,\"countriesCount\":87,\"facebookLikes\":451,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I've been on Metformin for 9 years. I've been a braider for 37. A wife for 33. A grandmother for 7. The drug gave me perfect numbers. Then it took my hands.\\n\\nI need to tell you what happened, because if your blood sugar is high and you're taking Metformin or thinking about it or trying to manage it on your own with cinnamon and apple cider vinegar and every other thing the internet says works, you need to read this. All of it.\\n\\nMy name is Joanne. I'm 61 years old. I opened my shop when I was 24, saved every dollar from 5 years of braiding hair on my grandmother's porch and put a deposit on a storefront that smelled like floor cleaner and old paint. Built it from nothing. Survived three recessions. Outlasted a strip mall demolition. Moved twice. Rebuilt twice. Never once closed. Not for COVID. Not for anything.\\n\\nI braided the way I was taught. Knotless box braids that lay flat from the scalp. Fulani patterns that looked like they were stitched in by hand. Tension so clean the parts could cut you. My grandmother was a braider. She braided in our living room from a wooden stool, six clients a day, every day, for 40 years. She gave me her wooden parting comb when I opened my shop and said, \\\"The day your fingers stop feeling the hair is the day you stop.\\\" I hung that comb on the wall behind my chair and I've used it every working day for 37 years.\\n\\nOr I did. Until two years ago.\\n\\nMy doctor put me on Metformin when I was 52. A1C was 7.4. He said 1000 milligrams, twice a day, come back in 90 days. I didn't question it. Why would I? I was healthy. I was active. I was on my feet 10 hours a day braiding hair. But my numbers were high and he said the drug was the smart move.\\n\\n90 days later my A1C was 6.6. Fasting glucose dropped from 156 to 118. He was thrilled. I was thrilled. The drug was doing exactly what it was supposed to do.\\n\\nSome tingling in my fingers in the mornings. Nothing I thought twice about. I was 52. I'd been working with my hands since I was a teenager. Tingling happens.\\n\\nBy year three the tingling had a name. Numbness.\\n\\nMy hands. Fingertips first, then up through my fingers. A dull, dead feeling that settled in around noon every day and didn't let up until I went to bed. Mornings were the worst. I couldn't feel the comb in my hand when I woke up. Had to run my hands under hot water for 10 minutes before I could grip a section of hair properly. 10 minutes. Every morning. Standing over the sink at 5 AM rubbing fingertips that didn't want to wake up, trying to get them ready for a day of work they used to do without thinking.\\n\\nI told my doctor.\\n\\nHe ran my labs. Looked at the A1C.\\n\\n\\\"Your A1C is 6.4. Fasting glucose is 112. These are excellent numbers, Joanne. Whatever you're doing, keep doing it.\\\"\\n\\nI said, \\\"I'm not asking about my A1C. I'm asking about my hands.\\\"\\n\\nHe said neuropathy is a known issue in diabetic patients. Affects some people. For most, the benefits of the medication significantly outweigh the risks. He'd keep an eye on it.\\n\\nKeep an eye on it.\\n\\nEasy to say when your hands aren't your whole life.\\n\\nBy year five the knotless box braids were gone.\\n\\nNot all at once. I didn't wake up one morning and decide to stop. It happened in pieces. First I stopped booking knotless on Mondays because my hands were stiffest at the start of the week. Then Mondays became Monday through Wednesday. Then I started telling new clients I was only doing regular box braids and twists. Then it was twists only, all the time, for everyone.\\n\\nWomen used to drive across town specifically for my knotless. I could feed in extensions so smoothly the braid looked like it grew that way. That wasn't a skill I learned in a year. That was 30 years of muscle memory built one braid at a time. And I could feel it leaving.\\n\\nThe shaking started around year five. My hands. Not dramatic. Just a fine tremor when I tried to hold tension on a small section. Barely visible to anyone who wasn't looking. But I was looking. Every day I was looking. Every time I picked up a section of hair I was watching my own fingers for the shake, hoping today would be the day it stopped.\\n\\nIt didn't stop. It got worse.\\n\\nClients started noticing. Nobody said anything. You don't tell your braider her hands are shaking the same way you don't tell a singer her voice is going. But I saw them glance in the mirror. I saw the regulars who used to close their eyes in my chair and lean back into the work now watching my hands with both eyes open.\\n\\nI started going slower. A woman who's braided over a thousand heads doesn't suddenly need 4 extra hours unless something is wrong. I was going slower because slower meant steadier. Slower meant I could hide the shake. Slower meant nobody had to see that the hands they'd trusted for years were failing.\\n\\nThe wooden parting comb, my grandmother's comb, started gathering dust.\\n\\nI went back to my doctor. Told him the numbness was worse. Showed him my hands. Told him I couldn't do knotless anymore, the thing that made my shop my shop, the thing women drove across town for, the thing my grandmother taught me, gone.\\n\\nHe looked at my chart.\\n\\n\\\"Joanne, your A1C is 6.2. Fasting glucose is 108. These are excellent numbers. I understand the neuropathy is frustrating, but the cardiovascular and kidney protection Metformin provides at your age is significant. The benefits outweigh the risks. I'd strongly recommend you stay on it.\\\"\\n\\nThe benefits outweigh the risks.\\n\\nHe said it the way you'd compliment a child's report card. Like the number on the chart was the only thing that mattered. Like the woman attached to the chart, the woman whose hands were dying, whose livelihood was disappearing, whose grandmother's comb was collecting dust, was irrelevant.\\n\\nI decided if the doctor wasn't going to help me, I'd help myself. Maybe I could get my numbers down naturally that I could lower the Metformin dose. Maybe come off it entirely with my doctor's approval. Get my hands back.\\n\\nSo I tried everything. And I mean everything.\\n\\nI started with cinnamon. Real Ceylon cinnamon. Not the cheap stuff. Every morning at 5 AM, stirring it into hot water because somebody told me it slows down how fast carbs hit your blood. I did that for a year. A full year. 365 mornings of cinnamon water. My A1C dropped 2 points. 2 points. My hands were still numb.\\n\\nApple cider vinegar. 2 tablespoons before every meal. The kind with the mother in it. 7 months. My morning numbers came down a little. Post meal? Nothing. And the taste was so bad I'd dread sitting down to dinner.\\n\\nBerberine. The one everybody on TikTok was calling nature's Ozempic. Three capsules a day, three different brands over six months. The cramping was worse than the Metformin. My numbers barely moved.\\n\\nMy doctor glanced at the results and said, \\\"Your A1C looks great, Joanne. Keep taking the Metformin.\\\"\\n\\nI could have put my fist through the wall. Except my fist didn't fully close anymore.\\n\\nAfter that I tried everything else I could get my hands on. Chromium. Bitter melon. Fenugreek. Banaba leaf. Gymnema sylvestre. Alpha lipoic acid because I'd read it helps with neuropathy. CoQ10. Magnesium. Psyllium husk. Some for months. Some for weeks. None of them moved the needle. My kitchen counter looked like a pharmacy. My husband counted the bottles one night and said, \\\"You have more pills than the Metformin was supposed to replace.\\\"\\n\\nHe was right.\\n\\nAnd the worst part? Through all of it, my A1C barely moved without the drug. And my hands kept getting worse on it.\\n\\nI was doing everything you're supposed to do. Everything the blogs say. Everything the health food store recommends. Everything my cousin swears by and everything the lady at church credits for her numbers dropping. None of it worked. And I couldn't figure out why.\\n\\nThe day I almost closed my shop was a Saturday in November.\\n\\nI was sectioning hair for a regular, a woman I'd been braiding for 16 years. Simple style. Big box braids, nothing fancy. Should have been a 4 hour appointment. I was holding her section in my left hand and my fingers seized. Not shook. SEIZED. The muscles in my hand cramped and my fingers locked and the section slipped through and pulled her hair the wrong direction.\\n\\nShe winced.\\n\\nI pulled back. Apologized. She told me it was fine. But I saw her glance at her phone after that, checking the time more than she should have. She didn't say anything. I didn't say anything. I finished the braids. She paid and left.\\n\\nShe didn't rebook.\\n\\nI sat in my own chair after she left. The shop was empty. 6 PM on a Saturday, a time when 3 women should have been waiting. I sat there looking at my hands, palms up, and I realized these aren't my hands anymore. These are what's left of my hands after 9 years on a drug that was supposed to keep me healthy.\\n\\nI looked at the photographs on the wall. The first dollar bill in a frame by the register. The wooden parting comb.\\n\\nMy grandmother said the day your fingers stop feeling the hair is the day you stop.\\n\\nI called my husband and told him I was thinking about closing. He was quiet for a long time. Then he said, \\\"Don't close yet. Don't make that decision yet. Just don't decide today.\\\"\\n\\nI didn't close. But I went home that night knowing something had to change. The Metformin was destroying me. The supplements weren't helping me. And my doctor was watching a number on a chart while my career died.\\n\\nI didn't start with the right question. I started where everyone starts. \\\"Can Metformin cause hand neuropathy.\\\"\\n\\n200,000 results.\\n\\n\\\"Metformin B12 depletion.\\\" Millions.\\n\\n\\\"Metformin nerve damage hands.\\\" Forums full of women describing exactly what I was living. Quilters who couldn't thread a needle. Pianists who couldn't feel the keys. Surgeons, SURGEONS, who had to stop operating. I wasn't alone. I wasn't crazy. And I wasn't \\\"just getting old.\\\"\\n\\nBut the search that changed everything was one I almost didn't click. \\\"Why do people on Metformin still see their A1C climb.\\\"\\n\\nThat's where I fell down the rabbit hole. And I didn't come back up the same person.\\n\\nHere's what 9 years of doctor visits never explained to me, and what every supplement I'd tried completely ignored.\\n\\nMetformin doesn't address what's actually broken inside your body.\\n\\nStay with me here. This is the part that changed everything for me.\\n\\nYour body has a pathway that controls how fast the food you eat turns into sugar in your blood. When food comes in, the pathway breaks it down at a controlled pace. Sugar trickles into your blood slowly. Your insulin sees it coming and handles it calmly. Your numbers stay steady.\\n\\nThat's how it worked when you were 30 and you could eat anything.\\n\\nBut that pathway is fragile. Stress wears it down. Processed food wears it down. Years of carrying extra weight wear it down. And one day it stops being able to do its job.\\n\\nFood doesn't trickle into your blood anymore. It floods in. The carbs in your meal that used to take 90 minutes to enter your blood now hit in 15. The flood arrives faster than your insulin can handle. So you spike. Your pancreas panics and dumps a wave of insulin to catch up. That wave is too much. So an hour later you crash.\\n\\nThat flood is the problem. Not the food. The broken pathway.\\n\\nNow here's what Metformin actually does. It works on your liver. It tells your liver to stop releasing stored sugar into your blood overnight. That's helpful for your fasting glucose number. The morning reading on the meter comes down. Your A1C looks better on paper.\\n\\nBut it doesn't touch the pathway that's letting food flood your blood every time you eat. It doesn't slow the breakdown. It doesn't restore the system that controls the speed. The flood still happens every meal. The spike still happens. The crash still happens.\\n\\nYour doctor was watching the morning number. Nobody was watching the flood.\\n\\nAnd here's where I saw my own hands in the research.\\n\\nMetformin depletes vitamin B12. Long term use, year after year, slowly drains your B12 levels. And your nerves need B12 to function. Your fingertips need it to feel. Your hands need it to grip, to hold tension, to braid hair the way they've been doing it for 37 years.\\n\\nI read that and I felt it in my fingers. I saw 9 years of mornings running hot water over fingertips that wouldn't wake up. I saw 37 years of muscle memory slowly losing the nutrient it needed to fire. The numbness wasn't aging. It wasn't \\\"just diabetes.\\\" It was my nerves running out of B12 because the drug was depleting it as part of how it works.\\n\\nThat's not a side effect. That's the drug doing what it does. And nobody told me.\\n\\nAbout 30% of long term Metformin users develop B12 deficiency. That's not a rare complication. That's nearly one in three. And doctors keep charting it as \\\"manageable\\\" while their patients can't thread needles and can't feel piano keys and can't part hair with their grandmother's comb.\\n\\nAnd here's what finally explained why everything else I tried also failed.\\n\\nAll those supplements I was taking? Cinnamon, apple cider vinegar, berberine, chromium, bitter melon, every single one of them was working downstream. They were trying to nudge insulin around the edges. Help my cells listen a little better to the wave of insulin my body dumps after a spike. None of them touched the pathway that's letting food flood my blood in the first place.\\n\\nI spent 2 years mopping the floor while the roof was leaking.\\n\\nThat's why I could drink cinnamon water at 5 AM every morning for a year and watch my A1C drop 2 points. That's why I could swallow apple cider vinegar before every meal until I dreaded eating and still see my post meal numbers spike. That's why I could fill my kitchen counter with bottles and spend $200 a month and still sit in my own braiding chair on a Saturday afternoon wondering if I had to close my shop.\\n\\nEverything I'd tried, the drug AND the natural alternatives, was aimed at the symptom. Nothing had addressed the broken pathway driving the symptom.\\n\\nSo I went back to the research. And I searched for the thing I should have been searching for from the beginning. \\\"What restores the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar.\\\"\\n\\nThat's when I found something I'd never heard of. And by the end of that night, I understood why nothing else had worked.\\n\\nThere's a fruit that's been used in Central Asia for over 5,000 years.\\n\\nThe healers who used it back then didn't have a name for blood sugar. They didn't know what insulin was. They didn't have a way to measure what was happening inside the body. But they knew what they saw. People who ate this fruit daily had steadier energy. Calmer digestion. They didn't crash after meals. They didn't fall into the slow decline everyone else seemed to. They lived longer. They aged better.\\n\\nThey called it the fruit of life.\\n\\nToday we call it pomegranate.\\n\\nI'd heard of pomegranate. You probably have too. That bothered me at first. If this worked, wouldn't I have seen it at the health food store next to the berberine that didn't work and the bitter melon that didn't move my numbers?\\n\\nBut you can't patent a fruit. And there's no billion dollar industry built around something that grows on a tree.\\n\\nThen I looked at the research. And I stopped caring that I'd heard of it. Because modern science now knows exactly why it works.\\n\\nPomegranate contains specific compounds called punicalagins. One of the most powerful compounds your body uses to slow the breakdown of food into sugar back down to its natural pace.\\n\\nHere's what punicalagins do.\\n\\nThey restore the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar in your blood. Not in your liver like Metformin. Not in your gut like cinnamon. Not insulin sensitization like berberine. The actual pathway that controls the speed of the breakdown in the first place.\\n\\nWhen the punicalagins are back in your system, the pathway has what it needs to do its job. Food enters your blood gently again. The way it's supposed to. The flood stops. Your insulin sees the trickle coming. It handles it calmly. The spikes ease. The crashes ease. The cravings ease.\\n\\nThe floodgate holds.\\n\\nThen I found the clinical analysis that made me sit back in my chair.\\n\\nA meta analysis of 34 clinical trials on standardized pomegranate extract. Across more than 1,500 people. Measurable improvements in fasting glucose. Insulin levels. Insulin resistance. Without depleting B12. Without numbness. Without the merry go round of side effects.\\n\\nA fruit. Backed by 34 clinical trials. And nobody in my doctor's office had ever mentioned it because you can't patent something that grows on a tree.\\n\\nI'll be honest. I almost didn't try it.\\n\\nI'd spent 2 years trying things that didn't work. I'd been burned by berberine that wrecked my stomach. By apple cider vinegar that ruined my appetite. By cinnamon that did almost nothing. Bottles on my counter and nothing to show for it except lighter pockets and the same numb hands.\\n\\nBut this was different. I could see it was different. Not because the marketing was better but because the mechanism was different. Everything else I'd tried was working downstream, on the symptoms, on the surface. This was working upstream, restoring the pathway that controlled how my body handled food in the first place. And it wasn't depleting B12. It wasn't blocking anything my body needed. It was giving my body back the raw material it had been missing.\\n\\nI found a company called Alevia that makes a standardized pomegranate extract. Not cheap fruit powder, not heat processed scraps with no consistency. A clinical strength extract, verified to contain the concentration of punicalagins that actually produces results. Organic. Third party tested for purity and potency. Made in the USA.\\n\\nI talked to my doctor about working with him to monitor my numbers monthly while I added something to my routine. He wasn't thrilled. But I told him my hands couldn't wait for him to be thrilled. We agreed to monitor my bloodwork closely.\\n\\nI ordered Alevia's pomegranate that night.\\n\\nThe first thing that changed was the mornings.\\n\\nDay 4. I woke up, rolled out of bed, and felt the sheet under my fingertips. Not strongly. But I felt it. No hot water. No 10 minutes standing over the sink rubbing fingertips that wouldn't wake up. Just felt it. The way you do without thinking. The way I used to before the drug took it from me.\\n\\nI stood in my bathroom at 5 AM staring at my own hands. I'd forgotten what waking up with feeling in my fingers felt like.\\n\\nBy week 2, my fasting glucose was running consistently lower. Not dramatically. But the kind of consistent drop that builds week after week. The kind of number that doesn't lie. And the deep numbness that hit every afternoon and had me dipping my hands in warm water between clients was fading. Not gone. Fading. Like someone slowly turning down a sound I'd gotten so used to I'd forgotten it was there.\\n\\nWeek 3, a client closed her eyes in my chair. First time in months someone had done that. She wasn't watching my hands. She just leaned back and closed her eyes the way you do when you trust the hands holding your hair. I almost had to stop braiding because the lump in my throat hit me out of nowhere.\\n\\nWeek 4, the parts were sharp again. The tension was even. The rhythm was back. I wasn't going slower to hide the shake because there was less shake to hide. The flow came back. That thing, the instinct, the thing you can't fake and 37 years of muscle memory either gives you or it doesn't, it was coming back.\\n\\nWeek 6, a new client sat down and asked if I did knotless box braids. I said not right now. She said, \\\"That's too bad. I heard you were the best.\\\" I looked at my hands. They were steady. Not still. I'm 61, there's a difference between youth and recovery. But steady.\\n\\nI thought about it for the rest of the day.\\n\\nLast Saturday.\\n\\nOne of my regulars, a woman I've been braiding for 19 years, sat down in my chair. Normal appointment. Normal day. She'd asked for twists.\\n\\nAnd then I stopped.\\n\\nI walked over to the wall. Took down the wooden parting comb. My grandmother's comb. The one that had been gathering dust for over a year. I ran my thumb across the wood. It was dry. Needed oil. I didn't care.\\n\\nI picked up the comb. Made the first part. Slow. Deliberate. The sound of the comb dividing the section so clean you could hear it. The other braiders looked up. The women waiting looked up. Nobody said a word.\\n\\nI turned to my client.\\n\\n\\\"Sit still for me, baby.\\\"\\n\\nI started sectioning for knotless. Pulling the smallest amount of her own hair at the base. Feeling the tension. Feeding in the extension. The way my grandmother taught me. The way I'd done it 10,000 times before the drug took it away.\\n\\nMy hands weren't shaking.\\n\\nWhen I finished the first braid I held it up where she could see it in the mirror. She looked at it. Looked at me. She'd been watching my hands decline for 2 years. She knew what this meant.\\n\\n\\\"Welcome back, Joanne.\\\"\\n\\nI didn't trust myself to speak. I just hung the comb back on the wall where it belongs and kept braiding.\\n\\nWhen she went to pay, I shook my head.\\n\\n\\\"This one's on me, sister.\\\"\\n\\nShe understood.\\n\\nI ran my bloodwork at 3 months.\\n\\nMy A1C: 5.9. On Metformin alone, it had been 6.2. With the pomegranate added in, my A1C was lower.\\n\\nFasting glucose: 102. On Metformin it had been 108. Lower.\\n\\nPost meal numbers: holding under 140 even after real meals. Bread, rice, the foods I used to be terrified of. The supplements I'd taken for 7 months never got my post meal numbers anywhere close to that.\\n\\nMy doctor studied the labs for a long time.\\n\\n\\\"Your numbers are the best I've seen on you in 9 years,\\\" he said. \\\"What changed?\\\"\\n\\nI told him. Pomegranate. The punicalagins. The 34 trial meta analysis. The 5,000 years of use. He listened. When I finished he said, \\\"I'm going to be honest with you. I don't know enough about this yet. But your numbers don't lie. And you look like a different woman than the one who was sitting in that chair 6 months ago.\\\"\\n\\nHe was right. I was a different woman. Because for 9 years a drug had been slowly erasing me, and in 3 months, I'd come back.\\n\\nI'm writing this for every woman sitting in a chair somewhere, a braiding chair, a sewing machine, a piano bench, a kitchen counter, whose hands aren't what they were.\\n\\n37 years. I wasn't ready to stop.\\n\\nIf you're on Metformin and your fingertips are tingling, your hands are numb in the mornings, your grip is weaker than it should be, your forearms ache in a way rest doesn't fix.\\n\\nIf you've been trying to manage your blood sugar naturally, the cinnamon, the apple cider vinegar, the berberine, the supplements that cost you $200 a month, and your numbers won't budge.\\n\\nIf your doctor keeps watching a number on a chart while your body falls apart.\\n\\nIf you've been told \\\"the benefits outweigh the risks\\\" by someone who doesn't have to live with the risks.\\n\\nMaybe it's not just aging. Maybe it's the drug. And maybe everything you've tried is working downstream while the problem is upstream. In the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar in your blood. Where nothing you've taken can reach.\\n\\nI spent 9 years on a drug that lowered a number while the pathway underneath stayed broken. Then I spent 2 years and thousands of dollars on supplements that were never going to reach the real problem.\\n\\nAlevia's pomegranate addressed it. The punicalagins restore the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar in your blood, the one thing the Metformin never did and the supplements never could. No B12 depletion. No enzyme blocking. No nerve damage. No numb hands.\\n\\nAlevia uses a process that preserves the active compounds at clinical strength. Organic. Third party tested for purity and potency. Made in the USA. 2 capsules every morning. That's it.\\n\\nThat's the difference between a supplement that sits in your system doing nothing and one that actually addresses the pathway driving your numbers up.\\n\\nRight now, they're running a promotion with significant savings. But Alevia is a small company. They make everything in small batches to preserve the active compounds. When they sell out, it's usually weeks before the next batch is ready.\\n\\n90 days to try it. If nothing changes, full refund. No questions asked.\\n\\nBut if it works?\\n\\nIf you wake up on day 4 and feel the sheet under your fingertips without running hot water over your hands for 10 minutes?\\n\\nIf by week 2 the numbness in your forearms starts fading and you stop dipping your hands in warm water between clients?\\n\\nIf a month from now your clients are closing their eyes in your chair again because they trust the hands holding their hair?\\n\\nIf 3 months from now you're sitting in your doctor's office watching him stare at numbers he can't explain, and he says \\\"whatever you're doing, keep doing it\\\"?\\n\\nIf your A1C comes down lower than it ever was on the drug alone?\\n\\nIf your grandmother's comb comes off the wall and goes back where it belongs?\\n\\nYou'll wish you started 9 years ago.\\n\\nI know I do.\\n\\n👉https://po.naxast.com/\\n\\nYou don't have to keep lowering a number while the pathway underneath stays broken.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"SHOP NOW\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://po.naxast.com/\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"po.naxast.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Organic Pomegranate Extract Capsules | AT2A\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"The 5,000-year-old fruit of life in a pure, potent organic extract.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":186,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":2,\"duplicates\":36,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AE\",\"AG\",\"AI\",\"AS\",\"AT\",\"AU\",\"AW\",\"BB\",\"BE\",\"BH\",\"BM\",\"BN\",\"BO\",\"BS\",\"BZ\",\"CA\",\"CG\",\"CL\",\"CO\",\"CW\",\"CY\",\"DE\",\"DK\",\"DM\",\"DO\",\"EC\",\"ES\",\"FJ\",\"FM\",\"FO\",\"GB\",\"GD\",\"GF\",\"GG\",\"GL\",\"GP\",\"GR\",\"GU\",\"GY\",\"HU\",\"IE\",\"IL\",\"IT\",\"JE\",\"JM\",\"JO\",\"KN\",\"KW\",\"KY\",\"LA\",\"LC\",\"MF\",\"MH\",\"MP\",\"MQ\",\"MS\",\"MX\",\"NR\",\"NZ\",\"OM\",\"PE\",\"PF\",\"PL\",\"PR\",\"PS\",\"PT\",\"PW\",\"RE\",\"SA\",\"SB\",\"SE\",\"SH\",\"SR\",\"SV\",\"SX\",\"TC\",\"TO\",\"TR\",\"TT\",\"TV\",\"US\",\"VC\",\"VG\",\"VI\",\"VU\",\"WS\",\"YT\"],\"mainCountry\":\"IE\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":252,\"currentRank\":252,\"rankDelta\":null,\"improvementPct\":6.23},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_949424881457467\",\"collationId\":\"4308996615912234\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-06-17T07:32:45.168Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-06-16T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-07-14T18:59:37.853Z\",\"daysRunning\":164,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/67d9df62a510cb3ea0932e607a5c93574ac2433164894efac34eb91da49c2249.jpg\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"972054479333651\",\"name\":\"Dr. Cornelius Carter\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/972054479333651.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"972054479333651\",\"liveAdsCount\":1316,\"reach30d\":809346,\"totalReach\":2017222,\"countriesCount\":5,\"facebookLikes\":45312,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"An Amish barn burned down last week outside Lancaster, Pennsylvania.\\n\\nInsurance investigator found 400 pounds of bark stored inside. Wrapped in oilcloth. Stacked in cedar-lined crates. Temperature controlled by a ventilation system built into the walls.\\n\\nHe valued it at $63,000.\\n\\nI asked why it was worth more than the barn.\\n\\nHe said sourcing.\\n\\nThe barn took three weeks to build with local timber.\\n\\nThe bark took 18 months to arrive from a single district in southern Sri Lanka—hand-harvested, sun-dried, and shipped through a supply chain that hasn't changed in 200 years.\\n\\nYou can rebuild a barn in a month.\\n\\nYou can't replace a direct line to the last groves on earth that grow true cinnamon.\\n\\nI'm Dr. Cornelius Carter. I spent 34 years at Johns Hopkins studying ancient pharmacology—the medicines civilizations trusted before modern medicine industrialized them.\\n\\nI didn't understand what made that bark so valuable until I met the farmer who lost it.\\n\\nHis name was Eli. Seventy-nine years old. Built like a man who'd carried lumber his whole life.\\n\\nHe walked me to where the barn used to be. Ash and charred wood and the smell of something sweet still rising from the ground.\\n\\nHe picked up a piece of blackened bark. Held it to his nose. Then held it like he was holding gold.\\n\\nI said, \\\"Can't you just order more?\\\"\\n\\nHe looked at me like I'd asked a foolish question.\\n\\n\\\"This is Ceylon. Cinnamomum verum. From Matara district. My grandfather brought the first shipment in 1947. Same family harvests it. Same groves. Same species.\\\"\\n\\nHe paused.\\n\\n\\\"What you buy at the grocery store is not cinnamon. It's Cassia. Different plant. Different chemistry. Different results.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"I know,\\\" I said.\\n\\nHe looked surprised. \\\"How?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Because I've spent my entire career studying this bark.\\\"\\n\\nThat was the beginning of one of the most important conversations I've ever had—because Eli knew something about cinnamon that I'd only read about in 600-year-old medical texts.\\n\\nHe was living it.\\n\\nI need to explain why I was standing in the ashes of an Amish barn in Lancaster County in the first place.\\n\\nThree years ago, I stopped being a historian and became a patient.\\n\\nFasting glucose: 181. A1C: 7.1. Blood pressure: 156/98.\\n\\nMy physician said the words I'd heard colleagues dread for years.\\n\\n\\\"We need to start you on metformin. And we may need to add an antihypertensive. Your blood pressure is heading toward Stage 2. At this rate, you're looking at real cardiovascular risk.\\\"\\n\\nI nodded. Took the prescription. Filled it at the pharmacy downstairs.\\n\\nAnd left it in my desk drawer for three months.\\n\\nNot because I'm reckless. Not because I'm in denial.\\n\\nBecause I've spent 34 years studying what medicine looked like before we industrialized it—and I knew there were answers buried in history that modern endocrinology and cardiology had stopped looking for.\\n\\nI'd written two books on ancient trade routes and medicinal compounds. I'd translated Arabic medical texts from the 10th century. I'd traced the movement of healing plants across empires.\\n\\nAnd the substance I kept coming back to, the one that appeared in more ancient pharmacopeias than any other, was cinnamon.\\n\\nNot the powder sitting in your kitchen cabinet. The original bark that empires fought wars over.\\n\\nIn the 1500s, Portuguese soldiers burned entire villages in Sri Lanka to control the cinnamon trade. The Dutch East India Company made it a capital offense to sell a single stick outside their supply chain. Men were executed for bark.\\n\\nKings didn't sprinkle it on oatmeal. They locked it in vaults.\\n\\nI have a 16th-century Dutch trade manifest in my office. Cinnamon was listed above silver. Above silk. Above everything except gold.\\n\\nPhysicians used it for what Dioscorides called \\\"disorders of the blood and vital humors.\\\" Ibn Sina—Avicenna—described it as essential for \\\"conditions of excessive thirst, wasting, and the stiffening of channels through which blood flows.\\\"\\n\\nExcessive thirst. Wasting. Stiffening blood vessels. The classical descriptions of what we now call diabetes and hypertension—the combination that kills hearts.\\n\\nAnd then I found a 12th-century Arabic translation of a 6th-century Sinhala medical treatise. Physicians described a preparation of cinnamon bark, ground and dissolved in coconut oil, given to patients whose \\\"sweet urine attracted ants\\\" and whose pulse had become hard and rapid.\\n\\nThat's how ancient physicians diagnosed diabetes and hypertension. Ants attracted to sugar in the urine. A pulse that meant the heart was working too hard.\\n\\nThe treatment was Ceylon cinnamon. Dissolved in fat.\\n\\nFor 1,400 years, this was documented medicine.\\n\\nThen demand outgrew supply.\\n\\nCeylon cinnamon—Cinnamomum verum—only grows in Sri Lanka. Limited land. Slow cultivation. Expensive to harvest.\\n\\nTraders found a shortcut. A cousin species from Southeast Asia. Cassia. Cinnamomum cassia.\\n\\nLooked the same. Smelled close enough. Cost almost nothing to grow.\\n\\nThey called it cinnamon and shipped it by the ton.\\n\\nThe chemistry wasn't the same—not even close.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon cinnamon contains Methyl Hydroxychalcone Polymer—MHCP. It mimics insulin at the receptor level. Same lock, same key, same door opening. Your cells can't tell the difference between MHCP and insulin. Glucose enters without your pancreas working overtime.\\n\\nCassia contains almost none of it.\\n\\nWhat Cassia does contain is coumarin—up to 250 times more than Ceylon. Coumarin is hepatotoxic. It damages your liver with daily use.\\n\\nWe replaced a medicine with a toxin, called them the same thing, and filed cinnamon under \\\"spice.\\\"\\n\\nThat's what I was researching when a colleague told me about the barn fire in Lancaster. An Amish farmer with hundreds of pounds of genuine Ceylon bark. I drove out the next morning.\\n\\nAnd that's how I found myself standing in the ashes with Eli.\\n\\nAfter I told him about my research, he went quiet for a long time. Then he said something I'll never forget.\\n\\n\\\"English people take pills to force blood through damaged vessels.\\\"\\n\\nHe tapped the charred bark in his hand.\\n\\n\\\"This repairs the vessels the blood flows through.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Pills manage the pressure. This fixes why the pressure is high in the first place.\\\"\\n\\nI asked what he meant.\\n\\nHe said, \\\"When your blood sugar is high, the sugar damages the walls of your arteries. Makes them rough. Inflamed. Stiff. That's what raises the pressure. Not salt. Not stress. Sugar destroying your vessels from the inside.\\\"\\n\\nI stared at him. A man with an eighth-grade education had just summarized the pathophysiology of metabolic hypertension more clearly than most cardiologists I know.\\n\\n\\\"Lower the sugar,\\\" he said, \\\"and the vessels heal. The pressure drops on its own. Because you fixed the cause, not the symptom.\\\"\\n\\nI asked how he took it.\\n\\nHe said his grandfather taught his father. His father taught him.\\n\\nThey grind the Ceylon bark—never Cassia, never the impostor—and dissolve it in fat. Coconut oil. Every morning. Every member of the community. For three generations.\\n\\nI asked to see his medical records.\\n\\nHe showed me without hesitation.\\n\\nEli. Seventy-nine years old. Never been on blood pressure medication. Never been on metformin. Never been on a statin.\\n\\nBlood pressure: 118/74.\\n\\nFasting glucose: 91.\\n\\nHis doctor had been asking him for 40 years what he does differently.\\n\\nHe never tells him.\\n\\nI asked why.\\n\\nHe looked at me with something between sadness and amusement.\\n\\n\\\"Because doctors don't profit from people dissolving bark in oil every morning. They profit from pills that manage what the bark would fix.\\\"\\n\\nI didn't argue. I'd seen my own prescription sitting unused in my desk drawer.\\n\\nI asked him the only question that mattered.\\n\\n\\\"Where do you get your Ceylon?\\\"\\n\\nHe told me the same family in Matara district, Sri Lanka. Same groves his grandfather sourced from. True Cinnamomum verum, never substituted, never blended, never adulterated with Cassia.\\n\\nThe bark in his barn was irreplaceable not because cinnamon is rare—Cassia grows everywhere. It was irreplaceable because true Ceylon, from verified groves, with intact MHCP content, is one of the scarcest medicinal substances on earth.\\n\\nAnd the way he prepared it—dissolved in coconut oil—wasn't tradition for tradition's sake.\\n\\nIt was biochemistry.\\n\\nCinnamon's active compounds—cinnamaldehyde and MHCP—are fat-soluble. Without a lipid carrier, they pass through the gut largely unabsorbed. Dry powder in a capsule does almost nothing. The compound needs fat to cross the intestinal membrane and enter the bloodstream.\\n\\nThe Sinhala physicians understood bioavailability 1,400 years ago. Eli's grandfather understood it in 1947. The entire Amish community understood it every morning when they stirred bark into oil.\\n\\nI was the educated one, and I was the last to figure it out.\\n\\nI'd already tried. Before meeting Eli, I'd ordered Ceylon cinnamon capsules from Amazon. Dry powder. $16.99. Took two every morning for three weeks.\\n\\nFasting glucose dropped from 181 to 174. Blood pressure didn't move.\\n\\nSeven points on glucose. Zero on the pressure that was actually threatening my heart.\\n\\nAfter meeting Eli, I understood why. The ancient texts said fat. His tradition said fat. The biochemistry confirmed fat.\\n\\nSo I did what Eli did. What his grandfather did. What the 6th-century Sinhala physicians did.\\n\\nI bought MCT oil—medium-chain triglycerides derived from coconut oil. Opened the capsules, mixed the Ceylon powder into a tablespoon of MCT, and took it every morning.\\n\\nThe taste was terrible. Gritty. Oily.\\n\\nBut I was committed.\\n\\nDay three: my usual 2 PM crash—the one where I'd fall asleep grading papers—didn't happen. I was alert. Steady. Present.\\n\\nDay five: I slept through the night for the first time in two years. No 3 AM bathroom trips. No lying awake until 4:30. Just sleep.\\n\\nI tested my fasting glucose that morning.\\n\\n158.\\n\\nTwenty-three points lower than when I started.\\n\\nI tested again. Same number.\\n\\nI called my wife into the kitchen. \\\"Look at this.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"That's good?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"That's the lowest number I've seen in eighteen months.\\\"\\n\\nWeek two: 134.\\n\\nWeek three: 118.\\n\\nWeek four: 101.\\n\\nI checked it three times. I hadn't seen a fasting glucose below 110 in over two years.\\n\\nI checked my blood pressure that week. 148/92. Down from 156/98.\\n\\nThe crashes were gone. The constant thirst was gone. The brain fog that made me re-read the same paragraph six times—gone. And for the first time in eighteen months, my blood pressure was moving in the right direction.\\n\\nEli was right. Lower the sugar, and the vessels begin to heal. Fix the cause, and the symptom resolves on its own.\\n\\nI felt like myself again.\\n\\nThree months later, I walked into my doctor's office with a glucose log, blood pressure readings, and the translation of the Sinhala medical treatise.\\n\\nShe looked at my numbers for a long time.\\n\\n\\\"Your fasting glucose is averaging 96.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Yes.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Your A1C is 5.7. Down from 7.1.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Yes.\\\"\\n\\nShe pulled up my blood pressure data.\\n\\n\\\"Your blood pressure is averaging 128/82. It was 156/98 three months ago.\\\"\\n\\nI nodded.\\n\\n\\\"Blood pressure this high doesn't usually reverse without medication. Once it climbs like yours had, we typically manage it pharmacologically long-term. But you've brought it down naturally to normal range.\\\"\\n\\nShe looked at recent cardiac markers.\\n\\n\\\"Your arterial inflammation markers are down significantly. Endothelial function improved. The stress on your heart has decreased.\\\"\\n\\nShe looked up. \\\"What did you do?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"I didn't take the metformin.\\\"\\n\\nHer face tightened.\\n\\n\\\"I took Ceylon cinnamon—the real species, Cinnamomum verum—dissolved in MCT oil. The same preparation documented in medical texts from the 6th century. And the same preparation an Amish community in Lancaster County has been using for three generations.\\\"\\n\\nI handed her a folder with eight published papers, my translation of the Sinhala treatise, and Eli's medical records with his permission.\\n\\nShe read for ten minutes. Closed the folder.\\n\\n\\\"Your triglycerides dropped. Inflammatory markers down. Vascular function improved. Blood pressure normalized naturally.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"I know.\\\"\\n\\nShe sat back. \\\"Keep doing whatever you're doing.\\\"\\n\\nThat was nine months ago.\\n\\nMy fasting glucose is consistently between 92 and 100. My A1C is 5.5. My blood pressure is stable at 128/82. My doctor says my metabolic markers and cardiovascular health look better than they have in a decade.\\n\\nI sleep through every single night. The crashes are gone. The fog is gone. The fear that my arteries were being destroyed—that I was heading toward a heart attack—that's gone too.\\n\\nI found what the ancient physicians documented. What the empires fought wars over. What an Amish farmer in Lancaster kept alive for three generations while the rest of us forgot.\\n\\nAnd then I found something better than mixing powder into oil every morning.\\n\\nA company called Metabolae had already figured it out.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon cinnamon. Cinnamomum verum, sourced from Sri Lanka—DNA verified, not just labeled. The same species Eli's family has been sourcing from Matara district for 77 years. The same species the Sinhala physicians prescribed 1,400 years ago.\\n\\n7,200mg equivalent per softgel. A 12:1 concentrated extract. The clinical dose that corresponds to the ancient preparations and the Amish tradition—not a token 500mg amount.\\n\\nSuspended in MCT oil. The fat-soluble delivery that the Sinhala physicians understood, that Eli's grandfather understood, and that I had to learn the hard way when dry capsules barely moved my numbers.\\n\\nThird-party tested. Every batch verified for purity and coumarin levels—because Cassia, the impostor that replaced Ceylon, contains up to 250 times more coumarin and can damage your liver with daily use.\\n\\nOne softgel. Once a day. No gritty oil mixture. No guessing. No grinding bark at 6 AM.\\n\\nThe same compound. The same mechanism. The same medicine that an Amish farmer valued at more than his barn—but optimized for modern delivery.\\n\\nI drove back to see Eli six weeks after our first meeting.\\n\\nTold him about Metabolae. Showed him the softgels.\\n\\nHe turned one over in his hand. Read the label. \\\"Cinnamomum verum. Sri Lanka. MCT oil.\\\"\\n\\nHe nodded slowly. \\\"Same thing. Better container.\\\"\\n\\nThen he said, \\\"How many people know about this?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Not enough.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"That's the problem with this world. You have the medicine. You have the science. You've had the answer for a thousand years. And people are still taking pills that force blood through broken pipes instead of fixing the pipes.\\\"\\n\\nI've told five colleagues about this since meeting Eli. Three were pre-diabetic. Two were fully type 2 with elevated blood pressure.\\n\\nAll five came back with the same story. Better sleep within the first week. Stable energy. Lower fasting glucose within a month. Blood pressure dropping by week three or four.\\n\\nOne colleague—a fellow historian, 62 years old, fasting glucose of 176 and blood pressure of 151/94—dropped his glucose to 104 in six weeks and his blood pressure to 130/80. His cardiologist said his cardiovascular risk profile had shifted dramatically.\\n\\nHe called me and said, \\\"Why doesn't anyone know about this?\\\"\\n\\nI told him what Eli told me.\\n\\nBecause we forgot. Because we substituted. Because we filed one of the most powerful medicines in human history under \\\"spice\\\" and stopped paying attention. Because we replaced Ceylon with Cassia and called them the same thing. Because we didn't understand that the same compound that regulates blood sugar also heals damaged arteries and normalizes blood pressure—not by forcing blood through broken vessels, but by repairing the vessels themselves.\\n\\nHere's what I want you to understand.\\n\\nMHCP mimics insulin at the receptor level. Cinnamaldehyde activates AMPK—the same pathway exercise uses to move glucose into cells. Lower blood sugar means less glucose poisoning your arteries. Less inflammation means your vessels can relax. Blood pressure drops naturally because your arteries are no longer under constant inflammatory attack from elevated glucose.\\n\\nYou fix the cause. The symptoms resolve.\\n\\nEli knew it. His grandfather knew it. The Sinhala physicians knew it 1,400 years ago.\\n\\nBut you have to get it right.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon—not Cassia. Clinical dose—not 500mg token amounts. Fat-soluble delivery—not dry powder that passes through you. Third-party tested—not mystery capsules from unknown sources.\\n\\nThat's Metabolae.\\n\\nYou can rebuild a barn in a month. You can't rebuild your arteries without the right compound, at the right dose, in the right delivery system.\\n\\nEli lost $63,000 worth of bark in that fire. He started sourcing a new supply the next day.\\n\\nI asked him if he was upset about the loss.\\n\\nHe said, \\\"I'm 79. My blood pressure is 118 over 74. I've never taken a pill in my life. My father lived to 84. My grandfather to 88. I'll spend whatever it costs to keep going.\\\"\\n\\nThen he looked at me.\\n\\n\\\"The question isn't whether the bark is worth it. The question is what your arteries are worth to you.\\\"\\n\\nTap below. 90 days risk-free.\\n\\nSee what happens when you stop forcing blood through damaged pipes and start repairing the pipes themselves.\\n\\n~ Dr. Cornelius Carter, PhD, Medical History, Johns Hopkins University, 34 years\\n\\nP.S. A few things I wish I'd known before I started:\\n\\n1. Don't waste money on cheap cinnamon capsules. I tried them first. Seven points in three weeks. The powder doesn't absorb without fat. The ancient physicians documented this 1,400 years ago. An Amish community has been doing it for three generations. I had to learn it the hard way.\\n\\n2. Most \\\"cinnamon\\\" supplements are Cassia, not Ceylon. Different species. Up to 250 times more coumarin. Linked to liver damage with daily use. If the label doesn't specify Ceylon (Cinnamomum verum), it's almost certainly Cassia.\\n\\n3. Timeline: Sleep improves first—usually within a week. Energy stabilizes around week two. Blood sugar drops show up between weeks two and four. Blood pressure begins normalizing around weeks three and four. Full benefits take 8-12 weeks.\\n\\n4. Metabolae offers a 90-day money-back guarantee. If your numbers don't improve, you pay nothing.\\n\\n5. They sell out regularly. Small company, growing fast. If it's in stock, I'd order now.\\n\\nIf you're dealing with:\\n\\nHigh fasting glucose that won't come down\\n\\nBlood pressure that keeps climbing no matter what you do\\n\\nAfternoon crashes that destroy your focus\\n\\nBrain fog that makes simple tasks exhausting\\n\\nThe constant fear that a heart attack is coming\\n\\nThe feeling that pills are managing your symptoms but nothing is actually getting better\\n\\nThe medicine that empires went to war over—and that an Amish farmer valued more than his barn—still exists. The compound your cells recognize is still there. The preparation the ancient physicians documented still works.\\n\\nWe just forgot. Then we faked it. Then we moved on.\\n\\nMetabolae is the original.\\n\\nYou can rebuild a barn in a month. You can't rebuild your arteries without the right medicine.\\n\\nTap below. Try it for 90 days.\\n\\nSee what happens when you stop settling for sawdust and start taking the bark that built empires.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/lp-blood-pressure-1\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Metabolae\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":11174,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":101,\"duplicates\":35,\"reachDelta1d\":107,\"reachDelta7d\":406,\"reachDelta30d\":1593},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AU\",\"CA\",\"GB\",\"NZ\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":155,\"currentRank\":155,\"rankDelta\":-24,\"improvementPct\":11.78},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1910785363224885\",\"collationId\":\"1588273626066424\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-07-28T19:24:00.222Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-07-28T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-08-01T20:37:37.069Z\",\"daysRunning\":122,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/81287a8b9ab4af1f2f254a0010b4261874769c4b8d3fa5da5a0032cacf61afb5.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"958558520684789\",\"name\":\"John Tucker\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/958558520684789.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"958558520684789\",\"liveAdsCount\":1333,\"reach30d\":492752,\"totalReach\":1373110,\"countriesCount\":5,\"facebookLikes\":4388,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I Took Ceylon Cinnamon Every Single Morning For 11 Weeks. My Fasting Glucose Didn't Drop A Single Point. The Foam In My Toilet Never Cleared. Then A Lab Technician Showed Me Why.\\n\\nMy name is John Tucker. I'm 62 years old.\\n\\nI believed in Ceylon cinnamon the way some people believe in prayer.\\n\\nNot casually. Not as a \\\"maybe this will help\\\" afterthought.\\n\\nI believed because I'd read the studies. Real ones. Published in the Journal of Medicinal Food and Diabetes Care. Researchers giving subjects Ceylon cinnamon extract and watching fasting glucose drop measurably within weeks.\\n\\nI'd read the GLUT4 research. The insulin sensitivity data from peer-reviewed journals. I understood the pathway. Ceylon cinnamon activates the transporters on cell surfaces that pull glucose out of the bloodstream. Glucose clears. Blood sugar drops. The kidneys stop filtering sugar-saturated blood hour after hour.\\n\\nMy fasting glucose at my last panel: 144.\\n\\nMy creatinine had gone from 0.9 to 1.4 in two years. My GFR was dropping every quarter. My ankles swelled by evening. I was up two, three times a night. And one morning I flushed the toilet and the foam sat there. Thick. Frothy. Just sitting on top of the water.\\n\\nMy father spent the last four years of his life in a dialysis chair three days a week.\\n\\nThe science was clear. Ceylon cinnamon was my answer. I was sure of it.\\n\\nMy wife Linda found a health blog with dosing recommendations. Two heaping teaspoons of Ceylon cinnamon powder, mixed into a morning smoothie. We ordered a two-pound bag of organic Ceylon powder from Amazon. Thirty-eight dollars.\\n\\nEvery single morning. Seven days a week. I measured out two teaspoons, dumped them into my blender with a banana and almond milk, and hit the button.\\n\\nThe powder never fully dissolved. No matter how long I blended. There were always clumps. Gritty, chalky clumps that sat at the bottom of the glass and coated the back of my throat like wet sand.\\n\\nThe taste was sweet at first. Then medicinal. Then just overwhelming. By week three, the smell of cinnamon in the morning made my stomach turn before I even poured the glass.\\n\\nEvery. Single. Morning.\\n\\nLinda started making the smoothie some mornings when I left early for work. She'd text me a photo of the glass waiting in the fridge. A little heart emoji.\\n\\nWe were in this together.\\n\\nI bought a home glucose meter. Contour Next One. The same one my endocrinologist recommended.\\n\\nWeek 1: Fasting glucose 144. Foam still there every morning. No change.\\n\\n\\\"Give it time,\\\" Linda said. \\\"The studies said it takes a few weeks.\\\"\\n\\nWeek 2: 143. Within the margin of error. Foam unchanged.\\n\\nWeek 3: 144. Back up. Foam sitting thick on top of the water after every flush.\\n\\nWeek 4: 145. Higher.\\n\\nI stared at the meter. Checked the strip. Pricked my finger again.\\n\\n145.\\n\\nI sat at the kitchen table. The empty smoothie glass still had a ring of brown residue at the bottom. Cinnamon powder caked on the sides. I pushed it away.\\n\\n\\\"It takes longer,\\\" Linda said from the doorway. She didn't sound convinced.\\n\\nWeek 6: 143.\\n\\nWeek 8: 144.\\n\\nWeek 11: 144.\\n\\nEleven weeks. Seventy-seven consecutive mornings. Over thirty dollars a week between the powder, the bananas, and the almond milk. A blender crusted with brown cinnamon stains no amount of scrubbing could remove.\\n\\n144.\\n\\nThe exact same number I'd started with. And the foam was still there every single morning.\\n\\nMy next panel was in ten weeks. My doctor had been clear at the last visit: \\\"If your next panel shows the same trend, we're adding another medication. And honestly, John, if the GFR keeps dropping, we need to start having a different conversation.\\\"\\n\\nI knew what conversation she meant.\\n\\nThat night I didn't sleep. I lay in the dark and thought about my father. The three-hour sessions. The machine that cleaned his blood because his kidneys couldn't do it anymore. The look on his face when he told me he couldn't travel anymore because he couldn't miss a session. The way he stopped being himself at the end.\\n\\nLinda rolled over. \\\"You're awake again.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"I don't understand. The studies, the research is real. It works. Why doesn't it work for me?\\\"\\n\\nHer hand found mine in the dark. She squeezed it. Didn't answer. Because she didn't know.\\n\\nNeither did I.\\n\\nThe next morning was a Saturday. I drove to the farmers market. Not to buy cinnamon. I couldn't even think about cinnamon without my stomach churning. I went for Linda. She wanted heirloom tomatoes.\\n\\nI was standing near a spice vendor's booth when I overheard two women talking.\\n\\n\\\"Yeah, I actually work at a supplement testing lab. We run species verification and potency assays on commercial cinnamon products. You'd be amazed at what we find.\\\"\\n\\nMy ears locked in.\\n\\nThe woman was in her forties. Glasses. Matter-of-fact voice.\\n\\n\\\"Like what?\\\" the other woman asked.\\n\\n\\\"Most products labeled Ceylon cinnamon? They're not Ceylon at all. They're Cassia. Completely different species. Different chemical profile. Almost none of the active compounds that the clinical research is based on. And Cassia contains up to 250 times more coumarin than real Ceylon. That's a compound that stresses your liver at daily supplement doses.\\\"\\n\\nMy stomach dropped.\\n\\nI stepped closer. \\\"Excuse me. Did you say most Ceylon cinnamon products are actually Cassia?\\\"\\n\\nThe woman with glasses turned. \\\"Oh, yes. We test for species authenticity in our lab. Over 70 percent of products labeled Ceylon are partially or fully Cassia. The suppliers substitute because Cassia costs a fraction of the price. Most companies never verify because species testing costs money.\\\"\\n\\nMy jaw tightened. \\\"What about the powder? If I buy organic Ceylon cinnamon powder and mix it myself?\\\"\\n\\nShe tilted her head. \\\"Even if the species is right, you've got two more problems. First, dose. The clinical research showing measurable results uses doses equivalent to 6,000 to 7,200 milligrams of raw Ceylon bark daily. Most people take a teaspoon or two, maybe 2,500 milligrams. That's not a small gap. That's the difference between a therapeutic dose and a decorative one.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"And the second problem?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Delivery. Ceylon's active compounds are fat-soluble. They need a lipid carrier to cross the intestinal wall and enter the bloodstream. A dry powder, whether it's in a capsule or mixed into a smoothie, has no fat carrier. The compounds reach your gut, find nothing to carry them across, and pass through without absorbing. You could take the right species at the right dose in powder form and most of it would leave your body unused.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"So when the studies show Ceylon cinnamon lowering blood sugar and improving other numbers...\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Those studies use standardized extracts. Controlled species. Verified dose. Proper delivery vehicle. Every subject gets the exact same thing. That's why the studies work. It's not the cinnamon that doesn't work. It's the form. Powder can't guarantee species, dose, or absorption.\\\"\\n\\nI stood there. The farmers market noise, the chatter, the kids running between booths, it all sounded like it was underwater.\\n\\nEleven weeks.\\n\\nSeventy-seven mornings.\\n\\nAnd some of those mornings, I might have been drinking the wrong species at the wrong dose that couldn't absorb anyway.\\n\\n\\\"Is there,\\\" I cleared my throat, \\\"is there any way to actually get all three right?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"A softgel with a built-in fat carrier. That solves the absorption problem. If the species is verified Sri Lankan Ceylon, that solves the species problem. If it's concentrated to the clinical dose, that solves the dose problem. But all three have to be right at once.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Does something like that exist?\\\"\\n\\nShe pulled out her phone. \\\"We actually tested one recently that surprised me. Metabolae. Softgels with a built-in fat carrier. Verified Sri Lankan Ceylon. Concentrated to a 7,200 milligram raw bark equivalent per serving. And they publish their Certificate of Analysis. Every batch. Full transparency.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"You tested it in your lab?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"We did. Species matched. Dose matched. Active compound concentration matched the label. Which almost never happens in this category. Most products we test come in at a fraction of what they claim, when they're even the right species at all.\\\"\\n\\nI ordered it on my phone before I left the farmers market. Didn't even walk back to the tomato stand. Texted Linda: Found something. Coming home.\\n\\nThe bag arrived Monday.\\n\\nI took the first softgel that evening with dinner. No prep. No blender. No cleanup. No cinnamon-crusted kitchen.\\n\\nI sat on the back porch. Skeptical. Eleven weeks of powder had beaten the hope out of me.\\n\\nBy the third morning, something shifted.\\n\\nThe low-grade fatigue that had been dragging me down every afternoon was lighter. The grogginess that normally lasted until 10 AM was gone before 8. I stood in the bathroom, flushed the toilet, and looked.\\n\\nThe foam was thinner.\\n\\nNot gone. But thinner. For the first time in months, the surface of the water cleared in under a minute.\\n\\nI went to the kitchen. Pulled out the Contour Next.\\n\\n139.\\n\\nI blinked. Checked the strip. Pricked my finger again.\\n\\n139.\\n\\nDown from 144. In three days.\\n\\nI sat there staring at the number.\\n\\nPlease let this be real.\\n\\nI didn't tell Linda. Not yet. Didn't want to get her hopes up. Not again.\\n\\nI took it every morning. Same time. Same softgel with breakfast. Thirty seconds. No prep. No mess.\\n\\nDay 7: Fasting glucose 134. Foam barely visible. Ankle swelling easing by evening.\\n\\nLinda noticed something before the meter confirmed it. I was awake at 6 AM on a Sunday. Making coffee. Humming. I hadn't hummed in months.\\n\\n\\\"You seem different,\\\" she said carefully.\\n\\n\\\"I feel different.\\\"\\n\\nDay 10: 129. Foam gone. The water was clean.\\n\\nDay 14: 124.\\n\\nI showed Linda the log I'd been keeping. Two columns. Date. Reading. And a third column. Foam. Yes or no.\\n\\nHer eyes tracked down the numbers. The slow, steady descent. The column of yeses turning to noes.\\n\\nHer hand came up to her mouth. \\\"John...\\\"\\n\\n\\\"I know.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"But the powder. We did everything right.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"It wasn't the cinnamon that was wrong. It was everything else. Wrong species half the time. Wrong dose all the time. And none of it could absorb without a fat carrier. The studies work because they use the right species at the right dose in a form the body can actually use. The powder couldn't give us any of that.\\\"\\n\\nDay 21: 116. No foam. Ankle swelling gone. Sleeping through the night without a bathroom trip.\\n\\nI called my brother Tom that weekend. Tom's blood sugar had been climbing too. Runs in the family.\\n\\n\\\"116,\\\" I told him. \\\"And the foam is gone.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"What foam?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Protein leaking into my urine. The kidney damage. The foam cleared in ten days.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"How?\\\"\\n\\nI explained everything. The wrong species. The wrong dose. The absorption problem. The softgel that solved all three.\\n\\n\\\"I'm sending you a bag,\\\" I said. \\\"Stop messing around with the powder. It's not the same thing.\\\"\\n\\nDay 28: 110.\\n\\nWeek eight was my follow-up appointment.\\n\\nThe nurse took blood. I sat in the room for twenty minutes waiting.\\n\\nMy doctor walked in. Pulled up my chart. Looked at the screen. Looked at me.\\n\\n\\\"John. Your creatinine came back at 1.1. It was 1.4 at your last panel.\\\"\\n\\nSilence.\\n\\n\\\"Your GFR is at 76. It was 65.\\\"\\n\\nShe scrolled.\\n\\n\\\"Protein spillage is down significantly. And your fasting glucose is lower than it's been since your first panel with us.\\\"\\n\\nShe looked at me. \\\"What changed?\\\"\\n\\nI told her. The wrong species. The wrong dose. The absorption failure. The softgel that got all three right. The published lab results for every batch.\\n\\nShe listened. Took notes. Nodded slowly.\\n\\n\\\"I'll be honest. The research on Ceylon cinnamon and insulin sensitivity is solid. If you're getting verified species at the clinical dose in a form that absorbs, the mechanism is sound.\\\" She closed her laptop. \\\"No additional medication. Keep doing exactly what you're doing. I want to see you again in twelve weeks.\\\"\\n\\nNo additional medication. No dialysis conversation.\\n\\nI sat in my car. Hands on the steering wheel. Chest tight. But not from blood pressure.\\n\\nI called Linda.\\n\\n\\\"Creatinine 1.1. GFR 76. No new medication. No dialysis conversation.\\\"\\n\\nI heard her gasp. Then silence. Then a sound that might have been a laugh or might have been a sob.\\n\\n\\\"You did it,\\\" she whispered.\\n\\n\\\"We did it. We just needed the right form.\\\"\\n\\nThat was three months ago.\\n\\nMy fasting glucose this morning: 106. No foam. No ankle swelling. No cinnamon-crusted blender. No gritty smoothies at 6 AM.\\n\\nOne softgel. Thirty seconds. Done.\\n\\nLast weekend, Linda and I spent the entire afternoon in the backyard building a raised garden bed for the grandkids. Four hours of digging, leveling, hauling soil. I didn't sit down once.\\n\\nLinda took a photo of me with my hands in the dirt, the grandkids planting tomatoes beside me.\\n\\nShe texted it to our daughter with one line: \\\"This is 62.\\\"\\n\\nMy daughter texted back: \\\"What happened to the dad who couldn't make it through dinner without falling asleep?\\\"\\n\\nI laughed. Because I remembered that guy. That was five months ago. That guy was drinking cinnamon smoothies and watching the foam in his toilet every morning wondering when the dialysis conversation would start.\\n\\nThat guy didn't know the problem was never the cinnamon.\\n\\nIt was the form.\\n\\nIf you're reading this, you see yourself in my story.\\n\\nYou believe in the science. You've done the research. You know Ceylon cinnamon works.\\n\\nBut the powder isn't working for you. Or the capsules. Or whatever form you've been taking from Amazon.\\n\\nYour numbers aren't moving. The foam is still there. And your next appointment is getting closer.\\n\\nHere's what I want you to know:\\n\\nI spent eleven weeks mixing cinnamon powder into smoothies every single morning. Thirty dollars a week. Brown-stained mugs. A kitchen that smelled like a spice factory. And my fasting glucose didn't move. The foam never cleared.\\n\\nI thought cinnamon didn't work for me. I thought my genetics were too strong. I thought I was destined for the same chair my father sat in three days a week.\\n\\nI was wrong.\\n\\nThe cinnamon worked. The delivery method didn't. The powder gave me the wrong species, the wrong dose, and no way to absorb what little was there. I was doing the right thing in the wrong form.\\n\\nIf your numbers aren't moving, don't give up on Ceylon cinnamon. Give up on powder.\\n\\nTry a standardized softgel. Track your numbers. Give it an honest trial.\\n\\nYour kitchen will thank you. Your kidneys will too.\\n\\nI tried cinnamon powder for 11 weeks with zero results. Here's why Metabolae worked when powder didn't:\\n\\n1. Verified Sri Lankan Ceylon (Not Cassia Mislabeled As Ceylon)\\n\\nMy powder could have been Cassia. I had no way to know. No way to verify. And Cassia contains almost none of the active compounds that support GLUT4 activation. Plus up to 250 times more coumarin, a compound that stresses the liver at daily supplement doses.\\n\\nMetabolae uses verified Sri Lankan Ceylon. Every batch species-tested. Every bag confirmed. I stopped guessing what species I was swallowing and started knowing.\\n\\n2. Clinical Dose, 7,200mg Raw Bark Equivalent (Not A Decorative Amount)\\n\\nMy two teaspoons of powder delivered maybe 2,500 milligrams. The clinical research showing measurable results uses 6,000 to 7,200 milligrams of raw bark equivalent. That's not a small gap. That's the difference between a therapeutic dose and a decorative amount.\\n\\nMetabolae is standardized to the full clinical concentration. Every softgel. Every batch. No guessing.\\n\\n3. Softgel With Built-In Fat Carrier (Not Dry Powder That Can't Absorb)\\n\\nCeylon's active compounds are fat-soluble. They need a lipid carrier to cross the intestinal wall. Dry powder has no fat. The compounds reach the gut, find no carrier, and pass through unused.\\n\\nMetabolae delivers the compounds in a softgel with a built-in fat carrier. Absorption, not transit. I stopped sending compounds through my body unused and started actually absorbing them.\\n\\n4. Third-Party Tested With Published Results\\n\\nCinnamon powder has no Certificate of Analysis. No species verification. No potency testing. No way to know what's actually in the bag.\\n\\nMetabolae publishes everything. Independent lab testing. Species verification. Active compound concentration. Heavy metal screening. Every batch. Full transparency.\\n\\nI could see exactly what I was getting. No guessing. No hoping. Just data.\\n\\nTry Metabolae for up to 90 days. Track your fasting glucose weekly. Watch the foam. Note the energy.\\n\\nIf you're not satisfied for ANY reason. If your numbers don't improve. If the foam doesn't clear. If you don't feel the energy lift. Full refund.\\n\\nNo questions asked. You risk nothing.\\n\\nYou're at a fork in the road right now.\\n\\nOne path: Keep taking powder. Keep guessing the species. Keep hoping the dose is enough. Keep swallowing compounds that can't absorb. Keep watching your numbers stay flat and the foam persist while your next appointment gets closer. Keep walking toward another medication because the form of your solution was wrong, not the science.\\n\\nAnother path: Do what I did. Switch to a verified Sri Lankan Ceylon softgel with the clinical dose and a built-in fat carrier. Track your numbers. Stop guessing and start measuring.\\n\\nI switched. My fasting glucose dropped from 144 to 106. My creatinine reversed direction. My GFR climbed. The foam disappeared. My doctor cancelled the medication conversation entirely.\\n\\nThe science was never wrong. The delivery was.\\n\\nEleven weeks of cinnamon powder. Eleven weeks of gritty smoothies and brown-stained mugs and numbers that didn't budge and foam that never cleared.\\n\\nI almost gave up on cinnamon entirely. Almost accepted that another medication was inevitable. Almost accepted I was following my father's path to the dialysis chair.\\n\\nI was wrong.\\n\\nThe cinnamon was never the problem. The form was. Powder can't guarantee species, dose, or absorption. It's a different product every single morning dressed up in the same glass.\\n\\nMetabolae gives me the right species at the right dose in a form my body can actually use. Every time. No variability. No guessing. No 20-minute smoothie ritual.\\n\\nMy fasting glucose went from 144 to 106. My creatinine reversed. My GFR climbed. The foam is gone. My doctor said no additional medication. My wife says I look ten years younger.\\n\\nIf you're taking cinnamon and it's not working, it's not you. It's the form.\\n\\nTry the right one. Track your numbers. You'll see the difference.\\n\\nJohn Tucker\\n\\nP.S. I felt the energy shift within the first three days. Saw the foam thinning by day five. Fasting glucose dropping measurably by day seven. Creatinine reversed and GFR climbing at my eight-week follow-up. Avoided additional medication entirely. My blender is in the garage now collecting dust. Your timeline might be different. But you won't know unless you try.\\n\\nP.P.S. Every morning you spend mixing powder is another morning of the wrong species at the wrong dose that can't absorb. Every day that passes is another day of glucose-saturated blood passing through kidney filters that are already damaged. Stop guessing. Start knowing. Order now.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/lp-pd1-cc-mix-bloodsugar-vs-a\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Metabolae\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":16569,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":149,\"duplicates\":34,\"reachDelta1d\":206,\"reachDelta7d\":2692,\"reachDelta30d\":7505},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AU\",\"CA\",\"GB\",\"NZ\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":47,\"currentRank\":47,\"rankDelta\":-4,\"improvementPct\":3.53},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1585733003018505\",\"collationId\":\"2337015163797111\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-09-09T12:47:50.481Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-09-08T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-09-30T12:50:35.222Z\",\"daysRunning\":80,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/784e2a3625109af703e34806bd2f5e0adb429e753efbf81ee059ef71cedbadd9.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1320220534499362\",\"name\":\"Cristian - Co-Founder, Metabolae\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/profile_picture/1320220534499362.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1320220534499362\",\"liveAdsCount\":679,\"reach30d\":410049,\"totalReach\":778720,\"countriesCount\":5,\"facebookLikes\":1946,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"If it feels like you have been seeing fewer ads from us lately, you are right.\\n\\nWe pulled a chunk of our own advertising this month, on purpose, and we want to tell you the real reason before we turn it back on.\\n\\nBecause if you are about to buy Ceylon cinnamon for your kidneys, the reason we went quiet is the single most important thing you can know first. It is the difference between it working and it doing nothing at all.\\n\\nHere is what was happening.\\n\\nOur ads were working. People were seeing them, getting interested, and going off to buy. But instead of coming to us, a chunk of them searched our name, found a cheaper listing on a marketplace, and bought something they believed was Metabolae.\\n\\nIt was not our product. Not our formula. Not anything we had ever touched. But because it carried our name, they believed they were getting the same thing.\\n\\nThat is the trap we had to pull our own ads to stop.\\n\\nBut the counterfeit is only the loudest version of a much bigger problem. And the way we came to understand that problem is a little embarrassing to admit.\\n\\nFor a while, we kept hearing from people who told us they had tried cinnamon before and felt nothing. At first, we assumed they simply needed something different. Then we looked closer at what they had actually been taking.\\n\\nThey were not failing with cinnamon. They were each taking a completely different version of it, all wearing the same name.\\n\\nThat is when it clicked. The word cinnamon is doing something sneaky. It is hiding three completely different things behind one label. We started calling it the label illusion, and once you see it, you cannot buy cinnamon the same way again.\\n\\nHere is the part most people are never told.\\n\\nWhen researchers study cinnamon for the kidneys, they do not study it the way you buy it. They do not study a word on a jar. They study a specific plant, prepared a specific way, at a specific strength.\\n\\nThree things. The plant. The potency. The pathway. Those three decide whether two bags are actually the same product, or just share the same name.\\n\\nMiss even one, and it does not matter how honest the label is. You are no longer holding the thing that worked in the research.\\n\\nMost products miss all three.\\n\\nThe plant. This is where most people get fooled. Cinnamon sounds like one ingredient, but it is really a category. Like the word car covers both a new vehicle and a rusted-out one sitting in a field. Most of what is sold is Cassia, a cheaper bark that only shares the name, carries far more coumarin, and was never the plant in the studies.\\n\\nThe potency. Matching the plant is only half of it. A tiny amount of the right plant is still not what the research was built on. Most products carry a fraction of it.\\n\\nThe pathway. Even the right plant at the right strength has to get where your body can use it. Most cinnamon is a dry powder with nothing to carry it, so it slides through the gut and never reaches the tiny filters in your kidneys at all.\\n\\nSame word on the label. A completely different thing in the capsule.\\n\\nThat is why the cinnamon you may have already tried did nothing. The foam never cleared. The numbers never moved. Not because cinnamon does not work, but because you were never actually taking the thing that does.\\n\\nAnd here is what makes this different from any other supplement decision.\\n\\nThe biggest problem was never paying too much. It was paying for something without ever knowing what was actually inside. Most mistakes like that are cheap. But your kidneys filter everything your body is trying to get rid of, and when something matters that much, the hard part is not choosing. It is knowing whether the choice was based on the truth.\\n\\nSo we built Metabolae around those same three things, from day one. The right plant. The right potency. The right pathway.\\n\\nThe plant. Before a single softgel is ever bagged, every batch goes through species verification, because to the eye, Cassia and true Ceylon look almost identical. Every batch gets a Certificate of Analysis, a document that names the exact species and confirms it is Cinnamomum verum, grown on a single estate in Sri Lanka.\\n\\nThe potency. A concentrated 12 to 1 extract at the 7,200mg equivalent the studies were built on. Not a fraction of it.\\n\\nThe pathway. Paired with MCT oil, the fat that carries the compound to the filters that need it, instead of letting it pass straight through.\\n\\nThird party tested. Sealed in a bag. One softgel a day. Nothing in it that does not need to be there.\\n\\nThe right plant. The right potency. The right pathway. That is the whole thing.\\n\\nSo we are scaling our ads back up now. But before we do, we wanted to say this plainly, one time, so no one gets caught in the gap.\\n\\nIf you are considering Metabolae, buy it from us directly. Not from a marketplace. Not from a listing that happens to be cheaper. Everything else carrying our name is someone else's guess at what we make.\\n\\nThe real Metabolae is sold only through our own store. Nowhere else.\\n\\nAnd every order placed there is covered by our 90 day money back guarantee. Full bag or empty. No restocking fee. We cannot stand behind anything bought anywhere else, because anything bought anywhere else is not ours.\\n\\nWhen it comes to something as important as your kidneys, you deserve to know exactly what is inside the bag you choose. The plant. The potency. The pathway. Proven, not promised.\\n\\nBuy from the real source. It is the only one there is.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/lp-pd1-cc-mix-bloodsugar-vs-a\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Metabolae\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":35585,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":320,\"duplicates\":32,\"reachDelta1d\":331,\"reachDelta7d\":2491,\"reachDelta30d\":14805},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AU\",\"CA\",\"GB\",\"NZ\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":32,\"currentRank\":32,\"rankDelta\":-2,\"improvementPct\":4.71},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1026688673786706\",\"collationId\":\"1444172681136042\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-24T03:28:13.755Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-10-22T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-03T12:08:56.742Z\",\"daysRunning\":36,\"media\":{\"type\":\"video\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/thumbnails/42a36d6be898086c50acac28329f8aeff5de0e22a211af17ce076c6e2167866a.jpg\",\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/video/9ea2827d039c561db0351d85888b52a869cbd25f376019717f15f7c74383f55e.mp4\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"116565354374771\",\"name\":\"Blood Sugar Wellness\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/profile_picture/116565354374771.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"116565354374771\",\"liveAdsCount\":240,\"reach30d\":0,\"totalReach\":0,\"countriesCount\":1,\"facebookLikes\":2755,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Want to Support Healthy Blood Sugar — All Day Long? ❤️\\n\\nExperience the Power of Resilia Ceylon Cinnamon + Berberine!\\n\\n✅ Supports healthy blood sugar levels\\n✅ Supports healthy insulin response\\n✅ Supports healthy glucose metabolism\\n✅ Supports healthy post-meal blood sugar\\n✅ 1,000mg True Ceylon Cinnamon Extract\\n✅ Standardized for cinnamaldehyde & polyphenols\\n✅ Plus 500mg Berberine HCl\\n\\nSupport your metabolic health with two powerful ingredients working together — 30-Day Risk-Free Guarantee.\",\"transcript\":\"I came home forty minutes early. Lorraine, wait. Get out of my house. Thirty two years married, and I heard it before I saw it. Can I be honest with you even now? Go ahead. I'm sorry you found it like that. I am. I never wanted that. But when I look at you now, I see a tired old woman who gave up. Look at your face, Lorraine. Look at the weight you carry around. You let yourself go, Lorraine. Then we're getting divorced. And my mother turns 90 in six weeks. She loves you and she won't uninvite you, so we'll both be standing in that room. Okay. That's the last of it. Have you eaten today? I'll eat. That's not what I asked you, mom. Thirty two years, Nia. It fit in one truck. Take that dish home to Marcus. Nobody's cooking on my account. That first night, I sat at that counter a long time, and I couldn't think of one reason to turn the lamp on. So I did the only thing I knew how to do, up at 05:30, walking until my legs gave out. No wine, no bread, no sugar. I'm 61 years old. My mother turns 90 in five weeks, and there is more life in her face than in mine. Dennis didn't leave a marriage, he left this, and I still have to walk into that room. Weight coming off, belt in a notch. My face didn't get the message. Can I be honest with you? Everybody else has been. You look thinner and you look worse. You've gone puffy and gray, mom, like grandpa went that last year and it frightens me. I own a mirror, Nia. Grandma called me. She's more worried about your face than her own birthday. I'll be at the party. Okay. So for what you're describing, you want the metabolism booster, the collagen powder, the apple cider vinegar gummies, and honestly, I do the green tea capsules underneath as well. Will it work? It's the best thing we sell. Everybody says the same thing. Then give me all of it, whatever it takes. I bought the most expensive things on that shelf. Every morning, every night, a full week. And by Sunday, my face was worse than when I started, hungrier, more tired, and still gray. I sat on the edge of the tub and I thought, that's it then. This is just what I look like now. Gary walked out on me two years ago, told me I'd given up on myself. Sound familiar? Lorraine, you remember what I looked like that year? This is what changed it. What is this? A pouch of cinnamon? Real Ceylon cinnamon and berberine from Sri Lanka, not the cassia from China that's in every grocery store. The cassia strains your liver and does nothing for you. This one has a compound called cenamaldehyde. It opens the receptors on your cells back up so the sugar you eat has somewhere to go beside your waste in your face. Carmen, I've tried everything. The counter, the gummies, the most expensive stuff they had. Lorraine, I'm 63. Everybody at my work thinks I'm 45. Nobody believes me. And this is the only thing I take. There's about three weeks left in that pouch. Two capsules every single morning. Don't skip one. Oh, this is nonsense. But I took two with my coffee the next morning. Twenty minutes later, something I hadn't felt in years. My head was clear, not caffeine clear, different, steady. By three in the afternoon, I was still steady, no crash, no reaching for the fridge. The afternoon fog was gone. The pull towards sugar was gone. By day four, I woke up and my face wasn't puffy. The grayness I'd been living with was lifting. One side of my face was the woman he walked away from. The other side was me at 40. There I was. That evening at 06:30, I checked in the car mirror. The puffiness hadn't come back. The bags under my eyes were softer. By week one, I told myself it couldn't be two capsules because my whole body felt different. Lighter, warmer, awake. The gray was gone. It had been working on my cells the whole time I'd given up on them. Three weeks in, and Carmen's pouch was empty. The divorce papers came Tuesday. Nobody called anymore. Some days I barely spoke. Carmen, where do I get it? I'll send you the link. Recilia, real Ceylon cinnamon from Sri Lanka with berberine. One thousand milligrams of Ceylon, five hundred milligrams of berberine. Two capsules every morning. Made for women past 50. Thirty day guarantee, give me six. Two days later, it arrived. The party was a week away. Finally, I was ready for something. Almost a month on it now. And the night before my mother's dinner, I laid a red dress on the bed. First time since March, I wasn't dreading a room, and the dress fit the way it used to. You're ready and you look amazing. Oh, look at my daughter. Oh, baby. Where did you find that face? That's the one I raised. Happy birthday, mama. Lorraine, what did you do? Half this wowed is staring at you. Lorraine, you look beautiful. You look genuinely beautiful. I said you were tired. I'm sorry for what I said to you that night. I shouldn't have said any of it. She's only 90 once. Dennis, let's give her a good picture. Everybody in. Lorraine, you stay right here next to me. Okay. Out with it. Your mother thinks you've met somebody. Two capsules in the morning. That's it, that's it. I bought six. That one's yours. Denise asked me twice last year if I was sick. Twice, Beverly. Mom, I saw them back in March. What? Her car outside his office. I never told you I thought it would break you. And then a few weeks ago, I stopped being frightened for you. That's how I knew you were back. Come Sunday, I'm cooking. Two weeks on that stuff, and my husband won't let me leave the house without him. You look brand new. And listen, they stand behind it. Thirty days. If you don't see it, you don't pay a cent. I checked before I ordered more. I don't trust anybody's promises. Oh my gosh. In here. Food's on. It's called Resilia, real Ceylon cinnamon from Sri Lanka with berberine, not the cassia from China that strains your liver and does nothing. This one has cinnamaldehyne that opens your cell doors back up and berberine that flips the switch to burn the stored sugar instead of holding it in your face. One thousand milligrams of Ceylon, five hundred milligrams of berberine, two capsules every morning. That's it. It works on your cells the whole day. Six weeks ago, my husband told me I was a woman who'd given up. That woman doesn't live at this address. It's up to 70% off right now. Thirty day guarantee and the link's underneath.\",\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://resilia.shop/products/resilia-ceylon-cinnamon\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"resilia.shop\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Explore the Power of Ceylon Cinnamon Today!\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":null},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":32,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":4,\"currentRank\":4,\"rankDelta\":0,\"improvementPct\":1.67},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1083126674716971\",\"collationId\":\"1442317558075295\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-23T12:29:27.375Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-10-22T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-04T02:09:34.322Z\",\"daysRunning\":13,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/f