{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0157udoaZp29mvA7ANYcuaw5","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"requestId\":\"94c6110c-e90f-4f16-88ad-5382f93d7e6b\",\"data\":[{\"id\":\"facebook_1088847760284220\",\"collationId\":\"1395452092789787\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-09-13T05:51:59.406Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-09-12T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-10-05T08:31:30.991Z\",\"daysRunning\":23,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/a33f135ac8bbad196a57ef191a8100ce376dabace75a4c3ac9f9bccac628493e.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"101838912282067\",\"name\":\"Lisa Anderson\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/101838912282067.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"101838912282067\",\"liveAdsCount\":139,\"reach30d\":324,\"totalReach\":1188914,\"countriesCount\":3,\"facebookLikes\":299,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"My husband died a week ago.\\n\\nAnd there’s one photo on my phone I still can’t bring myself to delete.\\n\\nHe’s asleep in his recliner after dinner.\\n\\nMouth open.\\n\\nTV still on.\\n\\nGlasses and coffee sitting beside him.\\n\\nI took it because I thought it was funny.\\n\\nNow I look at that photo and wonder how many times his body was trying to tell us something we dismissed as “just getting older.”\\n\\nBecause that was our explanation for everything.\\n\\nThe naps after dinner.\\n\\nFalling asleep halfway through a movie.\\n\\nSaying he felt worn out even when he hadn’t done much that day.\\n\\nHe was 61.\\n\\nOf course he was more tired than he used to be.\\n\\nAt least that’s what we told ourselves.\\n\\nThen his cholesterol numbers started worrying his doctor.\\n\\nThat didn’t scare us much either.\\n\\nHe didn’t look sick.\\n\\nHe still cut the grass.\\n\\nStill drove our granddaughter to school when our daughter worked early.\\n\\nStill argued with our son about football every Sunday.\\n\\nStill complained whenever I bought the “healthy” bread.\\n\\nSo we did what we thought responsible people were supposed to do.\\n\\nWe changed breakfast.\\n\\nEggs became oatmeal.\\n\\nButter disappeared.\\n\\nI started checking labels at the grocery store.\\n\\nHe walked more.\\n\\nWe ordered fish instead of burgers.\\n\\nHe even stopped buying the cookies he’d eaten with his coffee for probably 20 years.\\n\\nEvery few months, there was another little sacrifice.\\n\\nAnd every time, he’d joke:\\n\\n“At this rate, you’ll have me eating cardboard by Christmas.”\\n\\nWe laughed.\\n\\nBut I could tell it bothered him.\\n\\nBecause food slowly stopped being something he enjoyed.\\n\\nIt became something he negotiated with.\\n\\nCan I eat this?\\n\\nHow bad is that?\\n\\nShould I skip dessert?\\n\\nMaybe I shouldn’t have the potatoes.\\n\\nAnd underneath all of it was the same belief:\\n\\nIf his cholesterol was high, he must still be eating the wrong foods.\\n\\nSo whenever his numbers weren’t where he wanted them, we blamed dinner.\\n\\nThen breakfast.\\n\\nThen snacks.\\n\\nThen ourselves.\\n\\nThat’s the part I keep thinking about now.\\n\\nBecause after he died, I sat at our kitchen table with his paperwork spread everywhere.\\n\\nOld blood work.\\n\\nAppointment notes.\\n\\nMedication lists.\\n\\nReceipts he’d stuffed into the same folder for reasons I’ll never understand.\\n\\nI wasn’t looking for anything specific.\\n\\nI think I just wanted to understand him.\\n\\nAnd somewhere between those pages, I realized how simplistic our thinking had become.\\n\\nWe had reduced cholesterol to a plate of food.\\n\\nEat the “bad” thing → cholesterol goes up.\\n\\nEat the “good” thing → cholesterol goes down.\\n\\nBut cholesterol is more complicated than any single breakfast or dinner.\\n\\nYour body produces cholesterol too, and factors beyond one meal can influence your overall numbers.\\n\\nThat sounds obvious when I write it now.\\n\\nIt didn’t feel obvious when we were standing in the grocery aisle debating whether he was “allowed” to buy cheese.\\n\\nThat realization sent me down a rabbit hole.\\n\\nNot because I thought I could go backward.\\n\\nI can’t.\\n\\nAnd I’m not going to pretend a supplement could have changed what happened to my husband.\\n\\nBut I needed to understand why we'd spent so much time treating every plate like it was the whole problem.\\n\\nEventually my reading led me to Ceylon cinnamon.\\n\\nI'd heard about cinnamon before.\\n\\nWhat caught my attention wasn't simply the ingredient.\\n\\nIt was how it was being delivered.\\n\\nCinnamon contains naturally occurring compounds including cinnamaldehyde.\\n\\nAnd cinnamaldehyde is fat-soluble.\\n\\nThat made me look differently at the dry cinnamon capsules I'd seen for years.\\n\\nPowder.\\n\\nCapsule.\\n\\nSwallow.\\n\\nDone.\\n\\nThen I found Primal Remedies.\\n\\nThey pair true Ceylon cinnamon from Sri Lanka with MCT oil inside an oil-based softgel.\\n\\nThe MCT oil acts as a lipid carrier for the fat-soluble cinnamon compounds.\\n\\nThey call the approach the Fat-Soluble Absorption Pathway.\\n\\nThat was what finally made the formulation make sense to me.\\n\\nNot “take more cinnamon.”\\n\\nThink about how the cinnamon is delivered.\\n\\nI also learned there is a meaningful difference between Ceylon cinnamon and the common cassia cinnamon most people recognize.\\n\\nCeylon naturally contains much lower levels of coumarin.\\n\\nAnd after everything I'd just been reading about health, I wasn't interested in blindly taking spoonfuls of random cinnamon from the kitchen cabinet.\\n\\nI wanted something simple that could fit beside the habits I was already trying to improve.\\n\\nSo I started using Primal Remedies Ceylon Cinnamon + MCT Oil as nutritional support alongside those habits.\\n\\nNot instead of eating well.\\n\\nNot instead of moving.\\n\\nAnd certainly not instead of speaking with a doctor and getting cholesterol checked.\\n\\nBecause if losing my husband taught me anything, it's that guessing how healthy someone is because they “look fine” isn't enough.\\n\\nLast Sunday, our daughter came over with the grandchildren.\\n\\nMy grandson climbed into Grandpa's recliner.\\n\\nSame chair from that photo.\\n\\nWithin 20 minutes, he was asleep sideways with one sock missing while everyone else was talking.\\n\\nFor half a second, I almost reached for my phone.\\n\\nThat's what I always did when his grandfather fell asleep like that.\\n\\nThen I stopped.\\n\\nThat chair doesn't make me laugh the same way anymore.\\n\\nBut it reminds me why I'm paying attention now.\\n\\nI don't want every meal to become a punishment.\\n\\nI don't want to spend the rest of my life afraid of breakfast.\\n\\nI just want to understand what I'm putting into my body, support it intelligently, keep getting my numbers checked, and be here for as many noisy Sunday afternoons with my family as I can.\\n\\nIf cholesterol has turned every plate in your house into a calculation, start by talking with your healthcare professional and understanding your actual numbers.\\n\\nAnd if you're already making healthier changes and want to learn about the Ceylon cinnamon + MCT oil approach I've added to my own routine, you can read about Primal Remedies below.\\n\\nBecause sometimes the photograph you thought was ordinary becomes the one that changes how you look at everything.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://primal-remedies.com/pages/adv1-news-cinnamon\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"primal-remedies.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"The Photo I Can't Look at the Same Way Anymore\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"🚨 UPDATE: Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg is SELLING OUT faster than expected! Lock in your order NOW to get 70% OFF + FREE Shipping\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":132059,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":1189,\"duplicates\":7,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta