{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AvnULKxgERvCFj7VBXd9Yu","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"requestId\":\"cfde9f76-d35c-4667-8d96-11ebc21a2f0f\",\"data\":[{\"id\":\"facebook_1113722024862628\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-11-26T17:52:51.794Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-11-26T08:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-26T17:54:55.599Z\",\"daysRunning\":1,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/d4d5e2dee66cf8c7ad0e41eb7c471ce4d028d9bf5b98490ce0e56467e87ae3f8.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1214883091704059\",\"name\":\"Blood Pressure Wellness Community\",\"logoUrl\":null,\"facebookPageId\":\"1214883091704059\",\"liveAdsCount\":785,\"reach30d\":100670,\"totalReach\":195214,\"countriesCount\":1,\"facebookLikes\":182,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I almost let my wife walk up the mountain alone on our 30th anniversary.\\n\\nI was sure my heart would quit halfway up, the way my father's quit in his armchair at 61. 7 months earlier, a cath lab nurse had asked me 5 questions on the phone that nobody had ever asked me. 5 yeses. And she told me it had already reached my heart.\\n\\nIt was the second day of our trip. We'd planned that hike for 6 months. Beech Mountain Trail in the Smokies. About 4.2 miles round trip. A moderate climb. The kind of hike a regular 56-year-old man with a regular heart should be able to do without thinking twice about it.\\n\\nWe'd done it 17 years earlier, in 2007, on our 13th anniversary, when our daughter was 11 and we'd left her with her grandmother for the weekend. Back then, Diane had been the one who got winded. I had been the one pulling her up the last switchback.\\n\\nThis time, she came out of the cabin at 8:15 in the morning in her hiking boots and the little day pack she'd bought for the trip. She looked at me sitting on the porch with my coffee and said:\\n\\nYou coming?\\n\\nAnd I almost said no. I almost said, You go ahead, honey. I'll meet you back here.\\n\\nBecause some part of me had already decided I wasn't going to make it. The last time I'd tried a real hill, my chest had gone heavy as a sandbag halfway up.\\n\\nI would rather have her think I was just being lazy than watch me fall behind on that trail. Or watch me have the same heart attack my father had, halfway up a mountain, 2 hours from the nearest hospital.\\n\\nMy name is Glenn Mabry. I'm 56. I'm the operations manager at a heating and cooling supply distributor in Pensacola, Florida. My wife Diane is 55 and teaches fourth grade. We've been married 30 years as of this past April, and we've lived in the same house off Nine Mile Road since 1998, the year my father died.\\n\\nI want to tell you about the hike. And about Diane's cousin Marlene, who has worked as a cardiac nurse in a cath lab for 26 years. Nobody had ever put it to me the way she did.\\n\\nBut to tell you about the hike, I have to tell you about the 4 years before it. Because the 4 years before the hike are the actual story.\\n\\nIt started so slowly I'm not even sure when it started. It doesn't announce itself. It just shows up one morning as a thing you've been doing for a while, and you don't remember the first day of it.\\n\\nI think it was probably 2021. The summer Diane and I went to Destin with her sister Pam and Pam's husband Greg. We walked the beach in the evening. About 20 minutes in, I felt something in my chest I didn't have a name for. Not pain. Just heaviness. Like somebody had set a soft weight on top of my heart.\\n\\nI stopped to take off my sandals and was actually grateful for the excuse. I bent over and took my time with the buckle. Then I straightened up, kept walking, and pretended nothing was different.\\n\\nThat was the first time I lied to myself about my body. After that, I lied more often.\\n\\nI took the elevator at work, after 22 years as a stairs guy. I stopped doing the yard work on Saturdays. I cut our walks from 45 minutes to 25, and then to, Maybe just around the block, honey. I'm tired today.\\n\\nThe lies were small. They were always reasonable. A long week at the warehouse. My back acting up. Just not in the mood.\\n\\nShe didn't push. Diane has never been a pusher. She just slowed down with me. We walked around the block instead of around the neighborhood. We sat on the porch instead of going for a drive. We watched a movie at home instead of going to the theater.\\n\\nThe world got smaller. I made it smaller. And she let me make it smaller, because she loves me.\\n\\nThen there were the other things. I think a lot of men reading this know exactly what I mean and have spent a long time pretending they don't.\\n\\nMy hands felt heavy when I put my work boots on in the morning. My fingers felt thick, like I was wearing thin gloves. I'd go to bed at 9:45 and still wake up at 6 feeling like I'd done a shift in a coal mine.\\n\\nDiane would come up behind me in the kitchen and put her hand on my back, the way she's done for 30 years, and I wouldn't turn around the way I used to. Some part of me had gotten quiet.\\n\\nI was breathing hard going up the porch steps. 3 steps, from the driveway to the front door. Some mornings I could feel my heart pounding before my feet ever touched the floor.\\n\\nMy socks left deep marks on my ankles by evening. I was thirsty at night and up to pee around 3 AM. My feet had this cold, buzzing feeling when I got in bed.\\n\\nAnd the man in the bathroom mirror was no longer the man who'd pulled Diane up that switchback on our 13th anniversary.\\n\\nI went to Dr. Lindgren for my yearly physical in November of 2023. My blood pressure was 142 over 88, which he said was creeping up there. My weight was up about 16 pounds.\\n\\nThen he looked at my bloodwork. LDL cholesterol: 164. Fasting sugar: 118. A1C: 6.2. Triglycerides high. Liver enzymes a little up.\\n\\nHe said, Glenn, your pressure, your cholesterol and your sugar are all heading the same direction. And that direction is your heart.\\n\\nI remember staring at the paper like it belonged to someone else.\\n\\nHe put me on lisinopril for the pressure and atorvastatin for the cholesterol. He said if that A1C crossed 6.5, we'd be starting metformin. Walk more, cut carbs, lose weight, come back in 6 months. We'll keep an eye on it, he said.\\n\\nI filled the prescriptions. And because Diane read somewhere that cinnamon was good for blood sugar, every morning that winter I shook some out of the jar on her spice rack onto my oatmeal.\\n\\nNothing changed.\\n\\nI want to be careful with that sentence. The pills did something. My pressure came down a little on the cuff at the drugstore. My cholesterol moved when they rechecked it in January.\\n\\nBut the way I felt walking out to the truck in the morning. The way I felt after lunch. The way I felt when I pictured Diane in her hiking boots, ready for a trail I knew I couldn't do. None of that changed.\\n\\nThe medication had addressed a number on a chart. The man underneath the number was still slowly being erased. And nobody was talking to me about that.\\n\\nIn February of 2024, I started sitting on the porch by myself at night. I thought about my father.\\n\\nMy father, Earl, worked at a paper mill in Mobile his whole life. In his late 50s, he started getting smaller. He stopped fishing. He stopped coming to my little brother's football games. He ended up watching golf on TV with the sound off, because he didn't have the energy to follow it.\\n\\nThat's how my mother found him, on a Sunday afternoon in October of 1998. In his armchair. Golf on. Sound off. His coffee cold on the side table. He was 61. His heart. A massive heart attack. He was gone before the ambulance turned onto their street. That summer, his doctor had told him his pressure was fine for a man his age.\\n\\nI had told myself my whole adult life that I was not going to be him. And I was sitting on a porch at 56 years old, 5 years younger than he was in that chair, becoming him.\\n\\nDiane came out at one point that night with a cup of coffee, even though it was 10 PM. She sat down next to me. She didn't say anything for a long time. Then she said:\\n\\nGlenn. Talk to me.\\n\\nI said, I don't know what's happening to me, Di.\\n\\nShe held my hand. She didn't say anything else. We sat there like that for probably 25 minutes. Then she went inside to bed. And I sat there for another hour by myself and made a decision.\\n\\nI was going to figure this out.\\n\\nThe next morning there was a yellow sticky note on the coffee maker in Diane's handwriting. Marlene's cell number. Nothing else. That's as close to pushing as my wife has ever come.\\n\\nMarlene is Diane's cousin. She's been a cardiac nurse for 26 years, all of them in the cath lab at a hospital in Mobile. That's the room where they run a thin wire up into your heart to see what's blocked. I called her on my lunch break, from my truck in the warehouse lot.\\n\\nI told her about the beach, the porch steps, the numbers. She let me finish. Then she asked me 5 questions, one at a time.\\n\\nDo your feet buzz or go cold at night? Yes.\\n\\nAre you up around 3 to pee? Yes.\\n\\nDo your socks leave deep lines on your ankles by supper? Yes.\\n\\nIs your sugar creeping, even a little? Yes.\\n\\nAnd your chest goes heavy on the stairs, and your doctor says your pressure's coming along?\\n\\nYes. 5 for 5. She was quiet for a second.\\n\\nGlenn, I've been in that cath lab for 26 years, she said. Men like you come through those doors every week. Most of them come in on a gurney, in the middle of a heart attack, with a chart full of numbers somebody called managed. Your feet, your nights, your ankles, your sugar, your chest. That's not 5 problems. That's one problem. And it's already reached your heart.\\n\\nShe said she'd send me some things to read, and to call her back with my questions.\\n\\nBefore I hung up, I asked her not to tell Diane yet. I didn't want to get her hopes up over nothing. Marlene said, For now.\\n\\nI am not a smart man. I want to say that up front. I went to community college for a year and a half. I am not somebody who reads medical papers for fun.\\n\\nBut I read everything Marlene sent. Half of it went over my head. So I wrote my questions down on a notepad from work, and that Saturday morning I called her back.\\n\\nMy first question was the dumbest one. How is my sugar part of my heart?\\n\\nOkay. Simple version, Marlene said. Your sugar isn't supposed to stay in your blood. It's supposed to go into your cells and get burned for energy. Every cell has little doors on it. Insulin is the knock that opens them.\\n\\nYou eat. Sugar goes into your blood. Insulin knocks. The doors open. The sugar goes in and becomes fuel. That's how it's supposed to work.\\n\\nBut after enough years, the doors quit answering. Insulin knocks. The door barely opens. So the sugar stays in your blood. Trapped. Your body pumps out more insulin to force the doors. More sugar. More insulin. More pressure on the whole system.\\n\\nYour doors quit answering, Glenn. So the sugar sits in your blood and rides around with every heartbeat, grinding the inside of your arteries raw. There's a thin lining in there, the wall of the artery, that's supposed to stay soft and smooth, so your arteries can open and relax. Raw goes stiff. Stiff makes your heart shove harder. That's your 142.\\n\\nAnd my cholesterol?\\n\\nSame trapped sugar. It tells your liver to pump out more. That's your 164. One problem. 2 numbers. 2 prescriptions.\\n\\nSo what are my pills doing?\\n\\nYour pills are holding the numbers down. That matters. But neither one opens a single door. Nobody's touched the wall.\\n\\nThen she said the part nobody had ever explained to me.\\n\\nThe smallest vessels go first, Glenn. Your feet at night. Up at 3. Your socks. Your sugar. Your chest. That was your 5 for 5. It's the same problem, in the little roads first. The roads to your heart are just the last stop. That's why 3 porch steps feel like a hill. That's why a man slowly stops doing everything he used to do.\\n\\nAfter we hung up, I sat at the kitchen table staring at the wall for a while. For the first time in 4 years, somebody had explained why I felt the way I felt.\\n\\nNot you're 56. Not it's just aging. Not walk more. An actual mechanism. My doors weren't opening. And my heart was paying for it.\\n\\nI kept reading. And the thing that kept coming up, over and over, was Ceylon cinnamon.\\n\\nI don't want to oversell this. I'm not a doctor. But true Ceylon has 2 compounds in it that kept showing up in everything I read.\\n\\nThe first is called MHCP. It works on the doors. It gets them answering the knock again, so the sugar can leave your blood and get into your cells.\\n\\nThe second is called cinnamaldehyde, the part that gives cinnamon its smell. It works on the wall. It calms that worn-down lining and helps it make its own nitric oxide again, the signal that tells an artery to relax and open.\\n\\n2 compounds. 1 plant. One opens the doors. The other calms the wall the trapped sugar has been grinding raw.\\n\\nNot cinnamon fixes your heart like some kitchen cure. I don't believe in that. But if shut doors and trapped sugar were wearing out my arteries, then getting the doors open and calming the wall made sense.\\n\\nThen I learned the part that almost made me quit. Most cinnamon is built wrong.\\n\\nThe first problem is the tree. Most cinnamon is cassia. The cheap grocery-store kind, mostly from China, and hundreds of times higher in coumarin, which is hard on your liver if you take it every day.\\n\\nI went and looked at the jar on Diane's spice rack. The one I'd been shaking on my oatmeal all winter. Cassia.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon is Cinnamomum verum. It comes from Sri Lanka, and it's low in coumarin. That was my first rule.\\n\\nThe second problem was dose. A lot of products hide 500 milligrams of cinnamon inside a blend and call it advanced heart support. That's not a real dose. That's a label.\\n\\nThe third problem was delivery. The MHCP and the cinnamaldehyde dissolve in fat, not water. They need a fat to ride in. But most cinnamon supplements are dry dust. And those big tubs you scoop into your coffee? A scoop of dust is still dust. Half of it floats, half of it sinks, and you just drink it and trust the label.\\n\\nI'd spent too many years trusting we'll keep an eye on it. I was done trusting labels.\\n\\nThe next Saturday, I called Marlene back and read her my 3 rules off the back of an envelope. Right tree. Real dose. In oil.\\n\\nShe laughed. Glenn, that's exactly why I take the one I take. Then she texted me the name.\\n\\nMetabolae.\\n\\nI ordered it from the company's own website that night, because Marlene warned me there are fakes on Amazon in a look-alike bag. It came with a 90-day money-back guarantee, which meant something to me, because I don't trust companies that don't stand behind their stuff.\\n\\nThe first bag came on a Thursday while Diane was at school. I put a saucer on the kitchen counter. On one side, a spoonful from the jar on her spice rack. On the other side, 1 softgel, cut open with my pocketknife.\\n\\nDry brown dust on the left. Deep amber oil on the right, and the whole kitchen smelled like a bakery.\\n\\nI stood there a long time. 4 months of my oatmeal was sitting on the left side of that saucer.\\n\\nThe right side answered all 3 of my rules. Right tree: true Ceylon from Sri Lanka, DNA-verified, low coumarin, tested every batch. Real dose: a 12 to 1 extract, so 1 softgel holds what's in 7,200 milligrams of bark. In oil: oil-fused, they call it. I know. That sounds like a word a company uses when it wants normal people to stop reading. It just means the extract is dissolved right into MCT oil from coconuts and sealed inside the softgel. Already in the fat it needs to ride in.\\n\\nI want to be clear. I did not stop my medication. I am going to say that twice. I did not stop my medication. I am not telling you to stop yours. That is a conversation between you and your doctor.\\n\\nWhat I did was add something that went after the part nobody had been talking to me about. The doors and the wall.\\n\\nI started in March. I did not tell Diane. I think I was a little embarrassed. I had confessed on that porch that I didn't know what was happening to me, and now I was quietly taking cinnamon. So I put the bag behind the protein shakes she never drank, and I took 1 softgel with breakfast every morning, right next to the lisinopril and the statin, same dose as always.\\n\\nThe first 2 weeks, I didn't notice much.\\n\\nAround week 3, the heavy, slow feeling I'd had for so long that I'd forgotten it wasn't normal started to soften. Like the air around me wasn't quite as thick.\\n\\nThen one Tuesday evening in April, about 5 weeks in, I came home from work and walked up the porch steps. I was halfway across the porch before I realized I hadn't thought about them at all. My heart wasn't pounding. My chest wasn't heavy. I had been counting those 3 steps for almost 2 years.\\n\\nI stopped at the front door. I stood there for a minute. Then I turned around and looked at the steps. And I just stood there.\\n\\nI felt something in my chest I had not felt in 4 years. Small. Quiet. Careful hope.\\n\\nBy the end of April, Diane and I were walking after dinner again. Around the block. Then the next block. Then around the elementary school 2 streets over, about 35 minutes. Diane didn't say a word about it. She just put on her shoes when I put on mine.\\n\\nIn the last week of May, I went back to Dr. Lindgren for my 6-month checkup. He took my pressure, looked at it, and took it again on the other arm. 124 over 78. Then 122 over 76. He pulled up my bloodwork and scrolled for a long time without saying anything. A1C: 5.7. Fasting sugar: 96. My cholesterol was right where he wanted it.\\n\\nHe turned the screen around so I could see it. Let's leave everything right where it is, Glenn, he said. No metformin.\\n\\nI told him about the cinnamon. He wrote it in my chart.\\n\\nThat same week, I told Diane. I sat her down at the kitchen table and told her everything. The sticky note. Marlene's 5 questions. The jar on her spice rack being the wrong tree. The saucer. The bag behind her protein shakes.\\n\\nI told her I wasn't cured. I told her I wasn't stopping my medication. I told her I just felt like my body was finally working with me again.\\n\\nShe listened to all of it. Held my hand the whole time. When I was done, she said:\\n\\nGlenn. I noticed. I just didn't want to say anything before you did.\\n\\nThen she smiled a little. Marlene told me in April. She said you'd tell me when you were ready.\\n\\nShe bought herself a bag the next day. She turned 55 in June and said if it was helping me, she was going to give her own body the same chance.\\n\\nIn July, we booked the cabin.\\n\\nIn September, we drove up to the Smokies for our 30th anniversary trip. And on the morning of the second day, Diane came out of the cabin in her boots and her little day pack. She looked at me on the porch with my coffee and said:\\n\\nYou coming?\\n\\nI almost said no. There is a part of me that I think will always almost say no. 4 years of saying no leaves grooves in you that don't disappear in 6 months. I want to be honest about that too.\\n\\nThere was a moment on that porch where I considered staying behind. Considered going for the easier answer. Considered telling her I'd meet her back at the cabin.\\n\\nThen I thought about my father in the armchair. Golf on. Sound off. I thought about all the walks I'd already made smaller. All the stairs I'd avoided. All the times Diane had slowed down because I was too proud to admit I was scared.\\n\\nI put down the coffee. Went inside. Put on my boots. Came back out and said:\\n\\nYeah. I'm coming.\\n\\nHalfway up, on the first steep switchback, I stopped and waited for it. The sandbag. The tightness. My father's chair. It never came.\\n\\nWe made it to the top. I was slower than Diane. I'm not going to pretend I led the way the whole time. There were 2 places on the upper switchbacks where I stopped to drink water and catch my breath. I'm sure she noticed. But she also didn't have to slow down for me the way I had been afraid she would have to.\\n\\nWe did the 4.2 miles in just under 2 and a half hours. The guidebook said that was a normal time for 2 adults in moderate shape.\\n\\nNormal. That word nearly got me.\\n\\nAt the top, there's a stone overlook. We sat on it and ate the granola bars she'd packed. We could see for what felt like 50 miles. The Smokies were doing the thing they do where the ridges fade into each other in different shades of blue.\\n\\nDiane leaned her head on my shoulder. And I sat there feeling my heart beating in my chest like a regular heart. In a regular man. In a regular body that wasn't being erased anymore.\\n\\nShe said, Glenn.\\n\\nI said, Yeah.\\n\\nShe said, I missed you.\\n\\nI said, I know. I missed me too.\\n\\nWe sat there for about 20 minutes. We didn't say a whole lot else. On the walk down, she held my hand for half a mile. The middle half. She doesn't usually hold my hand on a hike, because we hike with poles. But she put her pole in her pack and just walked with me. And I think she knew, the way wives know, that she was getting a piece of me back.\\n\\nIf any part of this story sounds familiar to you.\\n\\nIf you've been on a porch making your world smaller a little bit at a time. If you've been letting your wife walk up the mountain alone. If you've been telling yourself this is just what 56 looks like, or 58, or 62.\\n\\nIf your blood pressure is creeping up. If your cholesterol came back high. If your sugar is a little high.\\n\\nIf your doctor says we'll keep an eye on it while your feet buzz, your chest goes heavy, your socks leave marks, and your body feels older every month.\\n\\nI'd just say this. Take a real look at it.\\n\\nThe doors on your cells stop answering. The sugar stays trapped in your blood. The wall of your arteries takes the beating. Your heart has to work harder. The pills can turn the numbers down. They don't open the doors, and they don't touch the wall.\\n\\nThat's what Metabolae is built for. Not a cure. Not a replacement for your medication. A way to go after the part of the picture nobody had explained to me.\\n\\nMetabolae comes with a 90-day money-back guarantee. That guarantee is part of why I ordered it. I've been on prescriptions, and nobody ever offered me my money back when a prescription didn't do anything for the way I actually felt. Metabolae gives you 90 days. If it's not the right fit, one email and you get your money back. That means something.\\n\\nI'm not going to tell you it will fix everything. I'm not going to tell you it will replace any conversation you should be having with your doctor. I'm going to tell you what it did for the man who almost stayed on a porch on his 30th anniversary instead of walking up the mountain with the woman he loves.\\n\\nIt gave me back enough that I went.\\n\\nIf you want to do what I did, here's all it is. Keep every pill your doctor gave you. Order Metabolae from the company's own website tonight, and watch out for the fakes on Amazon. A bag is 30 softgels, 1 month. Get 3. It took me 5 weeks to forget the porch steps and 12 weeks to hear no metformin, and that's exactly what the 90 days are for.\\n\\nI'm not a doctor, and I don't work for that company. Nobody's paying me to write this. I'm an operations manager from Pensacola who reads too late at night.\\n\\nThere is still more to come. I'm still walking with Diane after dinner. I'm still taking my medication with my doctor's knowledge. I'm not at the end of any road. I'm just on a different one than I was on last winter. And the road I'm on now goes up the mountain instead of stopping at the porch.\\n\\nI think about that moment on the cabin steps a lot. The moment where I almost let her go alone.\\n\\nI am not ever going to let her go alone again.\\n\\nGlenn\\n\\nP.S. In October, we had dinner at Pam's house, and Greg was sitting out on the back patio by himself. Greg is 61. The same age my father was in that armchair. I went out with 2 beers and sat down next to him. He told me he hadn't been on a real walk in 18 months. His blood pressure pill had just gone up. Pam had stopped asking him to do things because he kept saying no. I didn't sell him anything. I just told him the story I told you. 3 weeks later, Pam called Diane crying. Greg had spent the whole afternoon raking the leaves out front.\\n\\nP.P.S. If you tried cinnamon before and it did nothing, ask what you actually tried. Was it true Ceylon from Sri Lanka? Was it a real dose? Was it already in oil? Or was it cassia from a spice jar, dry dust in a pill, or a scoop of something in your coffee? The plant didn't fail you. The form did.\\n\\nP.P.P.S. If you take lisinopril, a statin, metformin, or anything your doctor prescribed, do not stop. Metabolae goes on top of what your doctor gave you, not in place of it. But please do not confuse managed with resolved. My numbers were being managed. My world was still getting smaller.\\n\\nDon't wait. I waited 4 years. I almost let my wife walk up a mountain by herself on our 30th anniversary because I had spent 4 years telling myself I was fine. Today is a better day than that one was. And if there's a man at your table who keeps saying he's just tired, share this with him tonight.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/pre-ins-heart\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Supports healthy blood sugar levels and metabolism with premium Ceylon cinnamon. Shop Metabolae's science-backed supplements. Free shipping available.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":null,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":629,\"currentRank\":629,\"rankDelta\":0,\"improvementPct\":80.13},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}}],\"pagination\":{\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"total\":4878,\"totalPages\":4878,\"offset\":0,\"isTotalApproximate\":false},\"meta\":{\"query\":null,\"keywords\":[\"ceylon\",\"weight\"],\"searchIn\":\"ad_copy\",\"categoryIds\":[],\"country\":null,\"countryFilterKind\":\"audienceCountry\",\"adCountries\":null,\"mainCountries\":[],\"trendSignal\":\"reach_growth_7d\",\"sortBy\":\"newest\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"requestedLimit\":1,\"effectiveLimit\":1,\"activeOnly\":true,\"status\":\"active\",\"page\":1,\"minTrafficGrowthPercent\":null,\"trafficGrowthPeriod\":\"last30d\",\"trafficGrowth\":null,\"adsGrowth\":null,\"pageReachGrowth\":null,\"adReachGrowth\":null,\"language\":null,\"adLanguages\":[],\"cta\":[],\"currency\":null,\"currencies\":[],\"shopifyAppIds\":[],\"excludeShopifyAppIds\":[],\"pixelIds\":[],\"excludePixelIds\":[],\"themeIds\":[],\"filters\":{\"search\":[\"ceylon\",\"weight\"],\"searchType\":\"adCopy\",\"keywordMode\":\"all\",\"sortBy\":\"newest\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"platforms\":[\"facebook\"],\"status\":\"active\"},\"warnings\":[],\"paginationMaxOffset\":10000},\"fieldGuide\":{\"countryBreakdowns\":[{\"path\":\"data[].audience.targetedCountries\",\"meaning\":\"Full ad targeting country list, including non-EU markets such as US.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].audience.mainCountry\",\"meaning\":\"Primary audience country from Meta's EU/UK ad transparency data; null for ads outside that scope, even when the ad targets those markets.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].advertiser.countriesCount\",\"meaning\":\"Number of advertiser countries detected; not a country split.\"}],\"richNestedData\":[{\"path\":\"data[].collationId\",\"meaning\":\"Identifier of the combined creative represented by this search result. Pass it as scan_ad.collation_id to scan the same aggregate instead of only the representative ad.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].advertiser\",\"meaning\":\"Facebook page identity, reach, followers, and live ads.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].content\",\"meaning\":\"Ad copy, transcript fallback, CTA, and landing page.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].media\",\"meaning\":\"Media type, thumbnails, and media URLs.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].metrics\",\"meaning\":\"Reach, spend estimate, deltas, and duplicate metrics.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].audience\",\"meaning\":\"Country, gender, and age targeting fields.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].flags\",\"meaning\":\"EU, low-reach, media-changed, and partner-badge flags.\"}]},\"credits\":{\"requestedUnits\":1,\"effectiveUnits\":1,\"actualUnits\":1,\"costUnits\":2,\"recurringUnits\":2,\"topupUnits\":0,\"creditsRemaining\":9747,\"creditsSourceHeader\":\"recurring\",\"failurePhase\":null,\"releaseAttempted\":false,\"releaseSucceeded\":null}}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019L7NbFA7MVTC5XoUdm43Uq","agent_id":"aabf4cd78d36d527d","session_id":"f17f1593-2f67-4602-83cb-0d79fa14ee5c","uuid":"425f2ccf-751f-4349-b37d-fdee3961c212","timestamp":"2026-11-27T18:50:27.317Z","tool_use_result":{"_meta":{"io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo":{"name":"trendtrack-server","version":"0.0.1"}}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AvnULKxgERvCFj7VBXd9Yu","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Conteos de saturación de ángulos"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HEKQUzRrCZpQtn9ApENg7J","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (51KB). Full output saved to: /Users/kalethabh/.claude/projects/-Users-kalethabh-Anuncios-con-IA/f17f1593-2f67-4602-83cb-0d79fa14ee5c/tool-results/toolu_01HEKQUzRrCZpQtn9ApENg7J.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n{\"requestId\":\"74fc5993-ccbf-43f8-bc06-1e640615f7df\",\"data\":[{\"id\":\"facebook_1970290446996683\",\"collationId\":\"28427328536932802\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-09-26T10:39:37.349Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-09-25T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-16T06:24:00.743Z\",\"daysRunning\":52,\"media\":{\"type\":\"video\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/thumbnails/8190b5219050fc022458ab9a21664b5d00c20436f1143289e1f64c49338cdde8.jpg\",\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/video/1c43880064b86c960b3b7cb246cdd4feb7b2eb47293d69bad4519e2e5481153e.mp4\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1023779307483612\",\"name\":\"Venturo Store\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/1023779307483612.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1023779307483612\",\"liveAdsCount\":0,\"reach30d\":0,\"totalReach\":0,\"countriesCount\":1,\"facebookLikes\":189,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Si tu glucosa en ayunas sigue subiendo cada vez que te haces análisis — aunque ya mejoraste tu alimentación, caminas después de cenar y hasta probaste berberina — no es porque te falte fuerza de voluntad.\\n\\nEs porque tal vez has estado atacando solo la mitad del problema.\\n\\nY esto es algo que a muchos médicos en Estados Unidos nunca les explicaron bien: por qué la glucosa en ayunas puede ser tan difícil de bajar.\\n\\nTu azúcar en la sangre no depende solamente de cuánta azúcar comes.\\n\\nTambién depende de si tus músculos pueden sacar esa azúcar de la sangre y usarla como energía.\\n\\nY cuando ese sistema deja de funcionar bien, no necesariamente por tu dieta, sino porque tus células ya no responden como deberían, cortar más carbohidratos no siempre arregla el número de las 6 de la mañana.\\n\\nPiénsalo así.\\n\\nTu cuerpo tiene como dos controles para la glucosa: una llave de agua y un drenaje.\\n\\nLa llave es tu hígado. Tu hígado produce y libera glucosa en la sangre.\\n\\nEl drenaje son tus músculos. Dentro de tus células musculares hay transportadores llamados GLUT4, que ayuda\n...\n</persisted-output>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BM6ojhxoKRmXtc5hHHY9MK","agent_id":"a93b4961117d83ab3","session_id":"f17f1593-2f67-4602-83cb-0d79fa14ee5c","uuid":"985883c9-5162-4056-aa04-3c14d51a5468","timestamp":"2026-11-27T18:50:27.636Z","tool_use_result":{"_meta":{"io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo":{"name":"trendtrack-server","version":"0.0.1"}}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HEKQUzRrCZpQtn9ApENg7J","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Validación Colombia canela Ceylán"}
