{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L7B3knuSmrkHx2w6MKj48o","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"requestId\":\"da2c7ae3-2653-4f1e-a2ef-d65ac39a910a\",\"data\":[{\"id\":\"facebook_1589877466241155\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-11-26T19:50:45.819Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-11-26T08:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-26T19:51:00.245Z\",\"daysRunning\":1,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/f75170f4ec957eac0297d9acbad3c599f43b8ff70c9119b8c61bd4b96f53adb0.jpg\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"115719964955088\",\"name\":\"Youth Pro\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/115719964955088.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"115719964955088\",\"liveAdsCount\":15,\"reach30d\":63,\"totalReach\":1354,\"countriesCount\":1,\"facebookLikes\":14,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"The day I bought adult diapers at 60, right after my Type 2 diagnosis, the cashier gave me one look that made me want to disappear into the floor...\\n\\nBut what choice did I have? The diarrhea had started. Especially after Metformin. And everyone knows what comes next, right?\\n\\nI was preparing for the inevitable decline. Buying products meant for my 89-year-old mother-in-law who lived in assisted care.\\n\\nExcept here's what nobody tells you: I wasn't fighting the disease. I was just managing the wreckage.\\n\\nMy name is Sandra Keller. I've been an ICU nurse for 38 years. I've held the hands of dying diabetics. I've watched Metformin destroy quality of life for decades. I've seen the trajectory.\\n\\nSo when my own fasting glucose hit 187 and my A1C climbed to 7.2, I knew exactly what my doctor would say before she said it.\\n\\nWe need to start you on Metformin.\\n\\nI smiled. Nodded. Took the prescription.\\n\\nAnd threw it away three days later.\\n\\nNot because I'm reckless. Not because I'm in denial. But because I've seen what Metformin does to people who take it, and I've seen what diabetes does to people who don't. Both paths lead to misery. I was trying to find a third option.\\n\\nFor three years, I tried everything else. Apple cider vinegar before meals. Cinnamon in my coffee. Berberine twice daily. I cut carbs so aggressively I was eating 40 grams a day. I walked 10,000 steps. I did resistance training three times a week.\\n\\nMy fasting glucose dropped from 187 to 170. My A1C went from 7.2 to 6.8.\\n\\nBetter. But not good enough. Not even close.\\n\\nAnd the cost? I was exhausted. Irritable. Craving carbs constantly. My brain felt wrapped in cotton by 2 PM every single day. I'd wake up at 3 AM to pee, check my glucose out of habit. Still 160. Lie there until 4:30 AM unable to fall back asleep.\\n\\nI was doing everything right and still losing.\\n\\nMy husband found me crying in the bathroom one night at 11 PM, staring at my glucose meter. 192. After a dinner of grilled chicken and broccoli.\\n\\nJust take the damn Metformin, he said.\\n\\nI can't, I whispered. I've seen what it does.\\n\\nBut I was running out of options. And the thought that crossed my mind was worse than the diarrhea, worse than the sleepless nights. I'd watched uncontrolled blood sugar work through patients for nearly four decades. The kidneys go first, quietly, while the numbers look manageable. Then the nerves. Then the eyes. I knew exactly where this road ended if I couldn't get the glucose down.\\n\\nThen something happened that changed everything.\\n\\nI was discharging a patient. Type 2 diabetic, 61 years old, admitted three months earlier with a foot ulcer. Standard progression for someone whose blood sugar had been out of control for years.\\n\\nBut this time, something was different.\\n\\nHe looked lighter. Not just physically. His whole energy was different. His skin had that glow people get when inflammation drops. He was cracking jokes with the physical therapist.\\n\\nI pulled up his chart. His fasting glucose that morning: 94. Three months ago at admission: 178.\\n\\nI walked back into his room.\\n\\nMr. Patterson. Your blood sugar. What changed?\\n\\nHe grinned. You're not going to believe me.\\n\\nTry me.\\n\\nI stopped taking Metformin.\\n\\nMy stomach dropped. You stopped your medication?\\n\\nThree months ago. Started taking this Ceylon cinnamon supplement my daughter found. At2a. Took about two weeks and my blood sugar just dropped. After a month I felt better than I had in ten years. Talked to my doctor, she ran labs, said whatever I was doing was working. Weaned me off the Metformin.\\n\\nI stared at him.\\n\\nYou're telling me a cinnamon supplement dropped your fasting glucose by 84 points?\\n\\nI'm telling you I sleep through the night now. My brain works again. I'm not chained to a toilet. And my doctor is thrilled with my numbers.\\n\\nI went home that night and couldn't sleep. Not because of my blood sugar. Because of what he had said.\\n\\nI've been a nurse for 38 years. I don't believe in magic pills. I don't trust testimonials. I trust mechanisms and evidence.\\n\\nSo at 2:47 AM, still awake, I started researching.\\n\\nCeylon cinnamon. The true cinnamon, Cinnamomum verum, grown in Sri Lanka. Not the cheap Cassia variety that fills most supplement shelves. The real thing, used in medicine for centuries and now being studied in clinical trials for metabolic disease.\\n\\nThe first study I found was from Japan. Type 2 diabetics given clinical-dose Ceylon cinnamon extract showed significant improvements in insulin sensitivity and fasting glucose after 12 weeks.\\n\\nThe second was from India. Patients with metabolic syndrome showed reduced oxidative stress markers and improved blood sugar metabolism.\\n\\nThe third was from Italy. Ceylon cinnamon's active compound, cinnamaldehyde, reduced inflammatory cytokines and protected pancreatic beta cells from oxidative damage.\\n\\nI kept reading.\\n\\nThe mechanism made sense. Type 2 diabetes isn't just high blood sugar. It's chronic inflammation and oxidative stress damaging your cells' ability to respond to insulin.\\n\\nMetformin forces your liver to produce less glucose. It's a chemical override. It works, but it doesn't address the cellular damage causing the insulin resistance in the first place.\\n\\nCeylon cinnamon works differently. Cinnamaldehyde activates the same metabolic pathways that exercise activates. It moves glucose into cells without relying on insulin. It shuts down the NLRP3 inflammasome, the master switch behind chronic metabolic inflammation. It reduces the oxidative stress that's blocking your insulin receptors and damaging your blood vessels.\\n\\nIt's not forcing anything. It's removing the interference.\\n\\nI ordered cinnamon capsules that morning. Generic ones from Amazon. $14.99.\\n\\nTook two with breakfast on a Monday. Didn't expect anything.\\n\\nThat afternoon at work, something was different. My usual 2 PM crash, the one where my brain shuts off and I need coffee or I'll fall asleep standing up, didn't happen. I was steady. Clear. Functional.\\n\\nI figured it was placebo effect.\\n\\nThat night, I went to bed at 10:30 PM. I woke up at 6:45 AM. I hadn't woken up once. Not to pee. Not with restless legs. Not with my mind racing about tomorrow's shift.\\n\\nI slept through the night for the first time in 14 months.\\n\\nI tested my fasting glucose. 142. Forty-five points lower than my usual 187.\\n\\nI tested again. Same number.\\n\\nI called my husband into the bathroom. Look at this.\\n\\nThat's good, right?\\n\\nThat's the lowest fasting glucose I've had in two years.\\n\\nDay three: 134. Day five: my brain fog was lifting. I could focus during rounds. I could remember patient details without checking my notes three times. Day seven: sleeping through every night. Fasting glucose consistently between 110 and 120.\\n\\nWeek two: 98.\\n\\nI checked it three times because I didn't believe it. I hadn't seen a fasting glucose below 100 in over two years.\\n\\nThe afternoon crashes were gone. The constant thirst was gone. The 3 AM bathroom trips were gone. The diapers were long gone.\\n\\nI felt like myself again.\\n\\nThree months later, I walked into my doctor's office with a printout of my glucose log.\\n\\nShe stared at it for a long time.\\n\\nYour fasting glucose is consistently in the 90s.\\n\\nYes.\\n\\nYour A1C is 5.8. Down from 7.2.\\n\\nYes.\\n\\nShe looked up at me. What did you do?\\n\\nI didn't take the Metformin.\\n\\nHer face hardened. Sandra.\\n\\nI took Ceylon cinnamon. Concentrated extract, 7,200mg equivalent, suspended in MCT oil. There are studies. I brought them.\\n\\nI handed her a folder with six published papers.\\n\\nShe flipped through them. Read for five minutes. Closed the folder.\\n\\nYour kidney function improved. Your creatinine is 1.1. It was 1.4 three months ago.\\n\\nI nodded.\\n\\nKidney function doesn't improve. Once you hit stage 2, the goal is to slow the decline. Not reverse it. But you reversed it.\\n\\nI know.\\n\\nShe sat back in her chair. Keep doing whatever you're doing.\\n\\nThat was seven months ago.\\n\\nMy fasting glucose is consistently between 90 and 100. My A1C is 5.6. My kidney function is stable in the normal range. I sleep through every single night. The afternoon crashes are gone. The brain fog is gone. My irritability, the thing my husband had learned to just tolerate, is gone.\\n\\nI feel like I did ten years ago.\\n\\nNow here is what I need to tell you, because most people make the same mistake I made first.\\n\\nThe generic capsules I ordered from Amazon moved my numbers by maybe 10 points over two weeks. Noticeable, but not life-changing. Not anywhere close to what happened when I switched to At2a, the product Mr. Patterson had named. The difference between those two results comes down to three things, and understanding them explains why most people take cinnamon for months and see almost nothing.\\n\\nThe first is species. What is sold as cinnamon in most supplements, and in most grocery stores, is Cassia. A completely different plant from Ceylon. Grown in China, not Sri Lanka. Cassia contains up to 250 times more coumarin, a compound linked to liver damage with daily long-term use. Your body processes it differently. The clinical studies showing meaningful blood sugar results were conducted on true Ceylon cinnamon, Cinnamomum verum, not Cassia. Most brands will not tell you which one they are using. Some cannot tell you, because they do not actually know.\\n\\nThe second is dose. Clinical studies showing meaningful blood sugar results used concentrated Ceylon extract equivalent to 6,000 to 7,200mg of whole cinnamon daily. Most capsules contain 500 to 1,500mg of powder. A fraction of what actually moves the numbers. You could take the right species and still see nothing, because the dose is nowhere near therapeutic.\\n\\nThe third is delivery. Cinnamon's active compounds, cinnamaldehyde and the polyphenols, are fat-soluble. They need fat to absorb properly. Dry powder capsules have poor bioavailability. The right carrier is MCT oil from coconuts, which increases absorption dramatically and adds its own independent benefit to insulin sensitivity through a separate pathway.\\n\\nThe generic capsules I tried first had none of these three things right. Wrong species, wrong dose, wrong delivery. That is why they moved my glucose 10 points instead of 87.\\n\\nAt2a gets all three right. True Ceylon cinnamon, Cinnamomum verum, sourced from Sri Lanka. Dosed at 7,200mg equivalent per softgel as a 12:1 concentrated extract. Suspended in MCT oil from coconuts. Third-party tested, GMP-certified, every batch verified for purity, potency, and coumarin levels. One softgel every morning with breakfast. That is the protocol.\\n\\nOne thing I want to be direct about. At2a is not sold on Amazon, Walmart, or any third-party marketplace. I have seen cinnamon supplements on those platforms with Ceylon on the label. Some are Cassia. Some are real Ceylon at doses that will not do anything. Buying directly from the official At2a website is the only way to verify you are getting the authenticated formula, the correct species, the stated dose, the MCT oil delivery, and the tested product. When you are taking something every day to manage your blood sugar, the supply chain matters.\\n\\nThe other thing I tell every patient I recommend this to: give it the full three months.\\n\\nHere is why that matters.\\n\\nThe sleep improvement and the energy shift happen fast. Most people notice within the first week. The fasting glucose drop follows in weeks two through four. But the full result, the A1C shift, the sustained metabolic change, the kind of kidney improvement my doctor said was not supposed to happen, that takes the full 8 to 12 weeks. There is no shortcut to that timeline.\\n\\nThis is why At2a sells a three-month supply. Not as a tactic. Because three months is the clinical window. You are not seeing the full result at 30 days.\\n\\nAnd this is why they back it with a 90-day money-back guarantee. Full 90 days. Open the bag, take it every day, see what happens to your fasting glucose, your sleep, your energy, your mental clarity. If your numbers do not move, send back what is left and get every dollar back. The guarantee is long enough to cover the full result window. That is not an accident.\\n\\nOne practical note. Supply turns over faster than you would expect, because most people who try it order the three-month supply. When that is what people are buying, inventory moves quickly. If it is available when you see this, I would not wait.\\n\\nI have recommended this to six patients now. All six have come back and told me the same thing. Better sleep, usually within the first week. Stable energy throughout the day. Lower fasting glucose within the first month. Improved mental clarity.\\n\\nOne of them cried when she told me she had slept through the night for the first time in two years.\\n\\nAnother patient's A1C dropped from 7.4 to 6.1 in three months. Her doctor told her to keep taking whatever she was taking.\\n\\nThat is not a coincidence. That is a pattern.\\n\\nI went from stage 2 kidney disease back to normal function. From a fasting glucose of 187 to 96. From waking up two to three times every night to sleeping through. From that Walmart checkout line, holding something I never imagined I would need at 60, to feeling like I did a decade ago.\\n\\nIf there is even a chance this could work for you the way it worked for me, it is worth 90 days to find out.\\n\\nSandra K., RN, ICU, 38 years\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"SHOP NOW\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"http://cc.muvost.com/\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"cc.muvost.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg with MCT Oil | AT2A\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"One softgel a day of genuine Sri Lankan Ceylon cinnamon to support healthy blood sugar.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":1,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":0,\"duplicates\":null,\"reachDelta1d\":0,\"reachDelta7d\":0,\"reachDelta30d\":0},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"GB\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":\"all\",\"ageMin\":18,\"ageMax\":65},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":10,\"currentRank\":10,\"rankDelta\":0,\"improvementPct\":66.67},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":true,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}}],\"pagination\":{\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"total\":13407,\"totalPages\":10000,\"offset\":0,\"isTotalApproximate\":true},\"meta\":{\"query\":null,\"keywords\":[\"ceylon\",\"Sri Lanka\"],\"searchIn\":\"ad_copy\",\"categoryIds\":[],\"country\":null,\"countryFilterKind\":\"audienceCountry\",\"adCountries\":null,\"mainCountries\":[],\"trendSignal\":\"reach_growth_7d\",\"sortBy\":\"newest\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"requestedLimit\":1,\"effectiveLimit\":1,\"activeOnly\":true,\"status\":\"active\",\"page\":1,\"minTrafficGrowthPercent\":null,\"trafficGrowthPeriod\":\"last30d\