{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QCg6pMyhY1NNV91Hy5iqKY","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"requestId\":\"622497c2-45ed-4384-92c6-0b39291d31c6\",\"data\":[{\"id\":\"facebook_1411297237491581\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-11-25T09:56:41.312Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-11-25T08:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-26T09:47:47.913Z\",\"daysRunning\":2,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/2b98cb81ee0dc7a4fa2184a567e2e2c8a6009783776f8ff0813e7f1657cbba4f.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1431394073380173\",\"name\":\"Daily Wellness Journal\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/profile_picture/1431394073380173.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1431394073380173\",\"liveAdsCount\":380,\"reach30d\":0,\"totalReach\":0,\"countriesCount\":1,\"facebookLikes\":20,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I was diagnosed with blepharitis at 55. I'm 62 now. Turns out I had only ever been treating half of it.\\n\\nFor seven years, I fought a flare I was never actually reaching.\\n\\nSeven years of lid wipes that stung so bad I dreaded the morning routine.\\n\\nSeven years of warm compresses, hypochlorous sprays, and lid-hygiene logs that ruled my life.\\n\\nSeven years of being handed a wipe and told to learn to live with it.\\n\\nI did everything they asked.\\n\\nI kept a flare log on my phone. Every morning, every night, which eye, how crusted. I switched wipes. I switched foams. I put away the mascara I had worn my whole life in Alabama. I did warm compresses every morning before my shift started, in the dark, before the heat came up.\\n\\nI was a nurse for 38 years. I knew what chronic inflammation did to a body. I had sat with patients whose eyes never stopped weeping. Patients who stopped going out because their lids crusted shut. Patients who were told it was nothing, then watched it come back month after month. I had held those hands. I had watched those faces.\\n\\nI knew exactly how exhausting this road was.\\n\\nSo I did not argue with my eye doctor. I did not look for shortcuts. I did the lid scrubs. I logged every flare. I showed up to every appointment and I did everything she asked.\\n\\nMy flares came back anyway.\\n\\nClear one week. Red and raw the next. Then crusted shut again. Then both eyes at once.\\n\\nAnd the routine was doing things to my mornings that I could not explain to people who had not experienced it. The stinging. The raw rims. I spent the first two hours of every morning working crust off my lashes. Some days I could not see straight until ten. I had worked twelve-hour shifts for three decades. I had never felt anything like this. I planned my entire life around what my eyes would or would not do that day.\\n\\nThen the grit went deeper. By two in the afternoon everything blurred. Not tired the way you get at the end of a long shift. Sandpaper. A different thing entirely. The kind of grit that drops do not fix and rest does not touch.\\n\\nThen the itching started along the lash line. Mild at first. Just at night. I told myself it was my pillow. Then my makeup. Then just getting older.\\n\\nI was a nurse. I knew what that itching meant.\\n\\nI remember sitting in my car in the hospital parking lot one evening, eyes too raw to drive home, thinking: I am doing everything right. I have always done everything right. And I am still losing.\\n\\nMy eye doctor adjusted my routine. We tried a different cleanser. We talked about adding a steroid drop.\\n\\nI did not tell her how frightened I was. I smiled and nodded and filled the new prescription at the Walgreens on the way home.\\n\\nThen something happened that I did not expect.\\n\\nA patient changed everything for me. Not a doctor. Not a specialist. A patient.\\n\\nShe was a retired teacher from Huntsville, seventy-one years old, red-rimmed lids for nine years. She came in for a routine visit and her eyes looked different than they should have. The rims that were always inflamed were calm. The crusting she had described for years had settled in a way it had not in a long time.\\n\\nHer doctor was confused. I was confused. Nobody had changed her lid routine.\\n\\nShe reached into the pocket of her jacket and set something on the table between us. A small sealed bag.\\n\\nI picked it up and read the label.\\n\\nCeylon oregano oil. Black seed oil. Suspended in a lipid softgel. Two softgels a night.\\n\\nI almost set it back down. I had seen patients bring in supplements before. I had smiled politely at a lot of things over 38 years.\\n\\nBut her calm lids were right in front of me. Her face did not lie.\\n\\nI took the bag home and I started reading. Not as a nurse reviewing a patient's choices. As a woman who was frightened about where her own eyes were going and who had run out of other explanations.\\n\\nWhat I found surprised me.\\n\\nBlepharitis is a chronic inflammatory condition, and it travels with the body's internal inflammatory tone, the same one behind rosacea and flaky skin, one that climbs with age and menopause. Not just the stuff at the lid margin you clean with a wipe. A systemic tone. Fed from within. Something nothing I owned could reach.\\n\\nI did not know any of this. After 38 years in medicine, I did not know the difference between what a wipe does on the outside and the inflammation feeding it from the inside. They had told me to keep cleaning. Nobody had asked what was re-lighting it.\\n\\nI had actually tried supplements twice before. Nothing happened either time. I had assumed this was one of those things people hoped would work and didn't. I had been wrong. The idea was not the problem. I had simply never taken the right thing.\\n\\nAnd even that was not the whole answer. What the bag in my patient's pocket had figured out was that the calming compounds, carvacrol in oregano oil and thymoquinone in black seed oil, work systemically, from within, the one place a wipe or a spray physically cannot go. Delivered orally in a lipid softgel, carried through the body, not swiped on the lid.\\n\\nRight target. Right delivery. That was the combination nobody had ever explained to me.\\n\\nI want to be careful here because I am a nurse and I spent four decades watching what happens when people believe things too quickly. So I will tell you exactly what I did.\\n\\nI did not stop my lid hygiene. I did not change anything my eye doctor had recommended. I ordered one bag and I added it to what I was already doing. I wanted to see.\\n\\nThe first week, my eyes held through the afternoon shift in a way they had not in two years.\\n\\nI did not know what to make of it. I kept going.\\n\\nWeek two, the grit was quieter. The afternoon blur that had been part of my day for so long I had stopped mentioning it eased off. Not gone. Quieter. The itching along the lash line was softer at night.\\n\\nWeek three, my mornings started changing in a way they had not changed in four years. Fewer lashes crusted shut.\\n\\nI called my eye doctor at the end of month two and told her.\\n\\nThe flares that used to come back every week or two had stretched out.\\n\\nShe asked what had changed. I told her. She was quiet for a moment. Then she said: whatever you are doing, keep your lid routine and keep doing it.\\n\\nSeven years of watching my flares come right back. First time a doctor had said keep doing it.\\n\\nMonth three, the rims were calmer than they had been in years. The itching was almost gone. My eyes held through full shifts. My mornings had been mine again for a while.\\n\\nI still do lid hygiene. I always will. But I finally understood: I never had a cleaning problem. I had an internal inflammatory tone that nobody was addressing, and a plant combination that nobody in my career had ever taught me to look at seriously.\\n\\nBefore you go looking for this, there is something you need to know.\\n\\nOrgatics is not sold on Amazon. It is not at Walmart. It is not at any third-party retailer. That is not an accident, and it matters more than most people realize.\\n\\nThe oregano supplement market is filled with products that label themselves wild Mediterranean and ship something else entirely. Some of the cheapest sources use oregano of unknown origin and relabel it by the time it reaches any marketplace shelf. There is no way to verify species, carvacrol concentration, or delivery from a third-party listing. None. You are trusting a label with nothing behind it.\\n\\nOrgatics sells only through their official website. That is where the species is verified, the carvacrol concentration is confirmed, the lipid delivery is real, and every batch is tested before it ships. That chain of verification does not exist anywhere else.\\n\\nOne more thing, and this matters.\\n\\nI noticed something in week one. My flares did not stretch out meaningfully until month two. The full change showed up at month three.\\n\\nThat is the honest timeline. That is how long the body takes to respond, how long it takes the internal tone to settle, how long before the relapse loop actually loosens its grip.\\n\\nThat is why Orgatics comes in a three-month supply. Not to sell you more product. Because that is the ritual that gives you an honest result. That is also why their guarantee is 90 days. Not 30. Not 60. Ninety. Because they know what the real timeline is and they are standing behind it.\\n\\nSupply goes quickly because people are buying three months at a time, the way you are supposed to. If it is in stock when you read this, do not put it off.\\n\\nFor seven years I fought the same flare with a routine that made my life smaller. I logged every flare, switched every wipe, did every compress, and watched my eyes crust shut again anyway while the exhaustion took things from me one by one.\\n\\nI am not telling you to stop your lid hygiene. Keep it. I am not telling you what to do at all. I am telling you what happened to me, and what I wish someone had told me seven years ago before the grit went deeper and the mascara went in the drawer and I sat in that parking lot too raw to drive home.\\n\\nThree months from now you will know whether your flares can calm too.\\n\\nSandra Keller, RN\\n38 years\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://store.tryorgatics.com/pages/blepharitis\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"store.tryorgatics.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Oregano 6000mg Equivalent with Black Seed\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Supports a calmer internal inflammatory tone with wild oregano and black seed oil. Shop Orgatics' science-backed softgels. Keep your lid routine.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":null,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":319,\"currentRank\":319,\"rankDelta\":0,\"improvementPct\":83.95},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}}],\"pagination\":{\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"total\":448,\"totalPages\":448,\"offset\":0,\"isTotalApproximate\":false},\"meta\":{\"query\":null,\"keywords\":[\"ceylon\",\"menopause\"],\"searchIn\":\"ad_copy\",\"categoryIds\":[],\"country\":null,\"countryFilterKind\":\"audienceCountry\",\"adCountries\":null,\"mainCountries\":[],\"trendSignal\":\"reach_growth_7d\",\"sortBy\":\"newest\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"requestedLimit\":1,\"effectiveLimit\":1,\"activeOnly\":true,\"status\":\"active\",\"page\":1,\"minTrafficGrowthPercent\":null,\"trafficGrowthPeriod\":\"last30d\",\"trafficGrowth\":null,\"adsGrowth\":null,\"pageReachGrowth\":null,\"adReachGrowth\":null,\"language\":null,\"adLanguages\":[],\"cta\":[],\"currency\":null,\"currencies\":[],\"shopifyAppIds\":[],\"excludeShopifyAppIds\":[],\"pixelIds\":[],\"excludePixelIds\":[],\"themeIds\":[],\"filters\":{\"search\":[\"ceylon\",\"menopause\"],\"searchType\":\"adCopy\",\"keywordMode\":\"all\",\"sortBy\":\"newest\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"platforms\":[\"facebook\"],\"status\":\"active\"},\"warnings\":[],\"paginationMaxOffset\":10000},\"fieldGuide\":{\"countryBreakdowns\":[{\"path\":\"data[].audience.targetedCountries\",\"meaning\":\"Full ad targeting country list, including non-EU markets such as US.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].audience.mainCountry\",\"meaning\":\"Primary audience country from Meta's EU/UK ad transparency data; null for ads outside that scope, even when the ad targets those markets.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].advertiser.countriesCount\",\"meaning\":\"Number of advertiser countries detected; not a country split.\"}],\"richNestedData\":[{\"path\":\"data[].collationId\",\"meaning\":\"Identifier of the combined creative represented by this search result. Pass it as scan_ad.collation_id to scan the same aggregate instead of only the representative ad.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].advertiser\",\"meaning\":\"Facebook page identity, reach, followers, and live ads.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].content\",\"meaning\":\"Ad copy, transcript fallback, CTA, and landing page.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].media\",\"meaning\":\"Media type, thumbnails, and media URLs.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].metrics\",\"meaning\":\"Reach, spend estimate, deltas, and duplicate metrics.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].audience\",\"meaning\":\"Country, gender, and age targeting fields.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].flags\",\"meaning\":\"EU, low-reach, media-changed, and partner-badge flags.\"}]},\"credits\":{\"requestedUnits\":1,\"effectiveUnits\":1,\"actualUnits\":1,\"costUnits\":2,\"recurringUnits\":2,\"topupUnits\":0,\"creditsRemaining\":9739,\"creditsSourceHeader\":\"recurring\",\"failurePhase\":null,\"releaseAttempted\":false,\"releaseSucceeded\":null}}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019L7NbFA7MVTC5XoUdm43Uq","agent_id":"aabf4cd78d36d527d","sessio