{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChTwCHg7c7jfso9GECZXW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011z8Qa1SJ4qeYExPSTzNCNF","name":"mcp__claude_ai_Trendtrack__scan_ad","input":{"collation_id":"1369169025206658"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1745,"cache_read_input_tokens":185432,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1745},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f17f1593-2f67-4602-83cb-0d79fa14ee5c","uuid":"55254e39-aaa7-4615-b657-d8694310c1df","timestamp":"2026-11-27T18:50:34.540Z","request_id":"req_011ChTwCHVCDiGALxqVerKRq","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_011z8Qa1SJ4qeYExPSTzNCNF":{"collation_id":"1369169025206658"}},"tool_use_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011z8Qa1SJ4qeYExPSTzNCNF","display_name":"Scan Ad","server_display_name":"Trendtrack","icon_url":"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=trendtrack.io&sz=64","server_id":"mcpsrv_01KGbiwtxNrjWi8utAMJWN3W","tool_name":"scan_ad"}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LeeDVkprHUUy6y6JLeLi9M","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"requestId\":\"fdb68ae0-832c-4d2e-935a-86b61eda4d8b\",\"data\":[{\"id\":\"facebook_1117369564332572\",\"collationId\":\"1681524076766841\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-11-22T13:46:09.435Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-11-21T08:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-26T13:02:40.277Z\",\"daysRunning\":6,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/22a2936ad417b773ee39e2a856ab6844f55d83030cc110008bd537909da18f48.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1231963813338783\",\"name\":\"Sandra Kellerman, RN\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/profile_picture/1231963813338783.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1231963813338783\",\"liveAdsCount\":1711,\"reach30d\":264457,\"totalReach\":277965,\"countriesCount\":1,\"facebookLikes\":176461,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I'm about to say something that might annoy BIG PHARMA, but after what I discovered about my own psoriasis, I can't keep quiet anymore.\\n\\nI'm Sandra Keller. I've been an ICU nurse for 38 years. I've watched skin tell the story of a disease long before the labs did. I've held the hands of dying diabetics. I've seen the trajectory.\\n\\nI've watched their skin crack, flare, and refuse to heal. I've watched their blood sugar spiral underneath it. I've watched inflammation that started on the surface of the skin turn out to be running through the whole body, because 10 years of uncontrolled glucose and chronic inflammation had been working on them from the inside.\\n\\nSo when the psoriasis on my elbows started spreading, and my own fasting glucose hit 187 with an A1C of 7.2, I knew exactly what was coming.\\n\\nAnd then I noticed something that terrified me more than the numbers themselves.\\n\\nFor two years, I'd been watching my skin change. It started subtly—a rough patch on one elbow, a little flaking at my hairline after a long shift—but by last winter, I couldn't pretend I didn't know what it meant.\\n\\nThick, silvery plaques on both elbows and both knees. Patches on my scalp that left flakes on my navy scrubs. Cracks in the plaques that bled into my sleeves. I'd started wearing a long-sleeve shirt under my scrubs so my patients wouldn't stare.\\n\\nSpreading plaques. That's the signal the inflammation inside you is winning.\\n\\nNot because your skin is weak. Because years of high blood sugar and oxidative stress keep the immune system on alarm, and psoriasis is what that alarm looks like on the skin. I've seen it in patients dozens of times. People with psoriasis are far more likely to have insulin resistance. I knew the trajectory.\\n\\nPsoriasis doesn't just show up on your skin. High blood sugar keeps feeding it from the inside. It triggers chronic oxidative stress that damages your cells' ability to respond to insulin. And that same inflammation keeps telling your skin cells to grow too fast, year after year, while you're walking around thinking it's just a skin problem.\\n\\nIt's not just a skin problem. It's a cellular inflammation problem. And the skin is where you see it first.\\n\\nThick, silvery plaques form because your skin, which normally replaces itself about once a month, is being pushed to do it in a matter of days. The new cells pile up faster than the old ones can shed. That pile is the plaque. And the signal pushing it comes from inflammation that high blood sugar and oxidative stress keep turning up.\\n\\nAnd if you let it go long enough? You're looking at:\\n\\nPlaques that keep spreading—to your arms, your back, your scalp—no matter what you put on them.\\n\\nCracks in the plaques that bleed and can get infected.\\n\\nYour dermatologist adding stronger creams each time, managing the flares instead of addressing the root cause.\\n\\nSteroid creams that thin your skin until it bruises and tears.\\n\\nBiologic injections every few weeks that turn down part of your immune system, for the rest of your life.\\n\\nItching so bad you can't sleep through the night.\\n\\nHiding your elbows, your scalp, your skin, in every season.\\n\\nAnd the constant fear that the next flare is the one that covers you.\\n\\nI wasn't about to wait for that.\\n\\nI refused metformin for 3 years. Then my psoriasis spread to cover about 10 percent of my body, and my dermatologist said, “We're out of creams. It's time for injections.”\\n\\nI knew she was right about where it was heading. I'd seen this before. Without a change, I was heading toward a lifetime of shots. My skin was being inflamed by my own blood sugar.\\n\\nSo I didn't start the injections yet. But I also couldn't ignore the trajectory anymore.\\n\\nFor three years, I'd tried everything else. Steroid cream every night. Coal tar shampoo. Apple cider vinegar before meals. Cinnamon in my coffee. Berberine twice daily. I cut carbs so aggressively I was eating 40 grams a day. I walked 10,000 steps. I did resistance training three times a week.\\n\\nMy plaques barely changed. My fasting glucose dropped from 187 to 170. My A1C went from 7.2 to 6.8.\\n\\nBetter. But not good enough. Not even close.\\n\\nAnd the plaques never stopped spreading. A new patch showed up on my lower back. I was running out of time.\\n\\nMy husband found me crying in the bathroom one night at 11 PM, staring at the blood spots on my scrub sleeves, my glucose meter on the counter. 192. After a dinner of grilled chicken and broccoli.\\n\\n“Just take the damn shots,” he said.\\n\\n“I can't,” I whispered. “I've seen what they do.”\\n\\nBut I was running out of options.\\n\\nThen something happened that changed everything.\\n\\nI was discharging a patient—type 2 diabetic, 61 years old, admitted three months earlier with an infected crack in a psoriasis plaque on his shin. His forearms had been covered in thick, silvery plaques. Standard progression for someone whose blood sugar had been out of control for years.\\n\\nBut this time, something was different.\\n\\nHe looked lighter. Not just physically. His whole energy was different. His skin had that calm look people get when their inflammation drops. He was cracking jokes with the physical therapist.\\n\\nI looked at his forearms. The thick plaques had flattened into faint pink patches.\\n\\nHis shin had healed.\\n\\nI pulled up his chart. His fasting glucose that morning: 94.\\n\\nThree months ago at admission: 178.\\n\\nThe flaking on his scalp was gone.\\n\\nI walked back into his room. “Your skin. Your blood sugar. Everything. What changed?”\\n\\nHe grinned. “You're not going to believe me.”\\n\\n“Try me.”\\n\\n“Started taking this Ceylon cinnamon supplement my daughter found. Metabolae. Took about two weeks and the itching calmed down. After a month the plaques started flattening, and my blood sugar dropped too. I felt better than I had in ten years. Talked to my doctor, she ran labs, said whatever I was doing was working. She's been lowering my metformin.”\\n\\nI stared at him.\\n\\n“You're telling me a cinnamon supplement calmed your psoriasis AND dropped your fasting glucose?”\\n\\n“I'm telling you I sleep through the night now without scratching. I wear short sleeves again. My skin is calming down instead of spreading. And my doctor is thrilled with my numbers.”\\n\\nI went home that night and couldn't sleep. Not because of my skin. Because of what he'd said.\\n\\nI've been a nurse for 38 years. I don't believe in magic pills. I don't trust testimonials. I trust mechanisms and evidence.\\n\\nSo at 2:47 AM, still awake, I started researching.\\n\\nI already knew psoriasis and insulin resistance travel together. What I didn't know was that something could work on both.\\n\\nCeylon cinnamon. The true cinnamon—Cinnamomum verum—grown in Sri Lanka. Not the cheap Cassia variety that fills most supplement shelves. The real thing, used in medicine for centuries and now being studied in clinical trials for metabolic disease.\\n\\nThe first study I found was from Japan. Type 2 diabetics given clinical-dose Ceylon cinnamon extract showed significant improvements in insulin sensitivity and fasting glucose after 12 weeks.\\n\\nThe second study was from India. Patients with metabolic syndrome showed reduced oxidative stress markers and improved blood sugar metabolism.\\n\\nThe third was from Italy. Ceylon cinnamon's active compound—cinnamaldehyde—reduced inflammatory cytokines and protected pancreatic beta cells from oxidative damage.\\n\\nI kept reading.\\n\\nThe mechanism made sense. Psoriasis isn't just a skin condition, and type 2 diabetes isn't just high blood sugar. Both run on chronic inflammation and oxidative stress, the same forces that damage your cells' ability to respond to insulin.\\n\\nBut here's what matters for the skin: that same inflammation is what keeps telling your skin cells to pile up. That's where the plaques come from. That's why they keep spreading.\\n\\nSteroid creams calm the top layer of your skin for a while. Biologic injections block one signal in your immune system. Metformin forces your liver to produce less glucose. They're overrides. They can help, but none of them addresses the inflammation and insulin resistance underneath in the first place. And none of them rebuilds skin that has been inflamed for years.\\n\\nCeylon cinnamon works differently. Cinnamaldehyde—its primary active compound—helps calm the inflammatory signals that keep skin flaring. In research, it activates the same metabolic pathways that exercise activates and helps move glucose into cells. It helps quiet the NLRP3 inflammasome—a master switch behind chronic inflammation. It reduces the oxidative stress that's irritating your skin, blocking your insulin receptors, and damaging your blood vessels.\\n\\nIt's not forcing anything. It's removing the interference.\\n\\nAnd that matters for psoriasis too. Plaques aren't random. They're inflammation, fed by high blood sugar and oxidative stress, year after year. Reduce the inflammation. Reduce the blood sugar. Give the skin a chance to settle.\\n\\nI ordered a bag that morning. The same Ceylon cinnamon extract my patient had mentioned. Metabolae. 7,200mg equivalent, suspended in MCT oil.\\n\\nTook one with breakfast on a Monday. Didn't expect anything.\\n\\nThat first afternoon my skin looked exactly the same. But my usual 2 PM crash—the one where my brain shuts off and I need coffee or I'll fall asleep standing up—didn't happen.\\n\\nI figured it was placebo effect.\\n\\nThat night, I went to bed at 10:30 PM.\\n\\nI woke up at 6:45 AM.\\n\\nI hadn't woken up once to scratch my elbows. Not with my mind racing about tomorrow's shift.\\n\\nI slept through the night for the first time in 14 months.\\n\\nI tested my fasting glucose. 142.\\n\\n45 points lower than my usual 187.\\n\\nI tested again. Same number.\\n\\nDay three: the itch on my scalp had eased. Fasting glucose 134.\\n\\nDay five: fewer flakes on my navy scrubs. My brain fog was lifting. I could focus during rounds.\\n\\nDay seven: I was sleeping through every night without scratching. My fasting glucose was consistently between 110 and 120.\\n\\nWeek two: the plaques on my elbows were less red, and the edges looked softer. Fasting glucose 98.\\n\\nI checked it three times because I didn't believe it. I hadn't seen a fasting glucose below 100 in over two years.\\n\\nBut here's what really got my attention.\\n\\nWeek three: I pulled off my scrub top one evening and realized something was different.\\n\\nNo blood on my sleeves. The cracks in my plaques had stopped bleeding. For the first time in two years, my sleeves were clean.\\n\\nWeek four: I took photos of my elbows and knees and held them up next to the ones from the month before.\\n\\nThe plaques were flatter. Thinner. Smaller.\\n\\nMy skin was starting to settle.\\n\\nThree months later, I walked into my dermatologist's office with my photos, and my glucose log in the same folder.\\n\\nShe stared at my arms for a long time.\\n\\n“Your psoriasis was covering about 10 percent of your body.”\\n\\n“Yes.”\\n\\n“It's about 4 percent now.”\\n\\n“Yes.”\\n\\n“And your A1C...” She looked at the labs my doctor had sent over. “Your A1C is 6.3. It was 7.2.”\\n\\n“Yes.”\\n\\nHer face hardened. “Sandra—”\\n\\n“I didn't start the injections. I took Ceylon cinnamon. Concentrated extract, 7,200mg equivalent, suspended in MCT oil. There are studies. I brought them.”\\n\\nI handed her a folder with six published papers.\\n\\nShe flipped through them. Read for five minutes. Closed the folder.\\n\\n“Your psoriasis improved. Psoriasis at this stage doesn't usually calm down like this without stepping up treatment. But yours did.”\\n\\n“I know.”\\n\\n“How is that possible?”\\n\\n“Because I went after the root cause. The inflammation. The blood sugar feeding it. Once you turn the inflammation down, skin can settle.”\\n\\nShe sat back in her chair. “Let's hold off on the injections. Keep doing whatever you're doing.”\\n\\nThat was seven months ago.\\n\\nMy psoriasis is down to a few faint patches on my elbows and one small spot on my scalp. My A1C is 5.6. My fasting glucose is consistently between 90 and 100.\\n\\nThe bleeding cracks are completely gone.\\n\\nI sleep through every single night without scratching. I wear short-sleeve scrubs again. The afternoon crashes are gone. The brain fog is gone.\\n\\nI feel like I did ten years ago.\\n\\nI've recommended Ceylon cinnamon to six patients now. All six have come back and told me the same thing: better sleep, usually within the first week. Less itching. Lower fasting glucose within the first month.\\n\\nAnd three of them had psoriasis. All three came back months later and said the same thing: their plaques were flatter. They were flaking less. The cracks had stopped bleeding.\\n\\nOne patient cried when she told me she'd worn a sleeveless dress to her granddaughter's wedding. The first time in five years she'd shown her arms. She thought she was headed toward injections.\\n\\nAnother patient's A1C dropped from 7.4 to 6.1 in three months. His psoriasis went from covering 8 percent of his body to 3. His dermatologist told him to keep taking whatever he was taking because she'd rarely seen plaques settle like that.\\n\\nBut here's what I need you to understand.\\n\\nI tried cheap cinnamon first. Generic capsules from Amazon. After two weeks my plaques looked exactly the same. My blood sugar had dropped maybe 10 points. Better than nothing, but not life-changing.\\n\\nMost cinnamon supplements fail for the same reasons:\\n\\nThey're not real Ceylon. What's sold as “cinnamon” in most supplements—and most grocery stores—is Cassia, a completely different species grown in China. Cassia contains up to 250 times more coumarin, a compound linked to liver damage with daily use. Your body processes it differently. The clinical benefits found in studies were for true Ceylon cinnamon—Cinnamomum verum—not Cassia. Most brands won't even tell you which one they're using.\\n\\nThey're underdosed. Clinical studies showing meaningful blood sugar benefits used concentrated Ceylon extract equivalent to 6,000–7,200mg of whole cinnamon daily. Most capsules contain 500–1,500mg of powder. A fraction of what actually moves the numbers. Not enough to calm the inflammation driving your plaques.\\n\\nThey use the wrong delivery format. Cinnamon's active compounds—cinnamaldehyde and the polyphenols—are fat-soluble. They need fat to absorb properly. Dry powder capsules have poor bioavailability. The right carrier is MCT oil from coconuts, which increases absorption dramatically.\\n\\nThey're not tested. Most supplements aren't third-party tested. You have no idea if you're getting real Ceylon or mislabeled Cassia. No way to verify the dose. No certificate of analysis.\\n\\nThe Ceylon cinnamon that actually worked for me—the one my patient told me about, the one I switched to after the generic barely moved the needle—is Metabolae.\\n\\nIt's true Ceylon cinnamon. Cinnamomum verum, sourced from Sri Lanka. DNA-verified, not just labeled.\\n\\nIt's dosed at 7,200mg equivalent per softgel. A 12:1 concentrated extract. The amount that corresponds to clinical benefit—not a token dose buried in a capsule.\\n\\nIt's suspended in MCT oil from coconuts. The bioavailability is dramatically higher than dry cinnamon powder. And MCT oil independently improves insulin sensitivity through its own pathway.\\n\\nIt's third-party tested. GMP-certified. Every batch verified for purity, potency, and—critically—coumarin levels. Confirmed safe for daily long-term use in a way Cassia never could be.\\n\\nIt's not cheap. But after trying the cheap version and watching it barely work, I understand why.\\n\\nYou're either getting real Ceylon cinnamon at a clinical dose with proper absorption, or you're taking ground Cassia from China that could stress your liver and won't calm your skin.\\n\\nThe difference between the generic I tried first and Metabolae was the difference between plaques that kept spreading and plaques that finally flattened. Between bloody sleeves after every shift and clean ones. Between my fasting glucose dropping 10 points and dropping 87 points.\\n\\nSame ingredient category. Completely different product. Completely different results.\\n\\nI'm not anti-medication. If your psoriasis treatment or your diabetes medication is working for you and you're not suffering, keep taking it. Don't stop anything on your own. And if a patch spreads fast, cracks open, or looks infected, get it seen.\\n\\nBut if you're like me—if your plaques keep spreading no matter what you put on them, if your numbers aren't improving, if you're being pushed toward injections you don't want, if you're desperate for something that actually works without destroying your quality of life—this is worth trying.\\n\\nMy patient told me about it. I tried it. It worked. I told six patients. It worked for all six. Three of them had psoriasis. All three saw their plaques settle.\\n\\nThat's not a coincidence. That's a pattern.\\n\\nI'm sharing this because I hope it can help someone else the way it helped me.\\n\\nHere's where I got mine, Metabolae: true Ceylon cinnamon, 7,200mg equivalent, suspended in MCT oil.\\n\\nI just ordered three more bags last week because they run promotions periodically and I'm not going back to waking up at 3 AM scratching, hiding my arms under long sleeves, or watching my skin flare while my blood sugar stays out of control.\\n\\nP.S. A few things I wish I'd known before ordering:\\n\\n1. They offer a 90-day money-back guarantee. No questions asked. If your skin doesn't improve, if your fasting glucose doesn't drop, if you see no difference, you pay nothing.\\n\\n2. Take one softgel daily with breakfast. Each softgel delivers 7,200mg equivalent of concentrated Ceylon extract in MCT oil. Don't substitute with generic cinnamon capsules—the dose and absorption are not comparable.\\n\\n3. Timeline: Most people notice less itching and better sleep within 3–7 days. Less redness and flaking around week two. Blood sugar drops start showing up between weeks 2–4. Full benefits—flatter, smaller plaques and the A1C improvement—take 8–12 weeks. Skin can take longer to fully settle.\\n\\n4. They sell out. It's a small company. They're getting more popular. If it shows “Out of Stock,” sign up for the notification. If it's in stock, I'd order now before it's gone.\\n\\n5. There's a promotion running right now. I got three bags at a significant discount. If you're going to try it, now's the time.\\n\\nIf you're dealing with:\\n\\nPsoriasis plaques that keep spreading no matter what you try\\nItching that wakes you up at night\\nCracks in your plaques that bleed onto your clothes\\nFlakes on your dark clothes and in your hair\\nHiding your elbows, knees, or scalp\\nBeing pushed toward injections you don't want\\nHigh blood sugar that won't come down\\nFear that the next flare will cover you\\n\\nAnd especially if your creams have stopped working, or you're refusing injections like I did, or you're watching your plaques spread while your doctor just adds more prescriptions...\\n\\nTry this. Give it 90 days. See what happens to your plaques. See what happens to your itching and your sleep. See what happens to your fasting blood sugar.\\n\\nIf it doesn't work, you get your money back. But if it works like it did for me, for my patient, for the six patients I've recommended it to—three of whom saw their psoriasis settle...\\n\\nYou'll wonder why you didn't try it sooner.\\n\\nI went from plaques covering 10 percent of my body to a few faint patches. From bloody sleeves after every shift to clean ones. From waking up scratching every night to sleeping through. From a fasting glucose of 187 to 96. From hiding my arms to short sleeves again.\\n\\nIf there's even a chance this could work for you, it's worth trying.\\n\\n~ Sandra Keller, RN, ICU, 38 years\\n\\nP.P.S. My patient who originally told me about this had to try three different brands before he found Metabolae. He wasted money on cheap capsules while his plaques kept spreading. Most weren't even real Ceylon—they were Cassia, a different species with up to 250 times more coumarin, linked to liver damage. And even the real Ceylon ones came in dry powder form that barely absorbs without a fat carrier. Wrong species, wrong format, wrong results. And because his skin was still inflamed and his blood sugar was still out of control, the crack on his shin got infected.\\n\\nOnce he switched to Metabolae, his plaques started flattening and his blood sugar came down. That's when his dermatologist was shocked.\\n\\nDon't make the same mistake. If you're going to try Ceylon cinnamon, get the version that actually works: true Ceylon source, 7,200mg clinical dose, MCT oil suspension, third-party tested. That's Metabolae. That's the only one worth taking. Especially if your skin depends on it.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/lp-ceylon-cinnamon-insulin-1\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Most Cinnamon Supplements Are Useless\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Supports healthy blood sugar levels and metabolism with premium Ceylon cinnamon. Shop Metabolae's science-backed supplements. Free shipping available.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":1,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":1100,\"currentRank\":1100,\"rankDelta\":207,\"improvementPct\":64.29},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}}],\"pagination\":{\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"total\":24,\"totalPages\":24,\"offset\":0,\"isTotalApproximate\":false},\"meta\":{\"query\":null,\"keywords\":[\"ceylon\",\"big pharma\"],\"searchIn\":\"ad_copy\",\"categoryIds\":[],\"country\":null,\"countryFilterKind\":\"audienceCountry\",\"adCountries\":null,\"mainCountries\":[],\"trendSignal\":\"reach_growth_7d\",\"sortBy\":\"newest\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"requestedLimit\":1,\"effectiveLimit\":1,\"activeOnly\":true,\"status\":\"active\",\"page\":1,\"minTrafficGrowthPercent\":null,\"trafficGrowthPeriod\":\"last30d\",\"trafficGrowth\":null,\"adsGrowth\":null,\"pageReachGrowth\":null,\"adReachGrowth\":null,\"language\":null,\"adLanguages\":[],\"cta\":[],\"currency\":null,\"currencies\":[],\"shopifyAppIds\":[],\"excludeShopifyAppIds\":[],\"pixelIds\":[],\"excludePixelIds\":[],\"themeIds\":[],\"filters\":{\"search\":[\"ceylon\",\"big pharma\"],\"searchType\":\"adCopy\",\"keywordMode\":\"all\",\"sortBy\":\"newest\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"platforms\":[\"facebook\"],\"sta