{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EEuac5Ak478Rh9CowCdzQm","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"requestId\":\"390182fb-6cc4-4028-b5aa-752fde0cf8ea\",\"data\":[{\"id\":\"facebook_1672629947744427\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"unknown\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-11-27T12:04:06.092Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-11-27T08:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":null,\"daysRunning\":0,\"media\":{\"type\":\"video\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/thumbnails/cc191a2486fce5a4288ff0028af6681fb05b9bd92e6781aa85f47bdf237a3592.jpg\",\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/video/dbd0e0a97099a1769eb1deb28d87517e0974801497b14cebd733c6030458ce35.mp4\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"958558520684789\",\"name\":\"John Tucker\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/958558520684789.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"958558520684789\",\"liveAdsCount\":1333,\"reach30d\":492752,\"totalReach\":1373110,\"countriesCount\":null,\"facebookLikes\":4388,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Metabolae Sri Lankan Ceylon Cinnamon is one of nature’s most treasured, time-tested ingredients for supporting healthy blood sugar levels.\\n\\nSourced from Sri Lanka, where generations of cultivation and ideal tropical growing conditions produce some of the world's finest true Ceylon cinnamon.\\n\\nMade with authentic Cinnamomum verum, concentrated into a powerful 12:1 extract, and blended with premium MCT oil for enhanced absorption.\\n\\nNo shortcuts. No unnecessary fillers. Just exceptional Ceylon cinnamon, crafted to support healthy glucose metabolism, balanced blood sugar, and everyday wellness.\\n\\nA premium daily ritual for those who refuse to compromise on their health.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/products/softgels-ceylon\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":null},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":null,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":null,\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":null,\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1489188693346430\",\"collationId\":\"1751400423226375\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"unknown\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-11-27T12:04:06.092Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-11-27T08:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":null,\"daysRunning\":0,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/eb541fa926d53b330cdb3353668ce3919ba186812ab28f8938993f1712b3a566.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"958558520684789\",\"name\":\"John Tucker\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/958558520684789.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"958558520684789\",\"liveAdsCount\":1333,\"reach30d\":492752,\"totalReach\":1373110,\"countriesCount\":null,\"facebookLikes\":4388,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Please don't buy Metabolae Ceylon Cinnamon softgels. They said it would improve my blood sugar levels, but they did not warn me how fast it would work.\\n\\nI'm John Tucker. Certified diabetes educator. Thirty-one years. I've sat across from thousands of patients and told them what to expect, what to track, what to change. Then last year, I got my own diagnosis. My A1C was 6.5. The wound on my foot that stayed open for weeks had turned into a foot ulcer. Red, scaly plaques appeared on my elbows. My skin kept being dry and itchy no matter what moisturizer I used. My wife Jane saw my face and knew.\\n\\nDay 1: I took one softgel with breakfast. I didn't expect anything. In thirty-one years I'd watched patients try every cinnamon on the shelf. Walgreens brand. Costco brand. The powder from the health food store. I could name the patients, the brands, and the disappointments. None of it moved their numbers and their skin remained unchanged. But Jane had read about this one. Metabolae. Ceylon cinnamon, not Cassia. Concentrated 12:1 and suspended in MCT oil so the fat-soluble compounds actually absorb. I said fine. I'd told patients for years to try anything with a guarantee behind it. And this one came with a guarantee.\\n\\nDay 3: I tested my fasting glucose the way I'd taught patients to for three decades. 128. I'd been waking up in the 140s since diagnosis. Tested again. 126. I didn't tell Jane. I wasn't going to build a story on three days.\\n\\nDay 5: Fasting glucose 121. Three mornings in a row under 130. In thirty-one years of coaching patients I had never seen numbers move that fast on a supplement.\\n\\nDay 7: The itching on my skin became less frequent. My skin felt less tight and dry. Some of the loose scalings began to disappear.\\n\\nDay 10: New, healthy tissue had started forming in my foot ulcer. My fasting glucose was 118. I stopped dreading my morning finger prick. For a year, testing my blood sugar had felt like grading myself on a test I couldn't pass. Now I actually wanted to see the number. Jane noticed I wasn't quiet at breakfast anymore. But I knew she hesitated to ask.\\n\\nDay 14: I noticed there were fewer white flakes. The plaque surface had become smoother. The itching on my skin became occasional rather than constant. I stopped waking up in the middle of the night from the pain caused by my ulcer.\\n\\nDay 17: Some of the scaling had substantially reduced. The redness around my ulcer was gone. New tissue kept forming in my ulcer.\\n\\nDay 21: The red patches on my elbows had become lighter. The surrounding skin looked healthier and less flaky. I checked my fasting glucose and it was 111. I tested twice because I didn't believe it. 111 and 110. Jane was watching me from the doorway. She'd been carrying the fear for a year. I could see the weight coming off her shoulders.\\n\\nDay 24: The itching on my dry skin had stopped. I ate pasta for dinner. Real pasta. With bread. I hadn't done that since my diagnosis. Tested two hours later expecting to pay for it. 132. That used to be my fasting number... And now, it was my after-pasta number.\\n\\nDay 27: The plaques had become noticeably flatter. The redness became less intense. Most of the thick scales had disappeared. My foot ulcer was smaller and shallower. I slept through every night that week. No lying in the dark running my numbers through my head. Just sleep.\\n\\nDay 30: Most of my plaques were nearly flat, with only small areas of residual scaling remain. My fasting glucose was 102. I stared at the meter for a long time. 102. A year ago, I was 148. Six months ago, my doctor said if my A1C hit 7.0 we'd start talking about metformin.\\n\\n102.\\n\\nJane came in and saw my face. What's wrong?\\n\\nI turned the meter around.\\n\\nHer eyes filled up. She hugged me and I hugged her back. Thank you, I whispered in her ear.\\n\\nSix weeks after I started, I went back for bloodwork. A1C came back at 5.6. Down from 6.5.\\n\\nMy doctor pulled up my chart. Looked at the trend. Looked at me.\\n\\nHe examined my skin too.\\n\\nWhat did you change?\\n\\nFor thirty-one years I'd been the one asking that question. Now I was the one answering it.\\n\\nI told him about Metabolae. About the true Ceylon. About the MCT oil and the concentration and how none of the ones I'd recommended to patients over the years ever hit the dose the studies actually used.\\n\\nHe wrote something in the chart. Then he said something I didn't expect.\\n\\nBetween us, I can't officially recommend a supplement. But I've had three patients this year come back with numbers like yours. Whatever you're doing, keep doing it.\\n\\nThat was three months ago.\\n\\nMy last A1C was 5.3. My fasting glucose runs between 98 and 106 most mornings. The foot ulcer had completely closed. The red, scaly plaques on my elbows had cleared. My skin isn't dry and itchy anymore. I eat pasta again. I have dessert on the weekend. I don't grade myself on my meter anymore.\\n\\nI'm not prediabetic anymore.\\n\\nHere's what thirty-one years of teaching patients finally taught me:\\n\\nMost cinnamon supplements don't work because they aren't real Ceylon. They're Cassia. A different species with up to 250 times more coumarin, a compound linked to liver damage over time. The clinical studies that actually moved blood sugar used Cinnamomum verum. True Ceylon.\\n\\nMost are underdosed. The studies used the equivalent of 6,000 to 7,200mg daily. Most capsules on the shelf have 500 to 1,500mg of ground powder.\\n\\nAnd most use dry powder that doesn't absorb. Cinnamon's active compounds are fat-soluble. Without fat, they pass right through you. That's why Metabolae suspends the 12:1 extract in MCT oil.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon. 7,200mg equivalent. MCT oil. GMP-certified. Third-party tested. Coumarin-free.\\n\\nIt isn't cheap. But it's cheaper than Metformin. Cheaper than the wound care specialist meds my patients ended up on. Cheaper than the fear my wife carried for a year while she waited for my numbers to start improving.\\n\\n90-day money-back guarantee. If your numbers don't move, you pay nothing. No hassle. No paperwork.\\n\\nOne softgel with breakfast. That's all I did.\\n\\nMy doctor gave me until 7.0. Six weeks later I was in the normal range.\\n\\nIf your A1C is creeping up. If you dread the next lab draw. If you've tried other cinnamons and they didn't work. If your doctor is starting to use words like Metformin and if this continues.\\n\\nTry this. Track your fasting glucose every morning. Give it 30 days.\\n\\nI went from 6.5 to 5.3. From prediabetic to normal. From the one giving the advice to the one taking it.\\n\\nJane doesn't watch what I eat at dinner anymore. My doctor stopped mentioning Metformin. I sleep through the night.\\n\\nThat's what 30 days did for me.\\n\\n~ John Tucker\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/lp-ceylon-cinnamon-insulin-1\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Your Natural Daily Reset\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Supports healthy blood sugar levels and metabolism with premium Ceylon cinnamon. Shop Metabolae's science-backed supplements. Free shipping available.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":3,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":null,\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":\"all\",\"ageMin\":18,\"ageMax\":65},\"rank\":null,\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":true,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_3924593594345101\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"unknown\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-11-27T12:04:06.092Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-11-27T08:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":null,\"daysRunning\":0,\"media\":{\"type\":\"video\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/thumbnails/b15bd682ac54ca6db15f4a0f831b247c8d35c4db2eaa399101a8b796e8ec4f2e.jpg\",\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/video/bfba5cfc311256dd6acf834d3653e98abecc43732d1501cdf7aab0c336b07ce6.mp4\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"958558520684789\",\"name\":\"John Tucker\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/958558520684789.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"958558520684789\",\"liveAdsCount\":1333,\"reach30d\":492752,\"totalReach\":1373110,\"countriesCount\":null,\"facebookLikes\":4388,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Looking to Support Healthy Blood Sugar Naturally? 🌿\\n\\nExperience the Power of Metabolae Sri Lankan Ceylon Cinnamon!\\n\\n✅ Helps support healthy blood sugar levels\\n✅ Supports steady daily energy\\n✅ Helps reduce blood sugar crashes\\n✅ Authentic Sri Lankan Ceylon cinnamon\\n✅ 200+ Clinical studies\\n✅ Enhanced with MCT oil for absorption\\n\\nSupport healthier glucose metabolism naturally with research-backed ingredients designed for daily stability.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/products/softgels-ceylon-standard\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":null},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":null,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":null,\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":null,\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_4750023455265322\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"unknown\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-11-27T12:04:07.380Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-11-27T08:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":null,\"daysRunning\":0,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/b3ae2ad9a1fdf68be9a523d8f4cb2d8a67f35bfcf5e49585d810eadc170fdb64.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"958558520684789\",\"name\":\"John Tucker\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/958558520684789.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"958558520684789\",\"liveAdsCount\":1333,\"reach30d\":492752,\"totalReach\":1373110,\"countriesCount\":null,\"facebookLikes\":4388,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"For three years, my dad kept pretending the ache under his ribs was nothing.\\n\\nIf only he'd heard this Sri Lankan woman I met, he'd still be alive.\\n\\nShe said the four warning signs someone with fatty liver must watch out for, and nobody is talking about the last two. Nor talking about what is actually happening inside the liver.\\n\\nBut first, let me tell you about what happened to my dad.\\n\\nMy name is John Tucker. I'm 57 years old.\\n\\nMy dad died at 63. Not from a heart attack. Not from cancer. From complications after his liver finally stopped compensating for what had been building inside it for over a decade. His doctors had been monitoring his enzymes for years. Elevated, they said. Worth watching. We'll retest in three months. He kept going back. The numbers kept climbing. And then one day they stopped climbing because his liver had stopped trying to keep up.\\n\\nHe went from a man who mowed his own lawn every Saturday to a man who could not get out of a chair without help. In less than two years.\\n\\nI was 53 when I sat in that funeral home. And even then, standing in that line of people shaking my hand, I could feel the dull ache under my own right ribs that I had been ignoring for two years.\\n\\nI told myself it was nothing. Same thing my dad had told himself.\\n\\nHere is the thing about watching someone die from something slow. You tell yourself you would catch it earlier. You tell yourself you would do something different. You tell yourself you would not wait.\\n\\nThen you feel the same ache in the same place and you tell yourself it is nothing.\\n\\nBy 55, I had a full picture of what was happening in my body.\\n\\nA1C sitting at 6.9. Fasting glucose in the 130s. Liver enzymes elevated, ALT and AST both flagged on three consecutive panels over three straight years. My belly was hard and stuck out no matter what I ate or did not eat. I hit a wall every afternoon around 2 PM. I would get up from the dinner table and need to put my feet up within twenty minutes. My wife thought it was just age catching up. I knew I had seen this before. I had watched someone else go through this, slowly, and I knew exactly where it ended.\\n\\nThen the skin tags started. One on my neck. Two more under my arms within a few months. And at night, I would lie in bed scratching my forearms and shins for no reason I could find. No rash. Nothing visibly wrong. Just an itch that would not stop.\\n\\nI looked up nighttime itching and fatty liver at 1 AM on a Tuesday.\\n\\nI did not sleep again until after four.\\n\\nI went to my doctor the next morning. He pulled up my chart, looked at my enzyme numbers, and said exactly what he always said. Elevated. Worth watching. Let's retest in three months. He handed me a printed sheet about low-fat eating and told me to lay off the fried food. He said we might add something for the blood sugar if the A1C did not come down on its own.\\n\\nI had heard all of it before. My dad had heard all of it before.\\n\\nI started the low-fat diet that week. I went to the pharmacy and picked up milk thistle, labeled liver support right on the front. I took it twice a day for four months. I went back for labs. My ALT was still elevated. My AST was still elevated. My A1C had climbed from 6.9 to 7.1. My fasting glucose was still sitting in the 130s. The ache under my right ribs had not moved.\\n\\nMy doctor added Metformin. Said it would help bring the blood sugar numbers down. It moved the number a little. But the ache stayed. My energy after meals stayed flat. The skin tags on my neck were still there, and my liver enzymes stayed flagged through every panel that followed.\\n\\nI kept going back and getting the same answer. Watch the numbers. Retest in three months.\\n\\nThat was the whole plan. That was always the whole plan.\\n\\nI was 56 and I could see exactly what I was walking toward. My father had been on this same path for eleven years. The monitoring had not saved him. The retest-in-three-months had not saved him. The low-fat diet had not saved him. His doctors had watched his enzymes right up until there was nothing left to watch.\\n\\nI was not going to let that be my story. But I had no idea what else to do.\\n\\nI started reading at night when I could not sleep. Medical journals. Health forums. Posts from people who had been getting the same answers from the same doctors for years without anything changing. And one thread kept appearing that I did not expect.\\n\\nThe title was: My Sri Lankan grandmother is 83. She eats rice every single day. Her liver enzymes have been perfect her whole life. What is she doing that we're not?\\n\\nI almost kept scrolling. Something made me click.\\n\\nThe post was from a man whose grandmother had come to the United States from Sri Lanka decades earlier. She ate rice with every meal. She ate fried food. She had never gone on a low-fat diet in her life and had never taken a supplement from a pharmacy shelf. At 83, her enzyme numbers were clean, her energy was intact, and there was no belly to speak of. Comment after comment piled in. Studies. Names. And one name kept appearing across multiple threads: a woman in a small Sri Lankan community in New Jersey. People said their families had been going to her for years. That she understood something about metabolism that the doctors here never explained.\\n\\nI looked at the clock. 2:17 AM.\\n\\nBy 5:00 AM I was dressed. By noon I was in New Jersey.\\n\\nI did not have an address. Just the name of a town and a description of the street. I drove until I saw a small house with a garden of plants I did not recognize. A tree by the front door I had never seen before. And there was an older woman sitting on the porch, sorting through something in a large wooden bowl in her lap. Small build. Dark hair pulled back with gray running through it. Her hands were moving fast and steady without any stiffness.\\n\\nI parked and walked up.\\n\\nI told her my name. I told her my father had died at 63 from liver complications. That I was 56, that my enzymes had been flagged for three straight years, that my A1C was sitting at 7.1, that I had been following every instruction my doctor had given me for years and nothing had moved. I told her I had found her name on a forum at 2 AM and driven four hours because I was out of better ideas. I told her I was terrified I was going to end up exactly where my father ended up.\\n\\nShe looked at me for a long moment.\\n\\nCome inside, she said.\\n\\nHer kitchen smelled like cinnamon and something I could not name. She poured tea without asking and pulled out a chair across from her.\\n\\nTell me what your doctor says about your liver, she said.\\n\\nI told her. Elevated enzymes. Worth watching. Retest in three months.\\n\\nShe nodded slowly.\\n\\nYour doctor is measuring what happens after the damage, she said. He is not explaining what is causing it. And those two things require very different conversations.\\n\\nShe folded her hands on the table.\\n\\nLet me tell you the four signs your body gives you when your liver is in serious trouble. By the time most people hear this, they realize they have been seeing the signs for years and did not know what they were looking at.\\n\\nShe held up one finger.\\n\\nThe first is the ache under your right ribs. Especially after you eat. Especially when you lie on your right side at night. Most people brush it off for years because it does not feel like a crisis. It feels manageable. It feels like something you can push through.\\n\\nShe looked at me directly.\\n\\nBut an ache that sits under your right ribs after meals, one that has been there for months or years, is almost always your liver telling you it is under stress. When fat fills liver cells over time, the organ enlarges. It presses against the tissue surrounding it. You feel that pressure after every meal, when digestion puts additional demand on it. That ache is not something to manage. It is information your body has been sending you for a long time.\\n\\nShe held up a second finger.\\n\\nThe second is the exhaustion after every meal. The wall you hit every afternoon. The belly that stays hard and sticks out no matter how little you eat.\\n\\nShe leaned forward slightly.\\n\\nYour liver is involved in almost everything that happens after you eat. It processes nutrients, decides what to convert, what to store, what to release into your bloodstream. When fat has packed into your liver cells and they are struggling to keep up, that whole process slows down. The energy from your food does not move cleanly through your body. Your blood sugar spikes, and then it drops hard. Your body has to work far more than it should just to get through a meal. That is the wall you hit at 2 PM. That is why you get up from dinner and need to lie down when you used to get up and go about your evening without thinking twice.\\n\\nShe held up a third finger.\\n\\nThe third is the skin tags. The ones on your neck. The ones under your arms. Most people assume they are just a nuisance that comes with getting older.\\n\\nShe shook her head.\\n\\nThey are not random. Skin tags that appear on the neck and under the arms are one of the oldest signals in medicine that insulin has been chronically elevated for a long time. Your body is growing those tags in response to years of metabolic stress. By the time they appear, the process behind them has been running quietly inside your body for years. That is what most people are never told.\\n\\nShe held up a fourth finger.\\n\\nThe fourth is the itching at night. No rash. No bug bites. No visible reason. Just an itch that finds you at 2 AM when you are lying still and there is nothing left to distract you.\\n\\nShe said this one slowly.\\n\\nYour liver's job includes filtering compounds out of your blood before they reach the rest of your body. When your liver is under chronic stress, it cannot do that job completely. Certain compounds build up. They find your skin. The itching you feel at night is not a skin problem. It is a liver problem showing up on the surface. And by the time it starts, the stress driving it has usually been building for years.\\n\\nShe said, Most people connect one of these signs to aging, another to stress, another to what they ate. They never see that all four are coming from the same place.\\n\\nShe looked at me.\\n\\nNow let me tell you what that place is. Because once you understand it, you will see exactly why everything you have already tried has not worked. And why that is not your fault.\\n\\nYour liver and your blood sugar are not two separate problems. They are the same problem showing up in two different rooms.\\n\\nEvery cell in your body has tiny doorways on its surface. When insulin arrives, it signals those doorways to open so glucose can pass in from your blood and get burned for energy. In a healthy body, this works automatically, with every meal, thousands of times a day.\\n\\nBut in insulin resistance, which is what drives Type 2 and prediabetes, those doorways stop responding the way they should. Insulin arrives and signals them to open. The doors barely move. The glucose has nowhere to go. So it stays in your blood, building up after every meal, hour after hour, day after day.\\n\\nNow here is where your liver comes in. And this is the part nobody explains.\\n\\nYour liver is constantly monitoring your blood. When it senses glucose building up and not being cleared, it pulls some of it in to help. At first it stores that glucose as a kind of reserve energy. But when that reserve fills up, and the glucose keeps coming, the liver switches to a different mode. It starts converting that excess glucose into fat. And that fat does not circulate out of the liver the way it should. It stays there, inside the liver's own cells.\\n\\nThat is how fat fills a liver.\\n\\nNot primarily from eating fat. From the liver converting years of trapped blood sugar into fat because the doorways in your cells stopped opening properly.\\n\\nShe said, Every gram of fat pressing against your liver cells right now was once sugar that had nowhere to go. The low-fat diet your doctor recommended limits what comes in. It does not fix why the doorways stopped responding. The conversion keeps happening every single day, while you eat less fried food and feel like you are finally doing the right thing.\\n\\nShe said, The milk thistle you are taking has a real function. It protects existing liver cells from some of the oxidative stress this process creates. What it cannot do is restore the doorways. The glucose keeps coming. The fat keeps accumulating. You are reducing one consequence of the problem without addressing what is driving it.\\n\\nShe said, Metformin tells your liver to produce less new glucose on its own, and that reduces how much glucose enters your blood overnight. That is a meaningful thing. But Metformin does not restore the doorways in your cells. The glucose that comes in from food still faces the same stiff doors. Your liver is still receiving more than it can properly clear. Your doctor gave you the best tools he has available. The problem is that none of those tools address the doorways. And the doorways are where this starts.\\n\\nI asked her what addresses the doorways.\\n\\nShe stood up and walked to a shelf along the wall. She came back with a small dark jar. Inside were thin, tightly curled pieces of bark. Deep red-brown. Layered like rolled paper. Nothing like anything I had ever seen in a spice aisle.\\n\\nShe said, True Ceylon cinnamon. Cinnamomum verum. It grows in Sri Lanka. My grandmother grew it. The people in my village ate rice and fried food every single day, cooked with this every single day, and did not develop the liver problems I hear about from people who come looking for answers. That is not a coincidence.\\n\\nShe said the difference comes down to one thing before anything else. The plant itself.\\n\\nAlmost every cinnamon supplement sold in America is not Ceylon cinnamon. It is cassia. A completely different species from China. The word cinnamon on the label means nothing if the plant inside is wrong. And if the plant is wrong, everything else is wrong with it.\\n\\nShe said true Ceylon carries two specific compounds that cassia does not carry in meaningful amounts.\\n\\nThe first is called MHCP. It goes directly to those stiff cellular doorways and activates them without waiting for insulin to signal them first. The doors that stopped opening start opening again. Glucose moves out of the blood and into the cells where it gets burned for energy. And when blood glucose starts being cleared properly, the liver stops receiving the continuous signal to convert the excess into fat. The process that has been running quietly for years begins to slow down.\\n\\nThe second is cinnamaldehyde, the compound that gives true cinnamon its smell. Inside your cells, there is a specific inflammatory process that has been keeping those doorways jammed shut for years. Cinnamaldehyde calms that inflammatory process directly. When the inflammation quiets, the doorways start functioning again. Cinnamaldehyde also supports the health of the cells lining your liver's blood vessels, the cells that have been under stress from years of elevated glucose passing through them.\\n\\nMHCP works on the doorways. Cinnamaldehyde works on what has been jamming them. Together, they address what nothing else in the standard protocol reaches.\\n\\nBut she said there is a second problem even with real Ceylon, and this is where most people fail even when they try to do it right.\\n\\nMHCP and cinnamaldehyde are fat-soluble compounds. They need oil to absorb. Without oil, they pass through your digestive system before they can reach your cells. Your body excretes them. Almost every Ceylon capsule on the market is dry powder. You feel nothing because nothing is actually getting through.\\n\\nShe said in Sri Lanka they always cook cinnamon with coconut. Her grandmother never knew the science behind it. She just knew they went together.\\n\\nThe right plant. Delivered in oil. Those two things together are what make the difference.\\n\\nShe said a published trial in Diabetes Care, the American Diabetes Association's own journal, documented fasting glucose reductions of up to 29 percent in Type 2 diabetics taking concentrated Ceylon cinnamon. A 2024 meta-analysis of 28 randomized trials across more than 3,000 patients confirmed the same results.\\n\\nShe said a clinical study on 100 patients with fatty liver found that liver enzymes began dropping and liver size began decreasing within weeks of proper supplementation. Published. Peer-reviewed. Available to anyone willing to look.\\n\\nShe said, American doctors do not recommend this. Not because it does not work. Because there is no prescription pad for it, and the research does not arrive in their office on its own.\\n\\nOver the years she had been looking for a product she could recommend to people who came to her with these same questions. Something that used the real species, at the right dose, delivered in oil. She had checked several. Most failed on at least one of those three. Eventually she found one that passed every standard she had.\\n\\nShe wrote something on a piece of paper from the counter, folded it, and handed it to me.\\n\\nMetabolae.\\n\\nShe said, True Ceylon cinnamon from Sri Lanka. DNA-verified, not just labeled. The actual species is confirmed. Twelve-to-one concentrated extract, equivalent to 7,200 milligrams per softgel. Suspended in MCT oil so the fat-soluble compounds actually absorb. Third-party tested. Coumarin levels confirmed safe for daily use.\\n\\nShe said, One softgel with breakfast. That is the protocol.\\n\\nI ordered three bags before I left her kitchen that afternoon.\\n\\nI want to tell you what happened over the next twelve weeks. Because it is the reason I am writing this.\\n\\nWeek one, nothing dramatic. I took my softgel with breakfast every morning. I kept taking my Metformin. I did not expect anything fast.\\n\\nWeek two, the wall I had been hitting every afternoon started to soften. I got up from dinner on a Thursday and did not need to lie down. I stayed up for a full evening with my wife for the first time in longer than I could clearly remember.\\n\\nWeek three, I checked my fasting glucose three mornings in a row because I did not trust the number I was seeing. 121. 117. 112. I had not seen 112 in four years.\\n\\nWeek four, the ache under my right ribs had pulled back from the constant pressure I had lived with. It was still there, but quieter. My belly felt less hard. My belt went in one notch.\\n\\nWeek six, my wife noticed before I did. She asked me when I had stopped waking up scratching at night. I realized I had not done it in at least two weeks.\\n\\nWeek eight, the energy after meals was different. The 2 PM drop was still there, but it was a dip rather than a wall. I was making it through afternoons without needing to sit down.\\n\\nWeek ten, I went in for my regular panel. Full bloodwork. A1C, fasting glucose, liver enzymes, lipids, kidney function.\\n\\nMy doctor walked in, looked at the screen, and went quiet for a moment.\\n\\nA1C: 6.5. Down from 7.1.\\n\\nFasting glucose: averaging 108 across the two weeks of home testing before my appointment.\\n\\nALT: dropped from elevated into normal range for the first time in three years.\\n\\nAST: same. Normal for the first time in three years.\\n\\nTriglycerides: down 28 points.\\n\\nThe ache under my right ribs was essentially gone by that point.\\n\\nHe asked me what I had changed.\\n\\nI told him about the Sri Lankan woman. The two compounds. The 2024 meta-analysis. The Diabetes Care trial. The mechanism she had walked me through. I talked for twenty minutes. He did not interrupt once.\\n\\nThen he said, John, I have not been trained on any of this. But your liver enzymes are normal for the first time since I've been treating you, and your A1C is the best it has been in years. Whatever you are doing, I am not going to tell you to stop.\\n\\nHe reduced my Metformin at that appointment. He said if the numbers held at six months, we would talk about adjusting further.\\n\\nThat was seven months ago. My A1C at my last panel was 6.4. My liver enzymes have held in normal range through two consecutive panels. The ache is gone. I sleep through the night. I have my afternoons back.\\n\\nI want to be honest with you. This is my experience. The numbers I tracked, the symptoms I had, the timeline it took, all of that is my own. Results may be different for you.\\n\\nWhat I can tell you is that six people in my life have started Metabolae since I told them what happened. Four have reported back. What stood out was not that their experience was identical to mine. It was that each of them described something their doctor had not managed to produce. One man told me his liver enzyme numbers came back normal for the first time in five years. His doctor asked him what he had changed. Another told me the scratching at night had stopped before the end of the first month. He had been dealing with it for three years and assumed it was just something he had to live with.\\n\\nNone of them had anything dramatic happen in week one. That was consistent with what I experienced. This is not a fast claim. It is a gradual one, tracked across ninety days.\\n\\nMetabolae offers a 90-day money-back guarantee. No questions asked. If your numbers do not move, if the ache stays, if nothing changes for you, you send it back and get every dollar returned. There is no risk in finding out whether this addresses what your body actually needs.\\n\\nOne softgel with breakfast. That is the whole protocol.\\n\\nI reorder every month. I will not stop.\\n\\n~ John Tucker, 57\\n\\nP.S. If you have tried cinnamon before and seen nothing happen, there is a specific reason. And it is not that cinnamon does not work.\\n\\nThe cinnamon in every grocery store and in almost every supplement capsule sold in America is not Ceylon cinnamon. It is cassia. A completely different plant from China. Cassia does not carry meaningful concentrations of MHCP, the compound that activates the cellular doorways that stop responding in insulin resistance. And cassia contains high levels of coumarin, a compound that puts direct strain on your liver at daily doses. If you have been taking a cinnamon supplement for liver support and it is cassia, you may be adding stress to the very organ you are trying to help.\\n\\nEven the products legitimately labeled as Ceylon are almost always sold as dry powder capsules. MHCP and cinnamaldehyde are fat-soluble compounds. Without oil, they pass through your digestive system before they can be absorbed. Your body excretes them. You feel nothing because nothing is actually reaching your cells.\\n\\nMetabolae is DNA-verified Ceylon cinnamon from Sri Lanka. Twelve-to-one concentrated extract, equivalent to 7,200 milligrams per softgel. Suspended in MCT oil so the fat-soluble compounds actually absorb. Third-party tested. Coumarin levels verified safe for daily use.\\n\\nIt is not the same product as what is on the drugstore shelf. It is a completely different thing in a completely different form, built specifically so the compounds that matter can reach the cells they need to reach.\\n\\nIf you tried cinnamon before and nothing happened, that is not evidence that this approach does not work. It is evidence that you were given the wrong plant in the wrong form.\\n\\nThat is worth knowing before you decide.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/pre-ins-liver\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Fix The Root Cause\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Supports healthy blood sugar levels and metabolism with premium Ceylon cinnamon. Shop Metabolae's science-backed supplements. Free shipping available.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":null,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":null,\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":null,\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":true,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}}],\"pagination\":{\"page\":1,\"limit\":4,\"total\":40615,\"totalPages\":2500,\"offset\":0,\"isTotalApproximate\":true},\"meta\":{\"query\":\"metabolae.com\",\"keywords\":[],\"searchIn\":\"domain\",\"categoryIds\":[],\"country\":null,\"countryFilterKind\":\"audienceCountry\",\"adCountries\":null,\"mainCountries\":[],\"trendSignal\":\"reach_growth_7d\",\"sortBy\":\"newest\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"requestedLimit\":4,\"effectiveLimit\":4,\"activeOnly\":true,\"status\":\"all\",\"page\":1,\"minTrafficGrowthPercent\":null,\"trafficGrowthPeriod\":\"last30d\",\"trafficGrowth\":null,\"adsGrowth\":null,\"pageReachGrowth\":null,\"adReachGrowth\":null,\"language\":null,\"adLanguages\":[],\"cta\":[],\"currency\":null,\"currencies\":[],\"shopifyAppIds\":[],\"excludeShopifyAppIds\":[],\"pixelIds\":[],\"excludePixelIds\":[],\"themeIds\":[],\"filters\":{\"search\":[\"metabolae.com\"],\"searchType\":\"domain\",\"keywordMode\":\"any\",\"sortBy\":\"newest\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"page\":1,\"limit\":4,\"platforms\":[\"facebook\"],\"status\":\"all\"},\"warnings\":[\"Reported total remains exact, but totalPages is capped by pageable depth (offset<=10000).\"],\"paginationMaxOffset\":10000},\"fieldGuide\":{\"countryBreakdowns\":[{\"path\":\"data[].audience.targetedCountries\",\"meaning\":\"Full ad targeting country list, including non-EU markets such as US.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].audience.mainCountry\",\"meaning\":\"Primary audience country from Meta's EU/UK ad transparency data; null for ads outside that scope, even when the ad targets those markets.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].advertiser.countriesCount\",\"meaning\":\"Number of advertiser countries detected; not a country split.\"}],\"richNestedData\":[{\"path\":\"data[].collationId\",\"meaning\":\"Identifier of the combined creative represented by this search result. Pass it as scan_ad.collation_id to scan the same aggregate instead of only the representative ad.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].advertiser\",\"meaning\":\"Facebook page identity, reach, followers, and live ads.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].content\",\"meaning\":\"Ad copy, transcript fallback, CTA, and landing page.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].media\",\"meaning\":\"Media type, thumbnails, and media URLs.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].metrics\",\"meaning\":\"Reach, spend estimate, deltas, and duplicate metrics.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].audience\",\"meaning\":\"Country, gender, and age targeting fields.\"},{\"path\":\"data[].flags\",\"meaning\":\"EU, low-reach, media-changed, and partner-badge flags.\"}]},\"credits\":{\"requestedUnits\":4,\"effectiveUnits\":4,\"actualUnits\":4,\"costUnits\":6,\"recurringUnits\":6,\"topupUnits\":0,\"creditsRemaining\":9695,\"creditsSourceHeader\":\"recurring\",\"failurePhase\":null,\"releaseAttempted\":false,\"releaseSucceeded\":null}}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018c6j4BBLrReQGZf4cZL9L6","agent_id":"a5dca292aa3337a43","session_id":"f17f1593-2f67-4602-83cb-0d79fa14ee5c","uuid":"80e8c2fa-c16d-4234-9fe2-fd43574f6430","timestamp":"2026-11-27T18:50:40.390Z","tool_use_result":{"_meta":{"io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo":{"name":"trendtrack-server","version":"0.0.1"}}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EEuac5Ak478Rh9CowCdzQm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Extraer hooks del mercado Ceylon"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FgtcUmCjwAWxVhb8LndSjW","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"requestId\":\"0a61fd9c-de3f-4b8b-a417-9c3aaa7526c0\",\"data\":[{\"id\":\"facebook_1474205961228342\",\"collationId\":\"2196202077601005\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-11-02T04:12:34.540Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-10-31T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-26T19:11:51.647Z\",\"daysRunning\":27,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/738839f9e96c2042dd4eeb44bc585aabe4f2d566808956a9a1d3c9419d2b48e1.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1013442788524883\",\"name\":\"Caroline Moore\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/1013442788524883.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1013442788524883\",\"liveAdsCount\":3389,\"reach30d\":1144134,\"totalReach\":2517956,\"countriesCount\":2,\"facebookLikes\":1884,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I need to tell you something that will make you f*cking livid about your doctor.\\n\\nMy name is Caroline. I am 62 years old. I have been a Type 2 diabetic for 15 years. Take Metformin twice a day, every morning and every night.\\n\\nAnd for the last five years, I have had diabetic neuropathy in both feet. The last three years, the burning and the numbness have gotten worse every single month no matter what I did.\\n\\nAnd when I say no matter what I did, I mean every damn thing.\\n\\nGabapentin for almost three years. Made me foggy enough that I would forget conversations my husband and I just had. Gained 18 pounds. Still woke up at 3am with my feet on fire.\\n\\nTried Lyrica next. $380 a month out of pocket. Worse than the Gabapentin. Felt like I was wearing a wet blanket over my brain. Could not think straight in the kitchen.\\n\\nI took B12. I took Alpha Lipoic Acid. I bought one of those expensive advanced nerve formulas off Amazon with four thousand five-star reviews.\\n\\nNothing.\\n\\nI stopped taking my morning walks. Stopped going out to the garden at sunrise. Stopped going to my granddaughter's dance recitals because sitting on those hard auditorium seats felt like sitting on hot coals.\\n\\nYou know what my doctor said? Caroline, this is just how diabetic neuropathy progresses. We can up your Gabapentin and add a second diabetes pill.\\n\\nI damn near walked out of that office.\\n\\nI am about to tell you exactly what they are hiding from you and why you will never hear it in a doctor's office.\\n\\nAnd by the end of this, you are gonna be upset too. Because once you see what is actually going on, you will understand why nothing you have tried has worked, and why the medical system has no real interest in telling you.\\n\\nThere are three things happening right now.\\n\\nOne, your body is screaming at you that something is very wrong.\\n\\nTwo, the medical system is telling you to just accept it and take more pills that fog your head and pack on weight.\\n\\nThree, there is a multibillion-dollar industry that profits every single day you stay in pain.\\n\\nI used to love getting up at 7 in the morning and walking my dog out to the trail behind my house.\\n\\nI used to love spending Saturday mornings in my garden before the sun got too high. I used to love sitting on the porch with a cup of coffee, before anybody else in the house was up.\\n\\nThat is not my life anymore.\\n\\nNow I plan every step around my feet. I check the floor before I take a step because half the time I cannot feel where they are landing. I fell off my own porch step a year ago and broke my wrist. Could not feel I had missed it.\\n\\nMy husband tells me to slow down. My daughter thinks I am just getting old.\\n\\nThey do not know that some nights the burning is so bad I lie there with my feet hanging off the end of the bed because the weight of a sheet feels like sandpaper.\\n\\nNobody knows. That is the worst part.\\n\\nYou look fine standing there. You are shaking hands at church and you look fine. Inside, you are counting down the minutes until you can sit. Until you can get your shoes off. Until the day is over.\\n\\nAnd when you finally get home, you sit in the recliner at 11pm with your feet in a bucket of cold water because that is the only thing that helps anymore.\\n\\nMy mother had diabetes too.\\n\\nShe took Metformin for 22 years. I watched what the disease did to her anyway. First they took two toes off her right foot. Then they took the whole foot. By 67 she was in a wheelchair. She died at 70, two weeks before my daughter's wedding. She never met her great-grandkids.\\n\\nI think about her every single day.\\n\\nI am 62. She started losing feeling in her feet at 58. By 64 she was already missing toes.\\n\\nI know what road I am on.\\n\\nLast Christmas, I was sitting at my kitchen table while the whole family was over. My grandkids were running through the house yelling. My daughter was making a pie. My husband was setting the table.\\n\\nAnd I was sitting. Not because I wanted to. Because I was scared to get up and walk across the kitchen in front of everybody. I had fallen twice that month. I did not want my granddaughter to see me go down.\\n\\nSo I sat there. Smiled. Passed the food. Laughed at the right moments.\\n\\nNobody at that table knew I had been dreading that night for two weeks. Nobody knew I was tracking every step I would have to take from the kitchen to the bathroom and back.\\n\\nI was not there. I was just surviving the room.\\n\\nI was terrified.\\n\\nBecause I had watched what happened to my mother. And I knew I was on the same road.\\n\\nI did everything right. I watched my blood sugar. I took every pill the doctor wrote me. I went to every nerve test, every six months, like clockwork.\\n\\nAnd every six months, the results came back a little worse.\\n\\nA little more nerve damage. A little more numbness creeping up.\\n\\nThen about four months ago, I was up at 2 in the morning because the burning would not let me sleep. I was on my phone, scrolling YouTube, just trying to distract myself.\\n\\nI found a 90-minute interview with a guy named Dale. Former Army medic from Tennessee. He had been Type 2 for over 20 years, had neuropathy bad enough his doctors had told him amputation was probably coming within five years.\\n\\nHe did not get the amputation. Three years later, he is walking three miles a day. Sleeping through the night. Feet warm again.\\n\\nAnd he explained something in that interview I had never heard before.\\n\\nHe said diabetic neuropathy is not really a separate condition. It is what diabetes does to your nerves when the disease keeps running underneath your medication. And it comes from two things happening at the same time, in the same place.\\n\\nThe first is the trapped sugar in your blood. Your cells stopped responding to insulin years ago, so glucose sits in your bloodstream with nowhere to go. Metformin does not pull that sugar out. It just slows your liver from making more. So the existing trapped sugar stays in your blood. And it does not sit there politely. It chemically attacks the proteins inside your nerve fibers. It bonds to them. Hardens them. Inflames them. That is why the signals start firing wrong, burning when nothing is touching you, tingling when there is nothing there. The sugar is grinding your nerves down from the inside.\\n\\nThe second is the small blood vessels that feed those nerves. Same trapped sugar damages those vessels too. They stiffen. They stop delivering oxygen and nutrients. So while your nerves are being chemically attacked, they are also starving. They cannot repair themselves because the supply line bringing them what they need has been cut off.\\n\\nThat is diabetic neuropathy.\\n\\nAnd here is why Gabapentin does not fix any of it.\\n\\nGabapentin does not pull the sugar out. Does not heal the vessels. Does not repair the nerves. All it does is numb the brain so it receives less of the pain signal. The damage is still happening underneath. The nerves are still dying. You just cannot feel it as much.\\n\\nThat is not treatment. That is a blindfold.\\n\\nAnd the same goes for B12, Alpha Lipoic Acid, and every nerve supplement on the market. They support nerve function in general. But they do not pull the trapped sugar out. They do not repair the vessels. They are trying to feed a nerve that is being attacked from two sides at once, and they are not built strong enough to fight either attack.\\n\\nThat is why nothing you have tried has worked.\\n\\nIt was never a vitamin problem. It was never a nutrient problem. It is a two-front attack on your nerves that almost nothing addresses.\\n\\nDale said the only thing he had ever found that did was a compound system inside a spice your grandmother had in her kitchen.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon cinnamon.\\n\\nNot the cinnamon on your spice rack. That stuff is Cassia, a cheap lookalike from China that comes from a completely different tree. It is barely related to true Ceylon chemically. The compounds your nerves actually need are barely present in it. And it contains a compound called coumarin that is hard on your liver in daily doses.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon comes from Sri Lanka. The chemistry is completely different. And inside the bark are three specific compounds that address the two-front attack on diabetic nerves.\\n\\nThe first compound is cinnamaldehyde. It is the compound that gives cinnamon its warmth. But what most people do not know is that cinnamaldehyde activates something called GLUT4 translocation. Think of GLUT4 as the doors on your cells that let sugar in. When you have diabetes, those doors get stuck shut. Insulin knocks, but the doors do not open. Metformin does not open those doors either. Cinnamaldehyde activates those doors through a completely different pathway. The same pathway that exercise uses. The trapped sugar finally moves out of your blood and into your cells where it belongs. The sugar attack on your nerves stops at the source.\\n\\nBut stopping the attack only solves half the problem. The vessels feeding your nerves have already been damaged from years of that same sugar grinding against them, and they are still not delivering. That is what the second compound, the proanthocyanidins concentrated in true Ceylon bark, takes care of. These are powerful antioxidant compounds that reduce the oxidative stress on the small blood vessels your nerves depend on. They support the vessel lining. Blood reaches the nerves again. The starving nerves finally get fed.\\n\\nAnd the third compound is the one that surprised me the most. Ceylon cinnamon contains anti-inflammatory polyphenols that suppress something called NLRP3 inflammasome activation. That is the master switch behind chronic inflammation. While the sugar is being pulled out and the vessels are coming back online, the nerve fibers themselves still need protection from the inflammation that has been building inside them for years. Research published in 2024 in the Journal of Inflammation found that cinnamaldehyde directly inhibits this inflammatory pathway, reducing the chemicals that destroy nerve tissue and supporting the body's own repair process.\\n\\nThree compounds. From one spice. Doing three things no nerve supplement or medication on the market is built to do.\\n\\nDale said your doctor will never tell you about it. You cannot patent a tree.\\n\\nI was skeptical. Everything else had failed me. But I figured, what do I have left to lose? My nerve test results were getting worse every six months. I was already walking the same road my mother did.\\n\\nI went back to that YouTube interview and scrolled through the comments. Hundreds of comments from other diabetic people who had tried cinnamon. And what they said lined up with what I had been suspecting.\\n\\nMost cinnamon supplements are useless. They use Cassia instead of Ceylon. They are processed at high heat that kills the active compounds. They come in dry capsules, and here is the part I did not know, the three compounds in cinnamon are fat-soluble. They do not absorb in a dry capsule. They have to be carried into your bloodstream by the right kind of fat. Without it, they just pass through you.\\n\\nSo even if you buy real Ceylon, in a dry capsule, you are paying for cinnamon that never reaches your nerves.\\n\\nThe brand that kept coming up in the comments was Metabolae.\\n\\nReal Ceylon, DNA-verified for the species. Concentrated 12 to 1. Suspended in organic MCT oil that actually carries the compounds across your gut wall and into your bloodstream where they can do something. Third-party tested. Made in the USA in small batches.\\n\\nThat is the difference. Same spice. Right form. Right delivery.\\n\\nI ordered a bag.\\n\\nOne softgel with breakfast. That is it.\\n\\nWeek 1. I slept five hours straight before the burning woke me up. Five hours. I had not done that in three years.\\n\\nWeek 2. The numbness in my left big toe, gone for almost two years, started feeling like something again. Not normal. But not nothing. A tingling. A warmth. Something that was not there yesterday was there today.\\n\\nI sat on the edge of my bed at six in the morning and put my fingers on my own toe and felt it back. I did not tell anybody. I just sat there.\\n\\nWeek 4. I walked out to my garden at sunrise like I used to. Stood there tending my flowers for two hours without having to sit down.\\n\\nWeek 6. Fasting glucose, 102. Down from 158.\\n\\nWeek 10. I walked Sadie all the way to the creek behind the house and back. About a mile each way. I came home and sat on the porch and my feet did not hurt.\\n\\nLast month, I went to my granddaughter's recital. Sat on those hard auditorium seats for two hours and forgot about my feet the whole time. Just watched her perform. After the show I walked across the lobby and hugged her teacher.\\n\\nI cried in the car on the way home. My husband asked me what happened. I told him I was just happy to be there.\\n\\nHe did not know I was crying because for the first time in three years I had been at one of her shows and actually been at it. Not surviving it. Being at it.\\n\\nThree months in, I went back to my doctor.\\n\\nShe ran the nerve test. Then ran it again. Then pulled up my chart, scrolled to my A1C, and stopped.\\n\\n5.9. Down from 7.2.\\n\\nShe sat there looking at the chart for a long second.\\n\\nCaroline, I do not know what you are doing differently. But keep doing it.\\n\\nI told her about Metabolae. She wrote it down in my file. Did not argue.\\n\\nI have been telling every diabetic woman I know about this.\\n\\nMy sister started on it two months after I did. She is 65, had neuropathy for eight years. Down to two hours of burning at night from six. Sleeping through most nights now.\\n\\nMy friend at church started on it last month. Already says the tingling in her hands is fading.\\n\\nWe started calling it the wake-up.\\n\\nBecause that is what it feels like. Not just turning down the volume on the pain. Actually waking the nerves back up.\\n\\nI am telling you this because I was exactly where you are right now. Up at 2am with your feet on fire, scrolling on your phone, scared of where this is heading. Watching the same pattern your mother or your father or your grandmother went through. Knowing you are on that road and not knowing how to get off it.\\n\\nGabapentin does not get you off it. It just blindfolds you while you keep walking down it.\\n\\nThe supplements you have already tried did not get you off it because they do not address the two-front attack happening in your nerves right now.\\n\\nMetabolae is different because it goes after both sides at the same time. Cinnamaldehyde activates the GLUT4 doors and stops the sugar attack at the source. The proanthocyanidins restore the vessels feeding your nerves. The anti-inflammatory polyphenols suppress the NLRP3 inflammasome and protect the nerve fibers themselves. That is the difference. That is why everything else failed you. That is why this worked when nothing else did.\\n\\nI know you are skeptical. I was too.\\n\\nYou have tried things. They did not work. Why would this be any different?\\n\\nBecause everything else was treating the symptom while the disease kept eating your nerves. This goes after the disease.\\n\\nIt really is that simple. And that infuriating.\\n\\n15 years of diabetes. Three years of Gabapentin fog. $380 a month on Lyrica. Nerve tests getting worse every six months like clockwork.\\n\\nThree months of Metabolae and I was walking my dog to the creek, standing in my garden at sunrise, sitting through my granddaughter's whole recital without thinking about my feet once.\\n\\nMy mother never got to know any of this was possible. She started losing feeling in her feet at 58. By 64 she was missing toes. By 67 she was in a wheelchair. By 70 she was gone. And I spent five years thinking I was walking the same road until I was not anymore.\\n\\nIf you are reading this because your feet are burning at 3am and you cannot sleep, if your A1C looks managed while your nerve tests keep getting worse, if Gabapentin made you foggy and Lyrica drained your bank account, if you have taken every supplement and nothing moved, if you are quietly watching the numbness spread further every six months and wondering how much time you have before this takes something from you that you cannot get back, you are not crazy. You are not weak. You are not just getting older.\\n\\nYou were just giving your nerves what they needed without stopping the two-front attack that was destroying them faster than anything could save them. Metabolae stops the attack. That is why this works.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon contains three compounds that address the two specific failures of diabetic neuropathy. Cinnamaldehyde activates the GLUT4 doors and stops the sugar at the source. The proanthocyanidins restore the vessels. The anti-inflammatory polyphenols protect the nerves. But only if it is the right cinnamon, in the right form, with the right delivery.\\n\\nCassia from your spice rack does not do this.\\n\\nDry capsules or powders from Amazon do not do this.\\n\\nMetabolae is the only brand I have found that gets all of it right.\\n\\nDNA-verified true Ceylon. Concentrated 12 to 1. Suspended in organic MCT oil. Third-party tested. Made in the USA in small batches.\\n\\n90 days to try it. If your burning does not quiet, if the numbness does not lift, if your sleep does not change, send back what is left and they refund every dollar. No paperwork. No questions.\\n\\nOne last thing before you go.\\n\\nMetabolae has been going viral the last few months. The before and afters people like me are posting. The stories. The nerve test results.\\n\\nAnd anytime something works that well, the knockoffs show up. Cheap imitators on Amazon and lookalike sites with names that sound almost like Metabolae. Same label. Cassia instead of Ceylon. Filler oils instead of MCT. No DNA testing. No idea what is actually in the softgel.\\n\\nIf you are going to try this, only order through the official site.\\n\\nNot Amazon. Not eBay. Not whatever pops up first on Google.\\n\\nThe real bag ships from the company directly with the 90-day guarantee attached.\\n\\nCaroline Moore\\n\\nP.S. The first time I walked all the way to the creek without my feet burning, I sat on a rock for ten minutes just listening to the water. I had forgotten what that sounded like at sunrise. You forget a lot of things when you are spending every minute managing your pain. You do not realize how much you forgot until you start to get it back.\\n\\nP.P.S. Do not wait until your doctor mentions the word amputation. My mother waited. I almost did too.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/preinsol\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Supports healthy blood sugar levels and metabolism with premium Ceylon cinnamon. Shop Metabolae's science-backed supplements. Free shipping available.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":127974,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":1152,\"duplicates\":8,\"reachDelta1d\":6169,\"reachDelta7d\":52172,\"reachDelta30d\":127119},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"GB\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":16,\"currentRank\":16,\"rankDelta\":1,\"improvementPct\":0.47},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}}],\"pagination\":{\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"total\":23746,\"totalPages\":10000,\"offset\":0,\"isTotalApproximate\":true},\"meta\":{\"query\":null,\"keywords\":[\"ceylon\",\"blood sugar\"],\"searchIn\":\"ad_copy\",\"categoryIds\":[],\"country\":null,\"countryFilterKind\":\"audienceCountry\",\"adCountries\":null,\"mainCountries\":[],\"trendSignal\":\"reach_growth_7d\",\"sortBy\":\"reachDelta7d\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"requestedLimit\":1,\"effectiveLimit\":1,\"activeOnly\":true,\"status\":\"active\",\"page\":1,\"minTrafficGrowthPercent\":null,\"trafficGrowthPeriod\":\"last30d\",\"trafficGrowth\":null,\"adsGrowth\":null,\"pageReachGrowth\":null,\"adReachGrowth\":null,\"language\":null,\"adLanguages\":[],\"cta\":[],\"currency\":null,\"currencies\":[],\"shopifyAppIds\":[],\"excludeShopifyAppIds\":[],\"pixelIds\":[],\"excludePixelIds\":[],\"themeIds\":[],\"filters\":{\"search\":[\"ceylon\",\"blood sugar\"],\"searchType\":\"adCopy\",\"keywordMode\":\"all\",\"sortBy\":\"reachDelta7d\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"page\":1,\"limit\":1,\"platforms\":[\"facebook\"],\"status\":\"active\"},\"warnings\":[\"Reported total remains exact, but totalPages is capped by pageable depth (offset<=10000).\"],\"paginationMaxOffset\":10000},\"fieldGuide\":{\"countryBreakdowns\":[{\"path\":\"data[].audience.targetedCountries\",\"meaning\":\"