{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ls5XALU4ySMiJ93wbuq1Bp","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"requestId\":\"02f9faa3-7028-4c6f-af01-84ada6a3832f\",\"data\":[{\"id\":\"facebook_1474205961228342\",\"collationId\":\"2196202077601005\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-11-02T04:12:34.540Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-10-31T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-26T19:11:51.647Z\",\"daysRunning\":27,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/738839f9e96c2042dd4eeb44bc585aabe4f2d566808956a9a1d3c9419d2b48e1.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1013442788524883\",\"name\":\"Caroline Moore\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/1013442788524883.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1013442788524883\",\"liveAdsCount\":3389,\"reach30d\":1144134,\"totalReach\":2517956,\"countriesCount\":2,\"facebookLikes\":1884,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I need to tell you something that will make you f*cking livid about your doctor.\\n\\nMy name is Caroline. I am 62 years old. I have been a Type 2 diabetic for 15 years. Take Metformin twice a day, every morning and every night.\\n\\nAnd for the last five years, I have had diabetic neuropathy in both feet. The last three years, the burning and the numbness have gotten worse every single month no matter what I did.\\n\\nAnd when I say no matter what I did, I mean every damn thing.\\n\\nGabapentin for almost three years. Made me foggy enough that I would forget conversations my husband and I just had. Gained 18 pounds. Still woke up at 3am with my feet on fire.\\n\\nTried Lyrica next. $380 a month out of pocket. Worse than the Gabapentin. Felt like I was wearing a wet blanket over my brain. Could not think straight in the kitchen.\\n\\nI took B12. I took Alpha Lipoic Acid. I bought one of those expensive advanced nerve formulas off Amazon with four thousand five-star reviews.\\n\\nNothing.\\n\\nI stopped taking my morning walks. Stopped going out to the garden at sunrise. Stopped going to my granddaughter's dance recitals because sitting on those hard auditorium seats felt like sitting on hot coals.\\n\\nYou know what my doctor said? Caroline, this is just how diabetic neuropathy progresses. We can up your Gabapentin and add a second diabetes pill.\\n\\nI damn near walked out of that office.\\n\\nI am about to tell you exactly what they are hiding from you and why you will never hear it in a doctor's office.\\n\\nAnd by the end of this, you are gonna be upset too. Because once you see what is actually going on, you will understand why nothing you have tried has worked, and why the medical system has no real interest in telling you.\\n\\nThere are three things happening right now.\\n\\nOne, your body is screaming at you that something is very wrong.\\n\\nTwo, the medical system is telling you to just accept it and take more pills that fog your head and pack on weight.\\n\\nThree, there is a multibillion-dollar industry that profits every single day you stay in pain.\\n\\nI used to love getting up at 7 in the morning and walking my dog out to the trail behind my house.\\n\\nI used to love spending Saturday mornings in my garden before the sun got too high. I used to love sitting on the porch with a cup of coffee, before anybody else in the house was up.\\n\\nThat is not my life anymore.\\n\\nNow I plan every step around my feet. I check the floor before I take a step because half the time I cannot feel where they are landing. I fell off my own porch step a year ago and broke my wrist. Could not feel I had missed it.\\n\\nMy husband tells me to slow down. My daughter thinks I am just getting old.\\n\\nThey do not know that some nights the burning is so bad I lie there with my feet hanging off the end of the bed because the weight of a sheet feels like sandpaper.\\n\\nNobody knows. That is the worst part.\\n\\nYou look fine standing there. You are shaking hands at church and you look fine. Inside, you are counting down the minutes until you can sit. Until you can get your shoes off. Until the day is over.\\n\\nAnd when you finally get home, you sit in the recliner at 11pm with your feet in a bucket of cold water because that is the only thing that helps anymore.\\n\\nMy mother had diabetes too.\\n\\nShe took Metformin for 22 years. I watched what the disease did to her anyway. First they took two toes off her right foot. Then they took the whole foot. By 67 she was in a wheelchair. She died at 70, two weeks before my daughter's wedding. She never met her great-grandkids.\\n\\nI think about her every single day.\\n\\nI am 62. She started losing feeling in her feet at 58. By 64 she was already missing toes.\\n\\nI know what road I am on.\\n\\nLast Christmas, I was sitting at my kitchen table while the whole family was over. My grandkids were running through the house yelling. My daughter was making a pie. My husband was setting the table.\\n\\nAnd I was sitting. Not because I wanted to. Because I was scared to get up and walk across the kitchen in front of everybody. I had fallen twice that month. I did not want my granddaughter to see me go down.\\n\\nSo I sat there. Smiled. Passed the food. Laughed at the right moments.\\n\\nNobody at that table knew I had been dreading that night for two weeks. Nobody knew I was tracking every step I would have to take from the kitchen to the bathroom and back.\\n\\nI was not there. I was just surviving the room.\\n\\nI was terrified.\\n\\nBecause I had watched what happened to my mother. And I knew I was on the same road.\\n\\nI did everything right. I watched my blood sugar. I took every pill the doctor wrote me. I went to every nerve test, every six months, like clockwork.\\n\\nAnd every six months, the results came back a little worse.\\n\\nA little more nerve damage. A little more numbness creeping up.\\n\\nThen about four months ago, I was up at 2 in the morning because the burning would not let me sleep. I was on my phone, scrolling YouTube, just trying to distract myself.\\n\\nI found a 90-minute interview with a guy named Dale. Former Army medic from Tennessee. He had been Type 2 for over 20 years, had neuropathy bad enough his doctors had told him amputation was probably coming within five years.\\n\\nHe did not get the amputation. Three years later, he is walking three miles a day. Sleeping through the night. Feet warm again.\\n\\nAnd he explained something in that interview I had never heard before.\\n\\nHe said diabetic neuropathy is not really a separate condition. It is what diabetes does to your nerves when the disease keeps running underneath your medication. And it comes from two things happening at the same time, in the same place.\\n\\nThe first is the trapped sugar in your blood. Your cells stopped responding to insulin years ago, so glucose sits in your bloodstream with nowhere to go. Metformin does not pull that sugar out. It just slows your liver from making more. So the existing trapped sugar stays in your blood. And it does not sit there politely. It chemically attacks the proteins inside your nerve fibers. It bonds to them. Hardens them. Inflames them. That is why the signals start firing wrong, burning when nothing is touching you, tingling when there is nothing there. The sugar is grinding your nerves down from the inside.\\n\\nThe second is the small blood vessels that feed those nerves. Same trapped sugar damages those vessels too. They stiffen. They stop delivering oxygen and nutrients. So while your nerves are being chemically attacked, they are also starving. They cannot repair themselves because the supply line bringing them what they need has been cut off.\\n\\nThat is diabetic neuropathy.\\n\\nAnd here is why Gabapentin does not fix any of it.\\n\\nGabapentin does not pull the sugar out. Does not heal the vessels. Does not repair the nerves. All it does is numb the brain so it receives less of the pain signal. The damage is still happening underneath. The nerves are still dying. You just cannot feel it as much.\\n\\nThat is not treatment. That is a blindfold.\\n\\nAnd the same goes for B12, Alpha Lipoic Acid, and every nerve supplement on the market. They support nerve function in general. But they do not pull the trapped sugar out. They do not repair the vessels. They are trying to feed a nerve that is being attacked from two sides at once, and they are not built strong enough to fight either attack.\\n\\nThat is why nothing you have tried has worked.\\n\\nIt was never a vitamin problem. It was never a nutrient problem. It is a two-front attack on your nerves that almost nothing addresses.\\n\\nDale said the only thing he had ever found that did was a compound system inside a spice your grandmother had in her kitchen.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon cinnamon.\\n\\nNot the cinnamon on your spice rack. That stuff is Cassia, a cheap lookalike from China that comes from a completely different tree. It is barely related to true Ceylon chemically. The compounds your nerves actually need are barely present in it. And it contains a compound called coumarin that is hard on your liver in daily doses.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon comes from Sri Lanka. The chemistry is completely different. And inside the bark are three specific compounds that address the two-front attack on diabetic nerves.\\n\\nThe first compound is cinnamaldehyde. It is the compound that gives cinnamon its warmth. But what most people do not know is that cinnamaldehyde activates something called GLUT4 translocation. Think of GLUT4 as the doors on your cells that let sugar in. When you have diabetes, those doors get stuck shut. Insulin knocks, but the doors do not open. Metformin does not open those doors either. Cinnamaldehyde activates those doors through a completely different pathway. The same pathway that exercise uses. The trapped sugar finally moves out of your blood and into your cells where it belongs. The sugar attack on your nerves stops at the source.\\n\\nBut stopping the attack only solves half the problem. The vessels feeding your nerves have already been damaged from years of that same sugar grinding against them, and they are still not delivering. That is what the second compound, the proanthocyanidins concentrated in true Ceylon bark, takes care of. These are powerful antioxidant compounds that reduce the oxidative stress on the small blood vessels your nerves depend on. They support the vessel lining. Blood reaches the nerves again. The starving nerves finally get fed.\\n\\nAnd the third compound is the one that surprised me the most. Ceylon cinnamon contains anti-inflammatory polyphenols that suppress something called NLRP3 inflammasome activation. That is the master switch behind chronic inflammation. While the sugar is being pulled out and the vessels are coming back online, the nerve fibers themselves still need protection from the inflammation that has been building inside them for years. Research published in 2024 in the Journal of Inflammation found that cinnamaldehyde directly inhibits this inflammatory pathway, reducing the chemicals that destroy nerve tissue and supporting the body's own repair process.\\n\\nThree compounds. From one spice. Doing three things no nerve supplement or medication on the market is built to do.\\n\\nDale said your doctor will never tell you about it. You cannot patent a tree.\\n\\nI was skeptical. Everything else had failed me. But I figured, what do I have left to lose? My nerve test results were getting worse every six months. I was already walking the same road my mother did.\\n\\nI went back to that YouTube interview and scrolled through the comments. Hundreds of comments from other diabetic people who had tried cinnamon. And what they said lined up with what I had been suspecting.\\n\\nMost cinnamon supplements are useless. They use Cassia instead of Ceylon. They are processed at high heat that kills the active compounds. They come in dry capsules, and here is the part I did not know, the three compounds in cinnamon are fat-soluble. They do not absorb in a dry capsule. They have to be carried into your bloodstream by the right kind of fat. Without it, they just pass through you.\\n\\nSo even if you buy real Ceylon, in a dry capsule, you are paying for cinnamon that never reaches your nerves.\\n\\nThe brand that kept coming up in the comments was Metabolae.\\n\\nReal Ceylon, DNA-verified for the species. Concentrated 12 to 1. Suspended in organic MCT oil that actually carries the compounds across your gut wall and into your bloodstream where they can do something. Third-party tested. Made in the USA in small batches.\\n\\nThat is the difference. Same spice. Right form. Right delivery.\\n\\nI ordered a bag.\\n\\nOne softgel with breakfast. That is it.\\n\\nWeek 1. I slept five hours straight before the burning woke me up. Five hours. I had not done that in three years.\\n\\nWeek 2. The numbness in my left big toe, gone for almost two years, started feeling like something again. Not normal. But not nothing. A tingling. A warmth. Something that was not there yesterday was there today.\\n\\nI sat on the edge of my bed at six in the morning and put my fingers on my own toe and felt it back. I did not tell anybody. I just sat there.\\n\\nWeek 4. I walked out to my garden at sunrise like I used to. Stood there tending my flowers for two hours without having to sit down.\\n\\nWeek 6. Fasting glucose, 102. Down from 158.\\n\\nWeek 10. I walked Sadie all the way to the creek behind the house and back. About a mile each way. I came home and sat on the porch and my feet did not hurt.\\n\\nLast month, I went to my granddaughter's recital. Sat on those hard auditorium seats for two hours and forgot about my feet the whole time. Just watched her perform. After the show I walked across the lobby and hugged her teacher.\\n\\nI cried in the car on the way home. My husband asked me what happened. I told him I was just happy to be there.\\n\\nHe did not know I was crying because for the first time in three years I had been at one of her shows and actually been at it. Not surviving it. Being at it.\\n\\nThree months in, I went back to my doctor.\\n\\nShe ran the nerve test. Then ran it again. Then pulled up my chart, scrolled to my A1C, and stopped.\\n\\n5.9. Down from 7.2.\\n\\nShe sat there looking at the chart for a long second.\\n\\nCaroline, I do not know what you are doing differently. But keep doing it.\\n\\nI told her about Metabolae. She wrote it down in my file. Did not argue.\\n\\nI have been telling every diabetic woman I know about this.\\n\\nMy sister started on it two months after I did. She is 65, had neuropathy for eight years. Down to two hours of burning at night from six. Sleeping through most nights now.\\n\\nMy friend at church started on it last month. Already says the tingling in her hands is fading.\\n\\nWe started calling it the wake-up.\\n\\nBecause that is what it feels like. Not just turning down the volume on the pain. Actually waking the nerves back up.\\n\\nI am telling you this because I was exactly where you are right now. Up at 2am with your feet on fire, scrolling on your phone, scared of where this is heading. Watching the same pattern your mother or your father or your grandmother went through. Knowing you are on that road and not knowing how to get off it.\\n\\nGabapentin does not get you off it. It just blindfolds you while you keep walking down it.\\n\\nThe supplements you have already tried did not get you off it because they do not address the two-front attack happening in your nerves right now.\\n\\nMetabolae is different because it goes after both sides at the same time. Cinnamaldehyde activates the GLUT4 doors and stops the sugar attack at the source. The proanthocyanidins restore the vessels feeding your nerves. The anti-inflammatory polyphenols suppress the NLRP3 inflammasome and protect the nerve fibers themselves. That is the difference. That is why everything else failed you. That is why this worked when nothing else did.\\n\\nI know you are skeptical. I was too.\\n\\nYou have tried things. They did not work. Why would this be any different?\\n\\nBecause everything else was treating the symptom while the disease kept eating your nerves. This goes after the disease.\\n\\nIt really is that simple. And that infuriating.\\n\\n15 years of diabetes. Three years of Gabapentin fog. $380 a month on Lyrica. Nerve tests getting worse every six months like clockwork.\\n\\nThree months of Metabolae and I was walking my dog to the creek, standing in my garden at sunrise, sitting through my granddaughter's whole recital without thinking about my feet once.\\n\\nMy mother never got to know any of this was possible. She started losing feeling in her feet at 58. By 64 she was missing toes. By 67 she was in a wheelchair. By 70 she was gone. And I spent five years thinking I was walking the same road until I was not anymore.\\n\\nIf you are reading this because your feet are burning at 3am and you cannot sleep, if your A1C looks managed while your nerve tests keep getting worse, if Gabapentin made you foggy and Lyrica drained your bank account, if you have taken every supplement and nothing moved, if you are quietly watching the numbness spread further every six months and wondering how much time you have before this takes something from you that you cannot get back, you are not crazy. You are not weak. You are not just getting older.\\n\\nYou were just giving your nerves what they needed without stopping the two-front attack that was destroying them faster than anything could save them. Metabolae stops the attack. That is why this works.\\n\\nTrue Ceylon contains three compounds that address the two specific failures of diabetic neuropathy. Cinnamaldehyde activates the GLUT4 doors and stops the sugar at the source. The proanthocyanidins restore the vessels. The anti-inflammatory polyphenols protect the nerves. But only if it is the right cinnamon, in the right form, with the right delivery.\\n\\nCassia from your spice rack does not do this.\\n\\nDry capsules or powders from Amazon do not do this.\\n\\nMetabolae is the only brand I have found that gets all of it right.\\n\\nDNA-verified true Ceylon. Concentrated 12 to 1. Suspended in organic MCT oil. Third-party tested. Made in the USA in small batches.\\n\\n90 days to try it. If your burning does not quiet, if the numbness does not lift, if your sleep does not change, send back what is left and they refund every dollar. No paperwork. No questions.\\n\\nOne last thing before you go.\\n\\nMetabolae has been going viral the last few months. The before and afters people like me are posting. The stories. The nerve test results.\\n\\nAnd anytime something works that well, the knockoffs show up. Cheap imitators on Amazon and lookalike sites with names that sound almost like Metabolae. Same label. Cassia instead of Ceylon. Filler oils instead of MCT. No DNA testing. No idea what is actually in the softgel.\\n\\nIf you are going to try this, only order through the official site.\\n\\nNot Amazon. Not eBay. Not whatever pops up first on Google.\\n\\nThe real bag ships from the company directly with the 90-day guarantee attached.\\n\\nCaroline Moore\\n\\nP.S. The first time I walked all the way to the creek without my feet burning, I sat on a rock for ten minutes just listening to the water. I had forgotten what that sounded like at sunrise. You forget a lot of things when you are spending every minute managing your pain. You do not realize how much you forgot until you start to get it back.\\n\\nP.P.S. Do not wait until your doctor mentions the word amputation. My mother waited. I almost did too.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/preinsol\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Supports healthy blood sugar levels and metabolism with premium Ceylon cinnamon. Shop Metabolae's science-backed supplements. Free shipping available.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":127974,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":1152,\"duplicates\":8,\"reachDelta1d\":6169,\"reachDelta7d\":52172,\"reachDelta30d\":127119},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"GB\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":16,\"currentRank\":16,\"rankDelta\":1,\"improvementPct\":0.47},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1460211769334302\",\"collationId\":\"1985492239453175\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"active\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-08T17:11:33.633Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-10-08T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-26T07:39:20.393Z\",\"daysRunning\":50,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/796e5c92c5069ae46d241adf0485cd3d771e6f329dcda06d89481275eb61ba93.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"1050252671500385\",\"name\":\"Dr. Matthew Walker\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/1050252671500385.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"1050252671500385\",\"liveAdsCount\":1106,\"reach30d\":805188,\"totalReach\":1927191,\"countriesCount\":2,\"facebookLikes\":4501,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Is it true you can flush all the sugar out of your blood through your urine.\\n\\nYes. And it is something 98% of diabetics have no idea about.\\n\\nI'm an endocrinologist. I have been treating Type 2 diabetics for 24 years. And if I were going to die tomorrow, I would want every diabetic, including you, to stop asking God to deliver you from Type 2 diabetes.\\n\\nHe already sent the answer. You are the one refusing to reach for it.\\n\\nBut before I show you what it is, you need to understand why you have not found it yet. There are three lies you have been told about Type 2 diabetes that have kept you sick, kept you on medications, and kept you from the one thing that actually addresses what is broken.\\n\\nI am exposing all three of them right now. And I am ashamed to tell you that for most of my career, I was the one telling them to you.\\n\\nThe first lie is that Type 2 diabetes is caused by eating too much sugar.\\n\\nThat is what I told my patients for years. Cut the carbs. Watch the sugar. Walk more.\\n\\nThen I became one of them.\\n\\nI was 54 years old when my own fasting glucose crossed the line. The endocrinologist who treats other people's diabetes was now diabetic himself. I did not tell my patients. I did not tell my colleagues. I told my wife, and I started doing exactly what I had been telling everyone else to do.\\n\\nI cut carbs for four months straight. I lost eleven pounds. I walked every morning. I tracked every gram of sugar that went into my body.\\n\\nMy fasting glucose went up.\\n\\nNot down. Up.\\n\\nI understood the textbook explanation. Insulin resistance progresses. Lifestyle modification has diminishing returns at later stages. I had said those words to a thousand patients.\\n\\nBut hearing yourself say them about your own body is a different thing entirely.\\n\\nType 2 diabetes is not caused by eating too much sugar. Type 2 diabetes is a cellular door disease.\\n\\nEvery cell in your body has a tiny door on it. When you eat, your blood sugar rises. Your pancreas sends insulin to open those doors. The doors open. Sugar moves out of the blood and into the cells where it gets burned for energy. Blood sugar drops. The system works.\\n\\nIn a Type 2 diabetic, the doors have stopped opening.\\n\\nInsulin arrives. It knocks. Nothing answers. The doors are stuck. So every gram of sugar from every meal stays trapped in your bloodstream with nowhere to go.\\n\\nThat trapped sugar grinds through the lining of every blood vessel in your body. Day after day. Year after year. Destroying the tiny vessels that feed your feet. Your kidneys. Your eyes. Your liver. Your heart.\\n\\nThat is why cutting carbs does almost nothing. You reduce the sugar coming in, but the sugar already trapped in your blood is still there. Still grinding. The doors are still closed.\\n\\nThat is why fasting does almost nothing. You stop eating for hours, but the doors are still closed. The trapped sugar has nowhere to go.\\n\\nThat is why walking for an hour a day does almost nothing. Your muscles burn a little glucose, but the doors on billions of cells throughout your body are still stuck shut.\\n\\nThe problem was never how much sugar you eat. The problem is that the doors on your cells stopped opening years ago, and nothing you have been told to do addresses that.\\n\\nI told my patients to cut carbs for two decades. I watched them try. I watched them fail. And I told them it was because their disease had progressed. That the medications were the only thing that would work now.\\n\\nI was wrong. I was not wrong about their commitment. I was wrong about the target.\\n\\nThe second lie is that your medications fix the problem.\\n\\nThis is the one I cannot forgive myself for. Because I prescribed every single one of them.\\n\\nI was on Metformin. 1,000 milligrams twice a day. The same prescription I had written for thousands of patients. My doctor added lisinopril for my blood pressure two years later. Two pills a day and my numbers were still getting worse.\\n\\nI knew what each medication did. I had explained it to patients ten times a week for 24 years. But I had never once framed it the way I am about to frame it for you.\\n\\nMetformin tells your liver to produce less new sugar. That is its job. Less sugar entering the bloodstream means a lower number on the meter.\\n\\nBut Metformin does not open the doors. The sugar already trapped in your blood stays trapped. The grinding continues.\\n\\nOzempic slows your stomach so less sugar enters your blood after a meal. The number on the meter comes down some. The chart looks better at the next visit.\\n\\nBut Ozempic does not open the doors either. The trapped sugar stays. The grinding continues.\\n\\nInsulin forces sugar through doors that have stopped opening. It works. But at what cost. Weight gain. Risk of hypoglycemia. Needles for the rest of your life. And a trajectory I have watched play out in my own patients for 24 years. The dose goes up. The complications still arrive. The outcome barely changes.\\n\\nIt is like turning down the faucet while the drain is clogged. The water level drops a little. The chart looks better. But the clog is still there. The water is still standing. And every day it sits, it damages the pipes a little more.\\n\\nThat is exactly what is happening inside your body right now.\\n\\nYour medications are managing a number on a chart. The trapped sugar that is grinding through your vessels, the sugar that is damaging your feet and your kidneys and your eyes and your heart, is still there. Still grinding. With every heartbeat.\\n\\nI know this because I watched it happen to my own body. The same body that sat on the other side of the desk from patients and told them their medications were working.\\n\\nSix months before I would have started myself on insulin, my feet started tingling at night. Sometimes burning. I would kick the covers off looking for cool sheets.\\n\\nMy vision was going blurry. I was squinting at lab reports on my own desk. An endocrinologist who cannot read his own patient's bloodwork clearly is an endocrinologist who should not be practicing.\\n\\nI was getting up to use the bathroom three times a night. Twice at minimum. It was wrecking my sleep.\\n\\nMy creatinine was climbing. I knew exactly what that meant. I had delivered that news to patients a thousand times with a calm face. Delivering it to yourself is not calm.\\n\\nMy liver enzymes were up. My blood pressure was creeping despite the lisinopril. I was looking at adding a second medication.\\n\\nI had gained 19 pounds over three years without doing anything different.\\n\\nAnd I was exhausted. The kind of exhausted that does not go away with a weekend off. I would finish a day of clinic and sit in my office for twenty minutes before I could walk to my car.\\n\\nAll of this while taking my own medications exactly as prescribed. Every day. Every dose. For years.\\n\\nThe medications were managing the number on the chart. My body was falling apart underneath it. And I was the one who had spent 24 years telling people those medications were the answer.\\n\\nThe third lie is that Type 2 diabetes is irreversible. That you will be managing it until the day you die.\\n\\nThis is the lie that almost cost me everything.\\n\\nI told my patients this. I told them the best they could hope for was to slow the progression. That eventually the medications would need to be increased, then stacked, then replaced with insulin. I told them this because that is what I was taught. That is what the guidelines said. That is what I believed.\\n\\nI had watched that exact trajectory play out in my own practice for 24 years. First the pills. Then more pills. Then the shots. Then the complications that show up anyway. The toes. The dialysis. The referrals to nephrology that I knew were the beginning of the end.\\n\\nI was starting to believe it was inevitable for me too. That I was going to become the worst version of my own patient.\\n\\nThen a colleague I have known for fifteen years sent me a paper I had never read.\\n\\nHe is a researcher. Molecular biology, not clinical practice. He had been looking at insulin receptor signaling and stumbled into a body of literature that most endocrinologists never see because it does not come from pharmaceutical research. It comes from plant biochemistry.\\n\\nHe told me research had identified two specific compounds in true Ceylon cinnamon that address both halves of the problem. Not the symptom. Not the number on the chart. The actual mechanism that is broken.\\n\\nI did not believe him. I am a doctor trained in evidence-based medicine. I do not believe things without data.\\n\\nSo he sent me the data.\\n\\nPeer-reviewed studies. Published in Diabetes Care, the American Diabetes Association's own flagship journal. The Journal of Diabetes Research. Cardiovascular journals.\\n\\nA 2003 trial. Sixty Type 2 diabetics. Forty days on cinnamon. Fasting glucose dropped 29 percent. Triglycerides dropped 30 percent. LDL cholesterol dropped up to 27 percent.\\n\\nA 2024 meta-analysis. Twenty-eight randomized trials. Over three thousand patients. Same direction across the board.\\n\\nMechanism papers showing cinnamaldehyde tested on vessel linings damaged by high glucose. The cells that had stopped producing nitric oxide started producing it again.\\n\\nI read every paper. I checked the methodology. I looked at the sample sizes and the p-values. That is the doctor in me.\\n\\nThen I pushed back. Because I had patients who told me they tried cinnamon before. I had dismissed every one of them. Nothing happened, they said. And I believed them, because the cinnamon they were taking was never going to work.\\n\\nAnd that is when I learned why.\\n\\nThere are three reasons the cinnamon you tried did not work. Three specific things that have to be right for the compounds to reach your bloodstream and do what the research says they do. If any one of them is wrong, you are wasting your money and your time.\\n\\nThe first is the species. Ninety percent of what is sold as cinnamon in America is not cinnamon. It is cassia. Cinnamomum cassia. A completely different tree from China. It looks similar. It smells similar. It has almost none of the active compounds. And it carries a substance called coumarin that damages your liver at daily doses. If you have been taking cinnamon supplements and your liver enzymes are creeping up, think about that.\\n\\nThe second is the dose. The clinical studies used doses equivalent to 6,000 to 7,200 milligrams of raw Ceylon cinnamon per day. Most supplements on the shelf contain 500 to 1,500 milligrams of dry powder. That is a fraction of what the research says is needed. You could take them for a year and never reach a clinical dose.\\n\\nThe third is the delivery. The active compounds in Ceylon cinnamon are fat-soluble. That means they only dissolve in fat. Swallow them in a dry capsule and they pass straight through your digestive system before they ever reach a cell. Your body cannot absorb them without a fat carrier. In Sri Lanka, healers have steeped the bark in coconut oil for over 3,000 years. There is a reason for that. The oil carries the compound through the gut wall and into the bloodstream where it can actually do its job.\\n\\nWrong species. Wrong dose. Wrong delivery.\\n\\nThree failures stacked on top of each other. My patients had been taking the broken version of something that actually works. I had dismissed them when they told me cinnamon did nothing. I owe them an apology. The cinnamon they were taking was never going to do anything. But the ingredient itself was never the problem.\\n\\nEvery person who has ever tried cinnamon for blood sugar and seen nothing happen was almost certainly taking cassia, at a fraction of the research dose, in a dry capsule that their body could not absorb.\\n\\nThey did not fail. They were failed.\\n\\nThere are two compounds in true Ceylon cinnamon bark that do what none of the medications I have prescribed for 24 years ever did.\\n\\nThe first is called MHCP. It signals the doors on your cells to start opening again. The same doors that stopped responding to insulin years ago. MHCP reaches those doors and tells them to unlock. When they unlock, the sugar that has been trapped in your blood for years finally has somewhere to go. It moves out of your bloodstream and into the cells where it belongs. Where it gets burned for energy instead of grinding through your vessels.\\n\\nThe fasting number comes down. The A1C comes down. Not because you reduced the sugar going in. Because the sugar finally has a way out.\\n\\nThe second compound is called cinnamaldehyde. It is what gives cinnamon its smell. And it does something none of the medications I have ever prescribed even attempted. It supports the vessel lining that has been damaged by all those years of trapped sugar grinding through your body.\\n\\nWhen sugar grinds through your blood vessels for years, it damages the cells that produce nitric oxide. Nitric oxide is the signal that tells your vessels to relax and carry blood where it needs to go. Without it, blood flow slows. The tiny vessels that feed your feet start to fail. The vessels feeding your kidneys stop delivering what they need. The vessels feeding your eyes deteriorate. The vessels feeding your heart work harder and harder.\\n\\nCinnamaldehyde helps those damaged vessel cells start producing nitric oxide again. Blood flow returns to the places it stopped reaching. The feet. The kidneys. The eyes. The liver. The heart.\\n\\nTwo compounds. Two jobs.\\n\\nThe first opens the doors so the trapped sugar finally clears.\\n\\nThe second repairs the damage the trapped sugar left behind.\\n\\nTogether, they address what every medication I have ever prescribed never could. Not the number on the chart. The actual disease underneath it.\\n\\nOnce I understood the three failure points, I started looking for a product that got all three right. Real Ceylon, not cassia. Concentrated to a clinical dose, not a sprinkle. Suspended in fat, not packed dry into a capsule.\\n\\nMost of what I found online was one or two out of three. Real Ceylon but underdosed. Clinical dose but dry powder. Suspended in oil but cassia inside.\\n\\nThen I found Metabolae.\\n\\nDNA-verified true Ceylon cinnamon from Sri Lanka. Not cassia from China. Not a blend. The real species, confirmed at the molecular level.\\n\\nConcentrated 12 to 1. That means one softgel delivers the equivalent of 7,200 milligrams of raw Ceylon cinnamon. That is the range the clinical research used. Not 500 milligrams. Not 1,500. The actual dose.\\n\\nSuspended in MCT oil. The fat carrier that allows the compounds to cross your gut wall and reach your bloodstream. The same principle behind steeping the bark in coconut oil, which is how it has been done in Sri Lanka for over 3,000 years.\\n\\nThird-party tested on every batch. Made in the USA in a GMP-certified facility.\\n\\nOne softgel every morning with breakfast. That is the whole thing.\\n\\nI ordered that night. Not because I fully believed. Because the mechanism was sound. Because the research was real. And because I had nothing left to lose.\\n\\nThe bag arrived on a Wednesday. I started the next morning.\\n\\nI want to walk you through what changed, organ by organ, because every single symptom I listed above started reversing in the same order the damage had built up. And I want you to find yourself in this list, because if your body has been doing what mine was doing, you deserve to know that it does not have to keep going.\\n\\nWeek one. My energy came back. Not like caffeine. Nothing sharp or jittery. Just a steady, even feeling I had not had in years. I finished a full day of clinic and walked to my car without sitting in my office first. I did not notice it the first day. By the fifth day, my wife noticed.\\n\\nWeek two. My post-meal crashes stopped. I used to fall asleep in my chair every night after dinner. My body would hit a wall an hour after eating and there was nothing I could do about it. By week two it was gone. I was awake after dinner. I was present with my family. That sounds like a small thing. It was not small.\\n\\nWeek three. I slept through the night. No bathroom trip at 2am. No second trip at 4am. I woke up once, looked at the clock, and it was morning. I lay there for a minute because I did not believe it. That was the first time in over a year I had slept straight through.\\n\\nThe kidneys were getting relief. When the trapped sugar clears out of the blood, the kidneys do not have to work overtime to filter it anymore. The nighttime trips were my kidneys working too hard. They stopped.\\n\\nWeek four. My ankles stopped swelling by the end of the day. My wedding ring was loose. My face looked less puffy. I had not changed a single thing about what I was eating. I stepped on the scale and I was down five pounds.\\n\\nWeek five. My feet stopped burning at night. The tingling that had been waking me up for six months faded. I stopped kicking the covers off. I stopped searching for cool sheets. The tiny vessels feeding the nerves in my feet were getting blood flow back. Cinnamaldehyde doing its job.\\n\\nWeek six. I could read lab reports on my desk again without reaching for my glasses. I had blamed my eyes on age. It was not age. It was the tiny vessels feeding my retinas getting starved by trapped sugar for years. When the sugar cleared and the blood flow returned, my near vision sharpened. I noticed it at work first. My wife noticed it second.\\n\\nWeek eight. I tested my fasting glucose at home. It had been running in the 160s and 170s for months. The meter read 97. I sat at the kitchen table staring at that little screen. Then I tested again to make sure the meter was not broken. 98.\\n\\nWeek ten. My home blood pressure cuff read 121 over 75. It had been 139 over 89 the month before. My heart was not working as hard because the vessels were not as stiff. Nitric oxide was doing what it is supposed to do.\\n\\nWeek twelve. I went in for my blood draw. I had a colleague run the labs so I could look at them the way I look at a patient's results. Without bias.\\n\\nMy A1C came back at 5.8. It had been 7.4 twelve weeks earlier.\\n\\nMy fasting glucose was 91.\\n\\nMy total cholesterol dropped from 224 to 179. My LDL dropped from 148 to 96. My triglycerides came down over 100 points.\\n\\nMy creatinine came back at 1.0. My eGFR climbed from 71 to 83. My kidneys were filtering better than they had in four years.\\n\\nMy liver enzymes were back in normal range for the first time since my diagnosis.\\n\\nMy blood pressure at the visit was 118 over 74.\\n\\nI was still on my Metformin. Same dose as always. The only thing I had added was one softgel every morning.\\n\\nMy colleague looked at the numbers. He looked at me. He looked at the numbers again.\\n\\nIn 24 years of practice, I have never seen results like this in a patient, he said. And you are telling me you saw them in yourself.\\n\\nI came off the Metformin at week fourteen. I came off the lisinopril at week sixteen. My numbers held. They are still holding.\\n\\nI am writing this because I owe it to you.\\n\\nFor 24 years I sat on the other side of the desk and told people like you that your disease was progressive, that your medications were working, and that the best you could hope for was management. I told you those three lies because I believed them. I do not believe them anymore.\\n\\nIf your A1C has been climbing despite the Metformin, the diet changes, the supplements, all of it, what is happening in your body is the same thing that was happening in mine.\\n\\nThe doors on your cells have stopped opening. Your medications are managing a number while the trapped sugar keeps grinding through your body with every heartbeat. Your feet. Your kidneys. Your eyes. Your liver. Your heart.\\n\\nThat is why the complications keep showing up no matter how well managed you look on paper.\\n\\nNone of your medications open the doors.\\n\\nThere are two compounds in real Ceylon cinnamon that do. MHCP signals the doors to start opening again. Cinnamaldehyde supports the vessels the trapped sugar has been grinding through for years.\\n\\nTogether, they address what every medication I have ever prescribed never could.\\n\\nThe brand I use is called Metabolae. It is the only one I have found that gets every piece right. Real Ceylon, not cassia. DNA-verified from Sri Lanka. Concentrated 12 to 1 to match the clinical research dose. Suspended in MCT oil so the compounds actually reach your bloodstream. Third-party tested on every batch. Made in the USA.\\n\\nOne softgel every morning with your first meal. That is the whole thing.\\n\\nThey stand behind it with a 90-day money-back guarantee. Take it for 90 days. Track your numbers. If they have not moved, send back what is left and get every dollar back. No prescription I have ever written for a patient came with a refund.\\n\\nA note on availability. They make it in small batches because real DNA-verified Ceylon from Sri Lanka at this concentration cannot be rushed. When it sells out, the next batch takes time. If the link below is still live, there is still stock.\\n\\nTwo compounds. Two jobs. One softgel.\\n\\nThat is the answer He already sent. Reach for it.\\n\\nDr. Matthew Walker, Endocrinologist, 24 years\\n\\nP.S. Please do not stop your Metformin the day Metabolae arrives at your door. Add it to your morning routine. Track your numbers. Let your doctor pull the medications when the labs show you do not need them anymore. That is how I did it with my own. Nobody in this story stopped a medication without medical supervision.\\n\\nP.P.S. There are knockoffs flooding online marketplaces with similar looking bags and similar looking softgels. Inside is cassia and cheap seed oil. The coumarin in cassia damages your liver at daily doses. That is not a scare tactic. That is something I have seen on liver panels in my own patients for years without knowing the cause. The only place to get the real Metabolae is the official page. When it is in stock, order.\\n\\nP.P.P.S. My colleague, the researcher who sent me the paper that started all of this, he started Metabolae himself two months after watching what happened to me. His fasting glucose dropped from 107 to 94 in the first month. He calls it insurance. I call it the most important thing I have learned in 24 years of medicine.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"LEARN MORE\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://metabolae.com/pages/lp-ceylon-cinnamon-insulin-1\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"metabolae.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Ceylon Cinnamon 7200mg Equivalent with MCT Oil\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"Supports healthy blood sugar levels and metabolism with premium Ceylon cinnamon. Shop Metabolae's science-backed supplements. Free shipping available.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":73942,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":665,\"duplicates\":5,\"reachDelta1d\":7372,\"reachDelta7d\":50371,\"reachDelta30d\":65721},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"GB\",\"US\"],\"mainCountry\":\"GB\",\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":16,\"currentRank\":16,\"rankDelta\":1,\"improvementPct\":1.45},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}}],\"pagination\":{\"page\":1,\"limit\":2,\"total\":13876,\"totalPages\":5000,\"offset\":0,\"isTotalApproximate\":true},\"meta\":{\"query\":\"metabolae.com\",\"keywords\":[],\"searchIn\":\"domain\",\"categoryIds\":[],\"country\":null,\"countryFilterKind\":\"audienceCountry\",\"adCountries\":null,\"mainCountries\":[],\"trendSignal\":\"reach_growth_7d\",\"sortBy\":\"reachDelta7d\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"requestedLimit\":2,\"effectiveLimit\":2,\"activeOnly\":true,\"status\":\"active\",\"page\":1,\"minTrafficGrowthPercent\":null,\"trafficGrowthPeriod\":\"last30d\",\"trafficGrowth\":null,\"adsGrowth\":null,\"pageReachGrowth\":null,\"adReachGrowth\":null,\"language\":null,\"adLanguages\":[],\"cta\":[],\"currency\":null,\"currencies\":[],\"shopifyAppIds\":[],\"excludeShopifyAppIds\":[],\"pixelIds\":[],\"excludePixelIds\":[],\"themeIds\":[],\"filters\":{\"search\":[\"metabolae.com\"],\"searchType\":\"domain\",\"keywordMode\":\"any\",\"sortBy\":\"reachDelta7d\",\"order\":\"desc\",\"page\":1,\"limit\":2,\"platforms\":[\"facebook\"],