{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SwewnpWwK5gtfKV6Utd22W","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"requestId\":\"16448628-67b7-4942-a25f-f31e4a4d4892\",\"data\":[{\"id\":\"facebook_1034422866335779\",\"collationId\":\"1658444935678322\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-10-29T18:11:08.714Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-10-29T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-11-01T18:35:50.757Z\",\"daysRunning\":3,\"media\":{\"type\":\"video\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/thumbnails/91f8fc261c9accaa235921feaac2bd87d8e4f1ce97d7f3ebb304ce3e23ec1ce0.jpg\",\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/video/aa46ab7c15b8170101565fd7b81d0640dd3f0c1b9eef0416ddb98d363dfc4cca.mp4\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"74946564564\",\"name\":\"Longevity Methods\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/profile_picture/74946564564.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"74946564564\",\"liveAdsCount\":20,\"reach30d\":71514,\"totalReach\":79836,\"countriesCount\":4,\"facebookLikes\":7002,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"Sed constante…\\nHormigueo o entumecimiento en los pies…\\nFatiga diaria agotadora…\\nVisión borrosa…\\nPicos en los niveles de azúcar en sangre…\\nE incluso el miedo a sufrir complicaciones peligrosas…\\n\\nTodos ellos son síntomas de un problema de DIABETES mucho más grave.\\n\\nPero no te preocupes, no tienes por qué someterte a dietas estrictas ni a inyecciones interminables…\\n\\nEste método, aprobado por médicos, para equilibrar de forma natural el azúcar en sangre ya ha ayudado a miles de personas desde la comodidad de su propio hogar.\\n\\nEcha un vistazo al vídeo que aparece a continuación para obtener más información.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"SEE DETAILS\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://pereamurkyclod.site/\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"pereamurkyclod.site\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Haz ESTO dos veces al día para reducir el azúcar en sangre de forma natural [Haz clic aquí]\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":null},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":null,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":null,\"duplicates\":153,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"CL\",\"CO\",\"GT\",\"PE\"],\"mainCountry\":null,\"gender\":null,\"ageMin\":null,\"ageMax\":null},\"rank\":{\"positionInPage\":1,\"currentRank\":1,\"rankDelta\":null,\"improvementPct\":100},\"flags\":{\"isEuAd\":null,\"isLowReach\":false,\"isMediaChanged\":true,\"hasPartnerBadge\":false}},{\"id\":\"facebook_1053820354082735\",\"collationId\":\"1753967912807545\",\"platform\":\"facebook\",\"status\":\"inactive\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-09-30T12:20:51.230Z\",\"firstSeenAt\":\"2026-09-29T07:00:00.000Z\",\"lastSeenAt\":\"2026-10-05T16:36:28.326Z\",\"daysRunning\":6,\"media\":{\"type\":\"image\",\"thumbnailUrl\":null,\"mediaUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/facebook/image/c836813489c0fb01f2864cc54445e02bb199d2c97ff88e58b8211453a102e260.png\"},\"advertiser\":{\"id\":\"124148927438400\",\"name\":\"Ahuja Pulliam Montgomery\",\"logoUrl\":\"https://medias.trendtrack.io/profile_picture/124148927438400.jpg\",\"facebookPageId\":\"124148927438400\",\"liveAdsCount\":3437,\"reach30d\":232122,\"totalReach\":622979,\"countriesCount\":91,\"facebookLikes\":481,\"instagramFollowers\":null},\"content\":{\"title\":null,\"body\":\"I've been on Metformin for 9 years. I've been a braider for 37. A wife for 33. A grandmother for 7. The drug gave me perfect numbers. Then it took my hands.\\n\\nI need to tell you what happened, because if your blood sugar is high and you're taking Metformin or thinking about it or trying to manage it on your own with cinnamon and apple cider vinegar and every other thing the internet says works, you need to read this. All of it.\\n\\nMy name is Joanne. I'm 61 years old. I opened my shop when I was 24, saved every dollar from 5 years of braiding hair on my grandmother's porch and put a deposit on a storefront that smelled like floor cleaner and old paint. Built it from nothing. Survived three recessions. Outlasted a strip mall demolition. Moved twice. Rebuilt twice. Never once closed. Not for COVID. Not for anything.\\n\\nI braided the way I was taught. Knotless box braids that lay flat from the scalp. Fulani patterns that looked like they were stitched in by hand. Tension so clean the parts could cut you. My grandmother was a braider. She braided in our living room from a wooden stool, six clients a day, every day, for 40 years. She gave me her wooden parting comb when I opened my shop and said, \\\"The day your fingers stop feeling the hair is the day you stop.\\\" I hung that comb on the wall behind my chair and I've used it every working day for 37 years.\\n\\nOr I did. Until two years ago.\\n\\nMy doctor put me on Metformin when I was 52. A1C was 7.4. He said 1000 milligrams, twice a day, come back in 90 days. I didn't question it. Why would I? I was healthy. I was active. I was on my feet 10 hours a day braiding hair. But my numbers were high and he said the drug was the smart move.\\n\\n90 days later my A1C was 6.6. Fasting glucose dropped from 156 to 118. He was thrilled. I was thrilled. The drug was doing exactly what it was supposed to do.\\n\\nSome tingling in my fingers in the mornings. Nothing I thought twice about. I was 52. I'd been working with my hands since I was a teenager. Tingling happens.\\n\\nBy year three the tingling had a name. Numbness.\\n\\nMy hands. Fingertips first, then up through my fingers. A dull, dead feeling that settled in around noon every day and didn't let up until I went to bed. Mornings were the worst. I couldn't feel the comb in my hand when I woke up. Had to run my hands under hot water for 10 minutes before I could grip a section of hair properly. 10 minutes. Every morning. Standing over the sink at 5 AM rubbing fingertips that didn't want to wake up, trying to get them ready for a day of work they used to do without thinking.\\n\\nI told my doctor.\\n\\nHe ran my labs. Looked at the A1C.\\n\\n\\\"Your A1C is 6.4. Fasting glucose is 112. These are excellent numbers, Joanne. Whatever you're doing, keep doing it.\\\"\\n\\nI said, \\\"I'm not asking about my A1C. I'm asking about my hands.\\\"\\n\\nHe said neuropathy is a known issue in diabetic patients. Affects some people. For most, the benefits of the medication significantly outweigh the risks. He'd keep an eye on it.\\n\\nKeep an eye on it.\\n\\nEasy to say when your hands aren't your whole life.\\n\\nBy year five the knotless box braids were gone.\\n\\nNot all at once. I didn't wake up one morning and decide to stop. It happened in pieces. First I stopped booking knotless on Mondays because my hands were stiffest at the start of the week. Then Mondays became Monday through Wednesday. Then I started telling new clients I was only doing regular box braids and twists. Then it was twists only, all the time, for everyone.\\n\\nWomen used to drive across town specifically for my knotless. I could feed in extensions so smoothly the braid looked like it grew that way. That wasn't a skill I learned in a year. That was 30 years of muscle memory built one braid at a time. And I could feel it leaving.\\n\\nThe shaking started around year five. My hands. Not dramatic. Just a fine tremor when I tried to hold tension on a small section. Barely visible to anyone who wasn't looking. But I was looking. Every day I was looking. Every time I picked up a section of hair I was watching my own fingers for the shake, hoping today would be the day it stopped.\\n\\nIt didn't stop. It got worse.\\n\\nClients started noticing. Nobody said anything. You don't tell your braider her hands are shaking the same way you don't tell a singer her voice is going. But I saw them glance in the mirror. I saw the regulars who used to close their eyes in my chair and lean back into the work now watching my hands with both eyes open.\\n\\nI started going slower. A woman who's braided over a thousand heads doesn't suddenly need 4 extra hours unless something is wrong. I was going slower because slower meant steadier. Slower meant I could hide the shake. Slower meant nobody had to see that the hands they'd trusted for years were failing.\\n\\nThe wooden parting comb, my grandmother's comb, started gathering dust.\\n\\nI went back to my doctor. Told him the numbness was worse. Showed him my hands. Told him I couldn't do knotless anymore, the thing that made my shop my shop, the thing women drove across town for, the thing my grandmother taught me, gone.\\n\\nHe looked at my chart.\\n\\n\\\"Joanne, your A1C is 6.2. Fasting glucose is 108. These are excellent numbers. I understand the neuropathy is frustrating, but the cardiovascular and kidney protection Metformin provides at your age is significant. The benefits outweigh the risks. I'd strongly recommend you stay on it.\\\"\\n\\nThe benefits outweigh the risks.\\n\\nHe said it the way you'd compliment a child's report card. Like the number on the chart was the only thing that mattered. Like the woman attached to the chart, the woman whose hands were dying, whose livelihood was disappearing, whose grandmother's comb was collecting dust, was irrelevant.\\n\\nI decided if the doctor wasn't going to help me, I'd help myself. Maybe I could get my numbers down naturally that I could lower the Metformin dose. Maybe come off it entirely with my doctor's approval. Get my hands back.\\n\\nSo I tried everything. And I mean everything.\\n\\nI started with cinnamon. Real Ceylon cinnamon. Not the cheap stuff. Every morning at 5 AM, stirring it into hot water because somebody told me it slows down how fast carbs hit your blood. I did that for a year. A full year. 365 mornings of cinnamon water. My A1C dropped 2 points. 2 points. My hands were still numb.\\n\\nApple cider vinegar. 2 tablespoons before every meal. The kind with the mother in it. 7 months. My morning numbers came down a little. Post meal? Nothing. And the taste was so bad I'd dread sitting down to dinner.\\n\\nBerberine. The one everybody on TikTok was calling nature's Ozempic. Three capsules a day, three different brands over six months. The cramping was worse than the Metformin. My numbers barely moved.\\n\\nMy doctor glanced at the results and said, \\\"Your A1C looks great, Joanne. Keep taking the Metformin.\\\"\\n\\nI could have put my fist through the wall. Except my fist didn't fully close anymore.\\n\\nAfter that I tried everything else I could get my hands on. Chromium. Bitter melon. Fenugreek. Banaba leaf. Gymnema sylvestre. Alpha lipoic acid because I'd read it helps with neuropathy. CoQ10. Magnesium. Psyllium husk. Some for months. Some for weeks. None of them moved the needle. My kitchen counter looked like a pharmacy. My husband counted the bottles one night and said, \\\"You have more pills than the Metformin was supposed to replace.\\\"\\n\\nHe was right.\\n\\nAnd the worst part? Through all of it, my A1C barely moved without the drug. And my hands kept getting worse on it.\\n\\nI was doing everything you're supposed to do. Everything the blogs say. Everything the health food store recommends. Everything my cousin swears by and everything the lady at church credits for her numbers dropping. None of it worked. And I couldn't figure out why.\\n\\nThe day I almost closed my shop was a Saturday in November.\\n\\nI was sectioning hair for a regular, a woman I'd been braiding for 16 years. Simple style. Big box braids, nothing fancy. Should have been a 4 hour appointment. I was holding her section in my left hand and my fingers seized. Not shook. SEIZED. The muscles in my hand cramped and my fingers locked and the section slipped through and pulled her hair the wrong direction.\\n\\nShe winced.\\n\\nI pulled back. Apologized. She told me it was fine. But I saw her glance at her phone after that, checking the time more than she should have. She didn't say anything. I didn't say anything. I finished the braids. She paid and left.\\n\\nShe didn't rebook.\\n\\nI sat in my own chair after she left. The shop was empty. 6 PM on a Saturday, a time when 3 women should have been waiting. I sat there looking at my hands, palms up, and I realized these aren't my hands anymore. These are what's left of my hands after 9 years on a drug that was supposed to keep me healthy.\\n\\nI looked at the photographs on the wall. The first dollar bill in a frame by the register. The wooden parting comb.\\n\\nMy grandmother said the day your fingers stop feeling the hair is the day you stop.\\n\\nI called my husband and told him I was thinking about closing. He was quiet for a long time. Then he said, \\\"Don't close yet. Don't make that decision yet. Just don't decide today.\\\"\\n\\nI didn't close. But I went home that night knowing something had to change. The Metformin was destroying me. The supplements weren't helping me. And my doctor was watching a number on a chart while my career died.\\n\\nI didn't start with the right question. I started where everyone starts. \\\"Can Metformin cause hand neuropathy.\\\"\\n\\n200,000 results.\\n\\n\\\"Metformin B12 depletion.\\\" Millions.\\n\\n\\\"Metformin nerve damage hands.\\\" Forums full of women describing exactly what I was living. Quilters who couldn't thread a needle. Pianists who couldn't feel the keys. Surgeons, SURGEONS, who had to stop operating. I wasn't alone. I wasn't crazy. And I wasn't \\\"just getting old.\\\"\\n\\nBut the search that changed everything was one I almost didn't click. \\\"Why do people on Metformin still see their A1C climb.\\\"\\n\\nThat's where I fell down the rabbit hole. And I didn't come back up the same person.\\n\\nHere's what 9 years of doctor visits never explained to me, and what every supplement I'd tried completely ignored.\\n\\nMetformin doesn't address what's actually broken inside your body.\\n\\nStay with me here. This is the part that changed everything for me.\\n\\nYour body has a pathway that controls how fast the food you eat turns into sugar in your blood. When food comes in, the pathway breaks it down at a controlled pace. Sugar trickles into your blood slowly. Your insulin sees it coming and handles it calmly. Your numbers stay steady.\\n\\nThat's how it worked when you were 30 and you could eat anything.\\n\\nBut that pathway is fragile. Stress wears it down. Processed food wears it down. Years of carrying extra weight wear it down. And one day it stops being able to do its job.\\n\\nFood doesn't trickle into your blood anymore. It floods in. The carbs in your meal that used to take 90 minutes to enter your blood now hit in 15. The flood arrives faster than your insulin can handle. So you spike. Your pancreas panics and dumps a wave of insulin to catch up. That wave is too much. So an hour later you crash.\\n\\nThat flood is the problem. Not the food. The broken pathway.\\n\\nNow here's what Metformin actually does. It works on your liver. It tells your liver to stop releasing stored sugar into your blood overnight. That's helpful for your fasting glucose number. The morning reading on the meter comes down. Your A1C looks better on paper.\\n\\nBut it doesn't touch the pathway that's letting food flood your blood every time you eat. It doesn't slow the breakdown. It doesn't restore the system that controls the speed. The flood still happens every meal. The spike still happens. The crash still happens.\\n\\nYour doctor was watching the morning number. Nobody was watching the flood.\\n\\nAnd here's where I saw my own hands in the research.\\n\\nMetformin depletes vitamin B12. Long term use, year after year, slowly drains your B12 levels. And your nerves need B12 to function. Your fingertips need it to feel. Your hands need it to grip, to hold tension, to braid hair the way they've been doing it for 37 years.\\n\\nI read that and I felt it in my fingers. I saw 9 years of mornings running hot water over fingertips that wouldn't wake up. I saw 37 years of muscle memory slowly losing the nutrient it needed to fire. The numbness wasn't aging. It wasn't \\\"just diabetes.\\\" It was my nerves running out of B12 because the drug was depleting it as part of how it works.\\n\\nThat's not a side effect. That's the drug doing what it does. And nobody told me.\\n\\nAbout 30% of long term Metformin users develop B12 deficiency. That's not a rare complication. That's nearly one in three. And doctors keep charting it as \\\"manageable\\\" while their patients can't thread needles and can't feel piano keys and can't part hair with their grandmother's comb.\\n\\nAnd here's what finally explained why everything else I tried also failed.\\n\\nAll those supplements I was taking? Cinnamon, apple cider vinegar, berberine, chromium, bitter melon, every single one of them was working downstream. They were trying to nudge insulin around the edges. Help my cells listen a little better to the wave of insulin my body dumps after a spike. None of them touched the pathway that's letting food flood my blood in the first place.\\n\\nI spent 2 years mopping the floor while the roof was leaking.\\n\\nThat's why I could drink cinnamon water at 5 AM every morning for a year and watch my A1C drop 2 points. That's why I could swallow apple cider vinegar before every meal until I dreaded eating and still see my post meal numbers spike. That's why I could fill my kitchen counter with bottles and spend $200 a month and still sit in my own braiding chair on a Saturday afternoon wondering if I had to close my shop.\\n\\nEverything I'd tried, the drug AND the natural alternatives, was aimed at the symptom. Nothing had addressed the broken pathway driving the symptom.\\n\\nSo I went back to the research. And I searched for the thing I should have been searching for from the beginning. \\\"What restores the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar.\\\"\\n\\nThat's when I found something I'd never heard of. And by the end of that night, I understood why nothing else had worked.\\n\\nThere's a fruit that's been used in Central Asia for over 5,000 years.\\n\\nThe healers who used it back then didn't have a name for blood sugar. They didn't know what insulin was. They didn't have a way to measure what was happening inside the body. But they knew what they saw. People who ate this fruit daily had steadier energy. Calmer digestion. They didn't crash after meals. They didn't fall into the slow decline everyone else seemed to. They lived longer. They aged better.\\n\\nThey called it the fruit of life.\\n\\nToday we call it pomegranate.\\n\\nI'd heard of pomegranate. You probably have too. That bothered me at first. If this worked, wouldn't I have seen it at the health food store next to the berberine that didn't work and the bitter melon that didn't move my numbers?\\n\\nBut you can't patent a fruit. And there's no billion dollar industry built around something that grows on a tree.\\n\\nThen I looked at the research. And I stopped caring that I'd heard of it. Because modern science now knows exactly why it works.\\n\\nPomegranate contains specific compounds called punicalagins. One of the most powerful compounds your body uses to slow the breakdown of food into sugar back down to its natural pace.\\n\\nHere's what punicalagins do.\\n\\nThey restore the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar in your blood. Not in your liver like Metformin. Not in your gut like cinnamon. Not insulin sensitization like berberine. The actual pathway that controls the speed of the breakdown in the first place.\\n\\nWhen the punicalagins are back in your system, the pathway has what it needs to do its job. Food enters your blood gently again. The way it's supposed to. The flood stops. Your insulin sees the trickle coming. It handles it calmly. The spikes ease. The crashes ease. The cravings ease.\\n\\nThe floodgate holds.\\n\\nThen I found the clinical analysis that made me sit back in my chair.\\n\\nA meta analysis of 34 clinical trials on standardized pomegranate extract. Across more than 1,500 people. Measurable improvements in fasting glucose. Insulin levels. Insulin resistance. Without depleting B12. Without numbness. Without the merry go round of side effects.\\n\\nA fruit. Backed by 34 clinical trials. And nobody in my doctor's office had ever mentioned it because you can't patent something that grows on a tree.\\n\\nI'll be honest. I almost didn't try it.\\n\\nI'd spent 2 years trying things that didn't work. I'd been burned by berberine that wrecked my stomach. By apple cider vinegar that ruined my appetite. By cinnamon that did almost nothing. Bottles on my counter and nothing to show for it except lighter pockets and the same numb hands.\\n\\nBut this was different. I could see it was different. Not because the marketing was better but because the mechanism was different. Everything else I'd tried was working downstream, on the symptoms, on the surface. This was working upstream, restoring the pathway that controlled how my body handled food in the first place. And it wasn't depleting B12. It wasn't blocking anything my body needed. It was giving my body back the raw material it had been missing.\\n\\nI found a company called Alevia that makes a standardized pomegranate extract. Not cheap fruit powder, not heat processed scraps with no consistency. A clinical strength extract, verified to contain the concentration of punicalagins that actually produces results. Organic. Third party tested for purity and potency. Made in the USA.\\n\\nI talked to my doctor about working with him to monitor my numbers monthly while I added something to my routine. He wasn't thrilled. But I told him my hands couldn't wait for him to be thrilled. We agreed to monitor my bloodwork closely.\\n\\nI ordered Alevia's pomegranate that night.\\n\\nThe first thing that changed was the mornings.\\n\\nDay 4. I woke up, rolled out of bed, and felt the sheet under my fingertips. Not strongly. But I felt it. No hot water. No 10 minutes standing over the sink rubbing fingertips that wouldn't wake up. Just felt it. The way you do without thinking. The way I used to before the drug took it from me.\\n\\nI stood in my bathroom at 5 AM staring at my own hands. I'd forgotten what waking up with feeling in my fingers felt like.\\n\\nBy week 2, my fasting glucose was running consistently lower. Not dramatically. But the kind of consistent drop that builds week after week. The kind of number that doesn't lie. And the deep numbness that hit every afternoon and had me dipping my hands in warm water between clients was fading. Not gone. Fading. Like someone slowly turning down a sound I'd gotten so used to I'd forgotten it was there.\\n\\nWeek 3, a client closed her eyes in my chair. First time in months someone had done that. She wasn't watching my hands. She just leaned back and closed her eyes the way you do when you trust the hands holding your hair. I almost had to stop braiding because the lump in my throat hit me out of nowhere.\\n\\nWeek 4, the parts were sharp again. The tension was even. The rhythm was back. I wasn't going slower to hide the shake because there was less shake to hide. The flow came back. That thing, the instinct, the thing you can't fake and 37 years of muscle memory either gives you or it doesn't, it was coming back.\\n\\nWeek 6, a new client sat down and asked if I did knotless box braids. I said not right now. She said, \\\"That's too bad. I heard you were the best.\\\" I looked at my hands. They were steady. Not still. I'm 61, there's a difference between youth and recovery. But steady.\\n\\nI thought about it for the rest of the day.\\n\\nLast Saturday.\\n\\nOne of my regulars, a woman I've been braiding for 19 years, sat down in my chair. Normal appointment. Normal day. She'd asked for twists.\\n\\nAnd then I stopped.\\n\\nI walked over to the wall. Took down the wooden parting comb. My grandmother's comb. The one that had been gathering dust for over a year. I ran my thumb across the wood. It was dry. Needed oil. I didn't care.\\n\\nI picked up the comb. Made the first part. Slow. Deliberate. The sound of the comb dividing the section so clean you could hear it. The other braiders looked up. The women waiting looked up. Nobody said a word.\\n\\nI turned to my client.\\n\\n\\\"Sit still for me, baby.\\\"\\n\\nI started sectioning for knotless. Pulling the smallest amount of her own hair at the base. Feeling the tension. Feeding in the extension. The way my grandmother taught me. The way I'd done it 10,000 times before the drug took it away.\\n\\nMy hands weren't shaking.\\n\\nWhen I finished the first braid I held it up where she could see it in the mirror. She looked at it. Looked at me. She'd been watching my hands decline for 2 years. She knew what this meant.\\n\\n\\\"Welcome back, Joanne.\\\"\\n\\nI didn't trust myself to speak. I just hung the comb back on the wall where it belongs and kept braiding.\\n\\nWhen she went to pay, I shook my head.\\n\\n\\\"This one's on me, sister.\\\"\\n\\nShe understood.\\n\\nI ran my bloodwork at 3 months.\\n\\nMy A1C: 5.9. On Metformin alone, it had been 6.2. With the pomegranate added in, my A1C was lower.\\n\\nFasting glucose: 102. On Metformin it had been 108. Lower.\\n\\nPost meal numbers: holding under 140 even after real meals. Bread, rice, the foods I used to be terrified of. The supplements I'd taken for 7 months never got my post meal numbers anywhere close to that.\\n\\nMy doctor studied the labs for a long time.\\n\\n\\\"Your numbers are the best I've seen on you in 9 years,\\\" he said. \\\"What changed?\\\"\\n\\nI told him. Pomegranate. The punicalagins. The 34 trial meta analysis. The 5,000 years of use. He listened. When I finished he said, \\\"I'm going to be honest with you. I don't know enough about this yet. But your numbers don't lie. And you look like a different woman than the one who was sitting in that chair 6 months ago.\\\"\\n\\nHe was right. I was a different woman. Because for 9 years a drug had been slowly erasing me, and in 3 months, I'd come back.\\n\\nI'm writing this for every woman sitting in a chair somewhere, a braiding chair, a sewing machine, a piano bench, a kitchen counter, whose hands aren't what they were.\\n\\n37 years. I wasn't ready to stop.\\n\\nIf you're on Metformin and your fingertips are tingling, your hands are numb in the mornings, your grip is weaker than it should be, your forearms ache in a way rest doesn't fix.\\n\\nIf you've been trying to manage your blood sugar naturally, the cinnamon, the apple cider vinegar, the berberine, the supplements that cost you $200 a month, and your numbers won't budge.\\n\\nIf your doctor keeps watching a number on a chart while your body falls apart.\\n\\nIf you've been told \\\"the benefits outweigh the risks\\\" by someone who doesn't have to live with the risks.\\n\\nMaybe it's not just aging. Maybe it's the drug. And maybe everything you've tried is working downstream while the problem is upstream. In the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar in your blood. Where nothing you've taken can reach.\\n\\nI spent 9 years on a drug that lowered a number while the pathway underneath stayed broken. Then I spent 2 years and thousands of dollars on supplements that were never going to reach the real problem.\\n\\nAlevia's pomegranate addressed it. The punicalagins restore the pathway that controls how fast food becomes sugar in your blood, the one thing the Metformin never did and the supplements never could. No B12 depletion. No enzyme blocking. No nerve damage. No numb hands.\\n\\nAlevia uses a process that preserves the active compounds at clinical strength. Organic. Third party tested for purity and potency. Made in the USA. 2 capsules every morning. That's it.\\n\\nThat's the difference between a supplement that sits in your system doing nothing and one that actually addresses the pathway driving your numbers up.\\n\\nRight now, they're running a promotion with significant savings. But Alevia is a small company. They make everything in small batches to preserve the active compounds. When they sell out, it's usually weeks before the next batch is ready.\\n\\n90 days to try it. If nothing changes, full refund. No questions asked.\\n\\nBut if it works?\\n\\nIf you wake up on day 4 and feel the sheet under your fingertips without running hot water over your hands for 10 minutes?\\n\\nIf by week 2 the numbness in your forearms starts fading and you stop dipping your hands in warm water between clients?\\n\\nIf a month from now your clients are closing their eyes in your chair again because they trust the hands holding their hair?\\n\\nIf 3 months from now you're sitting in your doctor's office watching him stare at numbers he can't explain, and he says \\\"whatever you're doing, keep doing it\\\"?\\n\\nIf your A1C comes down lower than it ever was on the drug alone?\\n\\nIf your grandmother's comb comes off the wall and goes back where it belongs?\\n\\nYou'll wish you started 9 years ago.\\n\\nI know I do.\\n\\n👉https://po.muvost.com/\\n\\nYou don't have to keep lowering a number while the pathway underneath stays broken.\",\"transcript\":null,\"callToAction\":\"SHOP NOW\",\"landingPageUrl\":\"https://po.muvost.com/\",\"landingPageDomain\":\"po.muvost.com\",\"ctaDescription\":\"Organic Pomegranate Extract Capsules | AT2A\",\"ctaLinkDescription\":\"The 5,000-year-old fruit of life in a pure, potent organic extract.\"},\"metrics\":{\"reach\":128,\"aggregatedReach\":null,\"estimatedSpend\":1,\"duplicates\":41,\"reachDelta1d\":null,\"reachDelta7d\":null,\"reachDelta30d\":null},\"audience\":{\"targetedCountries\":[\"AE\",\"AG\",\"AI\",\"AS\",\"AT\",\"AU\",\"AW\",\"BB\",\"BE\",\"BG\",\"BH\",\"BL\",\"BM\",\"BN\",\"BS\",\"BZ\",\"CA\",\"CC\",\"CG\",\"CK\",\"CO\",\"CW\",\"CY\",\"DE\",\"DK\",\"DM\",\"DO\",\"EC\",\"EE\",\"ES\",\"FJ\",\"FK\",\"FM\",\"FO\",\"GD\",\"GF\",\"GG\",\"GI\",\"GL\",\